[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054440 :question/id #uuid "1acb903b-bdf0-5d16-8a1c-6fdce21f4c84" :question/math-academy-id "e-13172" :question/problem "If $A = \\{3, 21, 147,1\\,029,\\ldots,5\\,931\\,980\\,229\\},$ then what is $|A|?$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3, \\quad 21, \\quad 147, \\quad 1\\,029,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=7{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =7 \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{147}{21} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 r^{n-1} = 3(7^{n-1}) = \\dfrac37(7)^n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n : n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq N \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}(7)^{N} & =5\\,931\\,980\\,229 \\\\ {7}^{N} & =13\\,841\\,287\\,201 \\\\ N & ={\\log }_{7}⁡(13\\,841\\,287\\,201) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{\\log }_{10}⁡(13\\,841\\,287\\,201)}{{\\log }_{10}⁡(7)} \\\\ N & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n : n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 12\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $12$ elements, we must have $|A| = 12.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054540 :question/id #uuid "fc85eeb4-dbc0-5ea7-af3a-60270d6ae722" :question/math-academy-id "e-4089" :question/problem "Calculate $\\log_{16}(32)$ without using a calculator." :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=16$ and $X =32.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2,$ we set $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{16}⁡(32) & =\\frac{{\\log }_{2}⁡(32)}{{\\log }_{2}⁡(16)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{5})}{{\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054541 :question/id #uuid "15844e53-9573-5e6f-8fef-debbe0a142ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-4091" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\log x ?$ \n\nI. $\\dfrac {\\log_{2} x} {\\log_{2} 10}$\n\nII. $\\dfrac {\\log_{2} 10} {\\log_{2} x}$\n\nIII. $\\log_{2}\\left(\\dfrac{x}{10}\\right)$" :question/worked-solution "The change of base formula for logarithms is\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}.\n$$\n\nThe change of base formula works with any new base. In this example, the choices are written using base $2,$ so we rewrite $\\log x$ in terms of $\\log_2.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡x & ={\\log }_{10}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\log }_{2}⁡x}{{\\log }_{2}⁡10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054543 :question/id #uuid "890afebc-d130-5dbc-aaf7-c2cd4db193f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4100" :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate $\\log_3 (7)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate $\\log_3 (7)$ by changing the base $3$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=3,$ $X =7,$ and $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{3}⁡(7) & =\\frac{{\\log }_{10}⁡(7)}{{\\log }_{10}⁡(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.845\\,10}{0.477\\,12} \\\\  & ≈1.77.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd6edde5-507c-5ff6-8c58-ea2a1b440718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286519}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286519} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c26fd7f-de11-54f6-a913-91718d9fcabd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d64c45b7-2d40-58ad-9748-05e941de30d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-181454" :question/problem "If $A={3,6,12,24,48,…,98\\,304},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3,\\,6,\\,12,\\,24,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=3({2}^{n-1})=\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{N} & =98\\,304 \\\\ {2}^{N} & =65\\,536 \\\\ N & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(65\\,536) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{\\log }_{10}⁡(65\\,536)}{{\\log }_{10}⁡(2)} \\\\ N & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $16$ elements, we must have $|A|=16.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca66def7-3c65-5210-99d9-f0db68c4888c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522440} {:answer/id #uuid "745edc25-a61b-4419-a155-2f8412f950ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186651036}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522440} :answer-field/id #uuid "72fdab3a-5e80-5092-81a0-756921237d3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3ba35fd5-f0a2-573b-abd8-5bda612260aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-262884" :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, ${\\log }_{16}⁡(64)$ equals{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{\\log }_{b}⁡X}{{\\log }_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=16$ and $X=64.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2,$ we set $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{16}⁡(64) & =\\frac{{\\log }_{2}⁡(64)}{{\\log }_{2}⁡(16)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{6})}{{\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96517480-c58a-580a-bd6c-7724f8c25364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054010} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f3604e-93c4-543c-85d4-015c8fe7e1ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054011} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d3f4da-921c-581d-b2f2-9c94396b9176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054012} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd17a35-4d96-574e-94c2-099f33db7738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054013} {:answer/id #uuid "2d69af09-12b4-5f14-a76b-d95e41ad99a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054014}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054014} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c9c30ac-745b-5ac6-8f1f-5c84b0f7083a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d4f6e3a6-36a7-5dcc-b176-d20ea50c924d" :question/math-academy-id "q-66882" :question/problem "${\\log }_{36}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6})=$" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{\\log }_{b}⁡X}{{\\log }_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=36$ and $X=\\sqrt[3]{6}.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ can be presented as a power of $6,$ we set $b=6.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{36}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6}) & =\\frac{{\\log }_{6}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6})}{{\\log }_{6}⁡(36)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\log }_{6}⁡({6}^{1/3})}{{\\log }_{6}⁡({6}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83ff33ee-caa6-51d8-958d-bfcfda022a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054334} {:answer/id #uuid "ac037dd2-aa67-5dda-91e5-2a864d1cc729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054335} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f7a19d-57a8-550f-b117-e9656ddde586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054336} {:answer/id #uuid "d8bc585d-ed81-566c-b6a6-a612bcdd6c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054337} {:answer/id #uuid "05d90b4d-f42c-59f3-afaf-50adcb8c8df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054338}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054334} :answer-field/id #uuid "15a9598e-3841-5a8c-b408-bd666ace4326" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2013c32c-5fa6-5fa3-be49-a548908a8a84" :question/math-academy-id "q-957" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\log }_{9}⁡(27)$ without using a calculator." :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{\\log }_{b}⁡X}{{\\log }_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=9,$ and $X=27.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $3,$ we set $b=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{9}⁡(27) & =\\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡(27)}{{\\log }_{3}⁡(9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{3})}{{\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055970 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to evaluate $\\log_{4}9,$ but our calculator only has a key for the common logarithm $\\log$ (base $10$).\n\nIn such cases, we can use the **change of base formula**, which states that\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}.\n$$\n\nIt effectively allows us to change a logarithm from one base, $a,$ to another, $b.$ Note that $X,$ $a,$ and $b$ have to be all greater than zero and that $a \\ne 1,$ $b \\ne 1.$ \n\nWe usually choose the value of base $b$ that makes the problem easier. In particular, if we need to evaluate the expression using a calculator, we choose $b=10$ or $b=e$. \n\nFor example, to evaluate $\\log_{4}9$ using this formula, we can rewrite the logarithm from base $a=4$ to base $b=10.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{4}⁡9 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡9}{\\operatorname{log}⁡4} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.954\\,242}{0.602\\,059} \\\\  & =1.585\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :tutorial/id #uuid "bb104b87-e850-5cff-9826-fdd61c0287d1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2797 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059432 :question/id #uuid "eaf86423-5f7e-5da2-aa3d-cf35e9b16b13" :question/math-academy-id "e-13308" :question/problem "Let $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Prove that if $2a \\equiv 2b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 14)$, then $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7)$." :question/worked-solution "We start by using the definition of modular congruence. Recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n \\mid (a-b) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad a-b = nk\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\n2a \\equiv 2b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 14) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7)\n$$\n\nSo, we can reason as follows:\n\n> By the definition of modular congruence,\n> $2a≡2b(\\bmod 14) \\mid ⇔14∣(2a - 2b); ⇔14∣2(a - b)$\n\nNext, we use the definition of divisibility:\n\n> By the definition of divisibility in $\\mathbb{Z}$, $14 \\mid 2(a-b)$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that\n> $2(a-b) = 14k$.\n\nNow, we can cancel out the factor of $2{:}$\n\n> Dividing this equation by $2$, we obtain\n> $a-b = 7k$,\n> which in turn implies\n> $7 \\mid (a-b)$.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of modular congruence again:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, we have\n> $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059453 :question/id #uuid "d7516cec-bd96-59db-98cd-5d82e41fa719" :question/math-academy-id "e-14763" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $A$ given below?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n- $A$ is a singular matrix\n\n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-4)-2(7-3)+3(4-0) \\\\  & =-4-2\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-4-8+12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059469 :question/id #uuid "762e323d-62be-5cd0-ad06-7b11af56fe3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3136" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\sin^2\\theta$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta} = \\sin^2{\\theta}\\cdot(\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta})\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}) & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta} = \\sin^2\\theta.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059470 :question/id #uuid "f2b7e482-2bcd-5bd2-91ee-66415b375da4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3137" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta}{\\sin\\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\t=\t\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\cdot (\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta)} {\\sin \\theta}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta} = \\cot \\theta.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059471 :question/id #uuid "9ee1e04e-8a36-5597-8928-c2ebd67c1b1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3139" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the second term using the identity $\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\dfrac{\\cos{\\theta}}{\\sin{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nNext, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} &= \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} &= \\frac{({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} \\\\ &= \\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ &= \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta} = \\csc\\theta.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059479 :question/id #uuid "3b9c93fe-82ac-58c5-b14a-9bcd30b1f0e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3175" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the denominator using $1-\\cos^2 x = \\sin^2 x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}} = \\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{\\sin^2{x}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the expression by separating out the factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}}=\\cot{x}\\csc{x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059480 :question/id #uuid "e5821060-9f69-5df5-9456-a4574e92e910" :question/math-academy-id "e-3176" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-\\sin^2 x = \\cos^2 x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x} = \\dfrac{1-\\sin^2 x}{1-\\sin x}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x} = 1 + \\sin x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059486 :question/id #uuid "d917e025-59a4-5df5-b412-4eb6f2c4afc7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3329" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\sin x(2\\cos x - \\sin x) - \\cos^2x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 x$ and $\\cos^2 x$ terms. \n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x -(\\sin^2x + \\cos^2 x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{x} + \\cos^2{x} = 1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin x(2\\cos x - \\sin x) - \\cos^2x = 2\\sin x\\cos x - 1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059487 :question/id #uuid "056204bc-53b9-5a32-a53c-0f728729fd9f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3332" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right) = 3\\cos^2 x \\sec^2 x - 3\\cos^2 x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the identity $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}$ to further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x{\\sec }^{2}⁡x-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x & =3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cancel{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}}-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3(1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity $\\sin^2 x = 1-\\cos^2 x$ to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, as follows:\n\n$$\n3(1-\\cos^2 x) = 3\\cdot \\sin^2 x = 3\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right)= 3\\sin^2 x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059503 :question/id #uuid "31b9e2d8-1458-50b3-a920-703476825420" :question/math-academy-id "e-3631" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y =  3\\cos x  + 4\\sin x + 2x^3.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\sin x ) = \\cos x,$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\cos x ) = -\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\cos ⁡x+4\\sin ⁡x+2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\cos ⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin ⁡x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-3\\sin ⁡x+4\\cos ⁡x+2(3){x}^{2} \\\\  & =-3\\sin ⁡x+4\\cos ⁡x+6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059504 :question/id #uuid "ae922c00-2ff5-5a46-805a-62955bc5a08a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3632" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y = 4\\tan x + 5x$ at $x = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope at $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative $y'$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (\\tan x) = \\sec^2 x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\tan ⁡x+5x) \\\\  & =4{\\sec }^{2}⁡x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =4{\\sec }^{2}⁡0+5 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059509 :question/id #uuid "0a0ef460-7a16-5dc9-b522-567f0462c678" :question/math-academy-id "e-3771" :question/problem "$1 - \\cos \\left(\\dfrac \\pi 2 - y \\right) \\sin y=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the cofunction identity for sine and cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - y \\right) = \\sin y\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - \\cos(\\frac{π}{2} - y)\\sin y &= 1 - \\sin y\\sin y \\\\\n&= 1 - \\sin^{2} y \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059518 :question/id #uuid "3590f0e0-2ff9-58f4-8351-772b477c1cd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4109" :question/problem "If $\\log_7 (2x + 1) = 1,$ then what is $x?$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{7}⁡(2x+1) & =1 \\\\ {7}^{{\\log }_{7}⁡(2x+1)} & ={7}^{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $7^{\\log_7 (2x + 1)} = 2x+1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =7 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059557 :question/id #uuid "2b6e8845-a53d-510c-b44f-1b00271ee507" :question/math-academy-id "e-4434" :question/problem "Calculate $\\det(A),$ if <div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix} = ad - bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6-2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =18-10 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059558 :question/id #uuid "b066b73a-c777-550c-9c6e-af8093bf7fab" :question/math-academy-id "e-4435" :question/problem "Calculate $\\det(A),$ if <div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix} = ad - bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}-(-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059559 :question/id #uuid "043a5e58-e1a0-50a5-b1be-d404e6262991" :question/math-academy-id "e-4436" :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle {A} = \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        2 & -1  \\\\\n        -3 & k\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n$$\nand\n$\\det({A}) = 5$, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix} = ad - bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =2k-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=5,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =5 \\\\ 2k-3 & =5 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059560 :question/id #uuid "79841c0d-e191-566b-8fe2-63828dcfc23a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4439" :question/problem "Calculate the minor of the entry $b_{21}$ in the matrix\n$$\nB= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}10 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 2\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The minor of $b_{21} = 3$ (second row, first column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $3$ are removed: \n\n$$\nB= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{gray}∗} & 1 & 5 \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{gray}3}} & {\\color{gray}∗} & {\\color{gray}∗} \\\\ {\\color{gray}∗} & 7 & 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of $b_{21}=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =2-35 \\\\  & =-33.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059561 :question/id #uuid "51f60726-3d12-507e-b942-5d316fc28bd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4440" :question/problem "What is the minor of $c_{22}$ in the matrix\n$$\nC=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}7 x & 0 & - 3 y \\\\ 1 & x + 2 y & 4 \\\\ - y & 0 & 5\\end{array} \\right]}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and second column: \n\n$$\nC=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}7 x & {\\color{gray}∗} & - 3 y \\\\ {\\color{gray}∗} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{gray}x + 2 y}} & {\\color{gray}∗} \\\\ - y & {\\color{gray}∗} & 5\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det \\begin{bmatrix}7x & -3y \\\\ -y & 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}7x & -3y \\\\ -y & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7x\\cdot 5-(-3y)\\cdot (-y) \\\\  & =35x-3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059562 :question/id #uuid "afd06762-0a78-5559-b035-98bd05e6c084" :question/math-academy-id "e-4441" :question/problem "Given that the minor of $a_{23}$ is $5,$ find all the possible values of $k$ in the matrix\n$$\nA= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}k & 3 & - 1 \\\\ - 5 & 2 & 7 \\\\ - 1 & 2 k & - 6\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and third column:\n\n$$\nA= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}k & 3 & {\\color{gray}∗} \\\\ {\\color{gray}∗} & {\\color{gray}∗} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{gray}7}} \\\\ - 1 & 2 k & {\\color{gray}∗}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe minor of $a_{23}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det \\begin{bmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\cdot 2k-3\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of $a_{23}$ is $5,$ so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{k}^{2}+3 & =5 \\\\ 2{k}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =1 \\\\ k & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=-1$ and $k=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059563 :question/id #uuid "8cd94856-fbe5-53a4-b7f2-f5b29b7aa927" :question/math-academy-id "e-4514" :question/problem "Find the inverse of $A={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & - 3 & 8\\end{array} \\right]}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-(8-12)+2(-3-0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 14 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}9 & - ( - 4 ) & - 3 \\\\ - ( 14 ) & - 8 & - ( - 4 ) \\\\ 3 & - ( - 2 ) & - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}9 & 4 & - 3 \\\\ - 14 & - 8 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\nC^{T}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}9 & - 14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & - 8 & 2 \\\\ - 3 & 4 & - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{9}{2} & 7 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059564 :question/id #uuid "9180ff3b-46d2-5df7-81b6-ac75bef51e00" :question/math-academy-id "e-4515" :question/problem "The entry $a'_{31}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}3 & 2 & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & k & 0 \\\\ - 1 & - 3 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ \nequals $1$. Find the value of $k$." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\begin{vmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(3k-0)-2(-6-0)-2(6+k) \\\\  & =9k+12-12-2k \\\\  & =7k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{31}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7k}\\cdot (6+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{6+k}{7k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that $a'_{31}=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6+k}{7k} & =1 \\\\ 6+k & =7k \\\\ 6 & =6k \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059565 :question/id #uuid "26e9a55f-7711-5d8f-ab0a-e8638f2d2dec" :question/math-academy-id "e-4516" :question/problem "Determine the entry $a'_{23}$ in the inverse of the matrix $A={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}- 2 & 3 & - 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & - 1 & 2\\end{array} \\right]}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(2-0)-3(0-0)-3(0-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{23}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}{M}_{32} \\\\  & =(-1)^{5}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\na'_{23} = \\dfrac{1}{-1}c_{32} = -1\\cdot 0 = 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059592 :question/id #uuid "7055ec96-b829-5cca-8149-314b4c8fc6c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5111" :question/problem "Given that $2\\log_5 (x-3)+1 = 5,$ then what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make $\\log_5 (x-3)$ the subject of the equation by moving everything else to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\log }_{5}⁡(x-3)+1 & =5 \\\\ 2{\\log }_{5}⁡(x-3) & =4 \\\\ {\\log }_{5}⁡(x-3) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use a base of $5$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{5}⁡(x-3) & =2 \\\\ {5}^{{\\log }_{5}⁡(x-3)} & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $5^{\\log_5 (x - 3)} = x-3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & ={5}^{2} \\\\ x-3 & =25 \\\\ x & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059593 :question/id #uuid "77f6d553-3291-5110-a30e-11991ae88425" :question/math-academy-id "e-5118" :question/problem "If $\\log_2 x = 5,$ then what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $2,$ so we use a base of $2$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡x & =5 \\\\ {2}^{{\\log }_{2}⁡x} & ={2}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $2^{\\log_2 x} = x.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{5} \\\\ x & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059594 :question/id #uuid "bd3c7932-a6eb-5de6-a485-84e5d44656a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5119" :question/problem "If $7\\log_6{x} - 3\\log_6{x} = 2,$ then what is the value of  $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{\\log }_{6}⁡x-3{\\log }_{6}⁡x & =2 \\\\ (7-3){\\log }_{6}⁡x & =2 \\\\ 4{\\log }_{6}⁡x & =2 \\\\ {\\log }_{6}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $6,$ so we use a base of $6$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{6}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {6}^{{\\log }_{6}⁡x} & ={6}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $6^{\\log_6 x} = x.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={6}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39c21266-f6c2-5bea-9e7b-5c12a6cd64b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241696} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6a2a2e-5861-5f92-b070-b29670058f9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{x}-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241737} {:answer/id #uuid "404488cf-c12a-5d3e-8f12-bae9be5d6d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241756} {:answer/id #uuid "95789385-1849-5307-a089-1a094f6dcc15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}-2{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242493} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e15fa7-75e2-5444-be13-0b59e1863f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243110}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241756} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b79083a-ceaf-5ef9-8a92-8b480f0daf73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "31d5493d-598a-5cd3-9717-dec41584eaba" :question/math-academy-id "q-1089" :question/problem "Calculate $\\det (Z)$, if\n $$\n\\displaystyle Z=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{x} & y \\\\ {y}^{2} & \\sqrt{x}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(Z) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{x} & y \\\\ {y}^{2} & \\sqrt{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\sqrt{x}-y\\cdot {y}^{2} \\\\  & =x-{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3483de1a-1f65-5553-adea-5c1f86b3e57c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241641} {:answer/id #uuid "5eee975d-4bfb-507e-be00-76be487d029e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242033} {:answer/id #uuid "6b31fbe0-f8ff-5f08-9a26-caf4d911af3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242143} {:answer/id #uuid "a528f703-fb11-59ec-ad99-43d1904c252b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242634} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a4905a-bcb6-5d72-aad1-aa967ac93c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243339}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242033} :answer-field/id #uuid "23210561-cf23-5be2-bbb6-5445ea1b8741" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "367551dc-32de-5040-981b-1232c513fd73" :question/math-academy-id "q-1094" :question/problem "Calculate the minor of the entry ${y}_{13}$ in the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle Y=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The minor of ${y}_{13}=9$ (first row, third column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $9$ are removed: \n\n$$\nY=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ & 9 \\\\ 7 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & -1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${y}_{13}=9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-7-0 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059988 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e8e25ba-a316-557a-8f00-6b15de2a1e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{b}^{2}-2ac" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244581} {:answer/id #uuid "2d084569-d649-55d7-998a-14ff921b065c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3ab" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244717} {:answer/id #uuid "a5dc7d96-5629-5635-8667-1ca638707420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6ac-{b}^{c}2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245800} {:answer/id #uuid "da05ce6c-a474-57df-82c6-ca0b45e15bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2ac-{b}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246252} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf23d43-52ca-5021-a484-d53ff9ba58ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{b}^{2}+2ac" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246525}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244581} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba0d203-ed53-5985-a36e-5e409e4beb68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "16f02ef4-4851-5023-bd54-49a8bd7d2abc" :question/math-academy-id "q-1098" :question/problem "What is the minor of ${x}_{12}$ in the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}6a & -3a & c \\\\ -b & {b}^{2} & -2c \\\\ -a & 0 & b\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the first row and second column:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & -3a & ∗ \\\\ -b & ∗ & -2c \\\\ -a & ∗ & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det \\begin{bmatrix}-b & -2c \\\\ -a & b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-b & -2c \\\\ -a & b\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-b\\cdot b-(-2c)\\cdot (-a) \\\\  & =-{b}^{2}-2ac\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59284d45-5b23-5723-9f17-eb10e86cf6cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{r}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245101} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc18bca-b41c-5c15-9a91-3e23676b457a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-r-5rt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245309} {:answer/id #uuid "94e2bfd6-6a3f-5ae8-a19d-106b5b2093d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245618} {:answer/id #uuid "b45568ab-127a-5ea6-ae81-efa3fa24042c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245923} {:answer/id #uuid "e665644e-4547-50b7-8aed-f092b227e6fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3rs+2rt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246352}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246352} :answer-field/id #uuid "b05daa0f-e8b8-55a6-9a0a-9b9550d30ad2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "47a0f2fd-6622-508d-a61d-a108ebab293f" :question/math-academy-id "q-1099" :question/problem "What is the minor of ${d}_{32}$ in the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}3s & -{r}^{2} & 2t \\\\ -r & 0 & r \\\\ 5t & 4r & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the third row and second column:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3s & ∗ & 2t \\\\ -r & ∗ & r \\\\ ∗ & 4r & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det \\begin{bmatrix}3s & 2t \\\\ -r & r\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}3s & 2t \\\\ -r & r\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3s\\cdot r-2t\\cdot (-r) \\\\  & =3rs-(-2rt) \\\\  & =3rs+2rt\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de1991f6-b528-5998-88b7-54673d5bf939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056305} {:answer/id #uuid "0b104d48-c89f-50d8-aa70-08caaff8e170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056306} {:answer/id #uuid "299ea40c-ff2e-55e3-9ec0-9f7d8d31dedf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056307} {:answer/id #uuid "3138227c-8c5f-52fb-8708-ee41e7e743dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056308} {:answer/id #uuid "34747a1f-7188-5548-9064-656c70f5fa08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056309}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056305} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6ac5b0b-cfa5-5e6b-a1be-33551e6bff78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "38edf6bf-dbb8-50a2-9424-49395fd3a840" :question/math-academy-id "q-1120" :question/problem "If $\\frac{{\\log }_{7}⁡(x+1)}{2}=1$, then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\log }_{7}⁡(x+1)}{2} & =1 \\\\ {\\log }_{7}⁡(x+1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{7}⁡(x+1) & =2 \\\\ {7}^{{\\log }_{7}⁡(x+1)} & ={7}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${7}^{{\\log }_{7}⁡(x+1)}=x+1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={7}^{2} \\\\ x+1 & =49 \\\\ x & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0ff914a-1317-5490-ba59-b2437dd6665a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056365} {:answer/id #uuid "be028415-acff-5d6d-8080-1a4b0687fc37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k =-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056366} {:answer/id #uuid "28d6cf2e-8b16-58ce-af42-d46cb399a7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k =-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056367} {:answer/id #uuid "3882486c-1efa-5685-9f10-e83963bf1488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056368} {:answer/id #uuid "585ec4a5-edb5-5cf4-907c-653192911ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056365} :answer-field/id #uuid "463bd4e4-2bee-5ee4-b6dd-f8a4f765f86b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056364}] :question/id #uuid "cda11aab-d0f1-5aed-9faf-9b4214024dc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-11651" :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle C = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\det (C) = 3$, what is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\det(C)&=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=2\\cdot k-3\\cdot5 \\\\\n&=2k-15.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(C)=3$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2k-15&=3 \\\\\n2k&=18 \\\\\nk&=9.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc68d302-4e05-53f4-a13b-62357c5a7653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439477} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd87d1e-fd23-5dab-bc43-900e69f9433c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439478}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439477} :answer-field/id #uuid "91107b81-23d7-5b15-b57e-bf4ab1565fe8" :answer-field/key "$det(M)=10$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440096} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70ca64ce-81b4-5636-ba58-791451017b65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439479} {:answer/id #uuid "e05c2887-d7f6-57d2-a4c2-2f2da18a616f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439480}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439479} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2548bbb-9529-57ff-80c9-7eb2135baee7" :answer-field/key "$det(M)=-5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440097} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b319f9de-b31e-5b7f-b008-19047e4bc681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439481} {:answer/id #uuid "7743ec0d-8e7c-52c8-b5bf-a2c3ff1639ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439482}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439482} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3fd9d3c-6b34-5cd5-b3aa-e7329f24b386" :answer-field/key "$M$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440098} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8899c4e3-2645-5f8b-9127-fbf8c6f48ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667932} {:answer/id #uuid "478db39d-a240-5926-ab48-11c8e190272d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667933}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186667932} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae205eab-db1e-56c2-ac7d-5bc619d0d0ac" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d0d9bc49-c94e-5174-9068-2abf75c42684" :question/math-academy-id "q-128490" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 4\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}(12-15)-0+\\frac{1}{3}(0+9\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{5}+3\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\det (M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$M$ is a singular matrix” is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f06230e-8816-51ce-b392-2abd455f3b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285208} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8539bc-f4a1-50db-90f6-0b45e3ceff04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285517} {:answer/id #uuid "52f9a129-aa6f-59fe-9748-78c62d9d1a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285550} {:answer/id #uuid "d79adfbb-4209-5e76-80b7-c3a2f369dbcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286751} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b8e647-e565-5c04-9e76-013451742246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287048}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285517} :answer-field/id #uuid "cec0d804-b8fa-5b33-8525-d5694c64c34d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ce77ea41-d1eb-55f8-914d-e1a94a42a6b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-18279" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+4\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(6-0)+2(0+1)+4(0+3) \\\\  & =6+2+12 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-4-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21}=\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7576155e-753f-578e-a4ec-c1e146863345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285876} {:answer/id #uuid "7804ba9f-5287-5069-b31b-fbfc92859355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285906} {:answer/id #uuid "94a9a448-efa8-5e13-8fb2-e9929ad565e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286140} {:answer/id #uuid "e6986340-6e6f-5163-a46c-b7ce43a43001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286898} {:answer/id #uuid "ff4f3922-637f-578e-9c1d-529afc788b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287128}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285906} :answer-field/id #uuid "00f3d5af-0a6c-5449-9acd-f6ef2a68a144" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "882c4c5b-7c6a-5276-989d-a6c4749ae87b" :question/math-academy-id "q-18280" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =5\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5(0-2)+0+3(-2+3) \\\\  & =-10+0+3 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-15-0 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33}=\\frac{1}{-7}\\cdot (-15)=\\frac{15}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f479fae-ec27-5f79-825f-bb81f5b62e2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(a - b)∣13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057443} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd84d73-6ee3-5017-9508-51ce3957a53f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13∣(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057444} {:answer/id #uuid "06e7bdea-bfd9-5e64-8d90-e4d1354e9307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13∣(a - b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057445} {:answer/id #uuid "62e03779-45a0-55f8-937e-8853639ba73d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(a + b)∣13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057446}] :answer-field/id #uuid "826bdcea-c3d9-5913-b7ae-ce90cd7504f8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057442} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97a79526-994b-5ad2-a785-a97f95d612a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "prime numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057448} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f4d7fd-9345-5a83-a714-7a35344d6d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integer divisibility" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057449} {:answer/id #uuid "64ec0ebd-f017-5766-ac33-84c1edfd2b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answe