r-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2fe9bcdf-6522-538a-a529-97fa9381c2b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-138944" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡2t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡2t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{4(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{16{\\sin }^{2}⁡t{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t(16{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\sqrt{16{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot |\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\cdot \\sqrt{16{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{16{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfec902a-5cf6-5bd9-be1a-e3d96e022a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sin^{2} tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126447} {:answer/id #uuid "f93032b9-b3fc-5327-adc8-0e6f60a52086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\cos^{2} tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126448} {:answer/id #uuid "3efaf6b4-0d42-576d-955e-71bfd02a85ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\cos tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126449} {:answer/id #uuid "5a771360-6985-5115-abec-e29fd81217f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sin tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126450} {:answer/id #uuid "60a911b4-cf87-501e-9618-b1012b51b0c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sin t\\cos tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126451}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d9753294-5be4-52de-ae49-8ba1ea6b80bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126446} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14487349-5e36-54ef-b555-7e68b1718167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388587} {:answer/id #uuid "cee0d74a-83cd-5176-8f5a-2e4575be8319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388588} {:answer/id #uuid "ac546779-6d22-58f5-83b0-983de3177f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388589} {:answer/id #uuid "9335e776-53f3-549d-88f0-a3477bc64cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388590} {:answer/id #uuid "630ba254-d590-5a7a-87c2-7823432453fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388587} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbd66017-723a-53a9-aea0-dff9e46c0339" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "67f3e12f-3cac-5a99-84d0-463e68f2be51" :question/math-academy-id "q-138981" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{4{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+4{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{4({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t=1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129507 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "683571f2-07f3-587b-82c8-c91f90742301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276409} {:answer/id #uuid "875f9442-0384-5276-af8d-5acd1c256c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276697} {:answer/id #uuid "8db57813-6b8a-52b7-ac15-2e606c882c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276762} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3728d1-c1cf-5eef-a87d-a43db22f1937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277016} {:answer/id #uuid "cae52c74-ecff-52ad-a806-db5d5566d474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277280} :answer-field/id #uuid "55a2c95f-16e9-55cb-83b5-4438e90426c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ba3c81ec-9070-50b3-af3a-82e4e50bb35c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160380" :question/problem "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2u\\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ 3u\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$ and $y(u,v)=3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-(-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =6u({\\cos }^{2}⁡v+{\\cos }^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & =6u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,2)}=6(1)=6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49e9a4ed-cc02-552b-850f-590a1eabb508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{25}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374008} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6da4e6-4d70-5d57-830d-2c3550356100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{25}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374009} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c418fd-e2e2-58e2-b16d-b624dda6a4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374010} {:answer/id #uuid "0d81db19-07f6-520d-bd7f-8449d3b4156e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374011} {:answer/id #uuid "91e95a72-f432-5a4d-9e2e-324cee6736c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374012}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374012} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3d2f399-c08f-57b6-b31d-d84d8b9705c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "699f2901-2747-5828-8f8f-087336e97be3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161754" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=25.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $5$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{{\\rho }^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80de4587-dcf9-5d8e-b56f-181dbf1054a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126720} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a24dd0-408f-55e9-b605-390bb8a9c014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126721} {:answer/id #uuid "058d611c-3957-50eb-a39a-d6ad31b6422b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126722} {:answer/id #uuid "6369fd64-4d73-5d21-97c2-0a0348fe4e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126723} {:answer/id #uuid "67e212c8-9935-544e-a440-8c077803cbfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126724}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186126724} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac852333-df04-5ad7-904c-15ecf5d8effb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f503a261-8e11-5e60-9902-aca301725e95" :question/math-academy-id "q-162988" :question/problem "![](images/82/82ed1d9c8965c445488256f2319bd2e1a25b2f685d5ad39c88f2b2ba7f7f5525.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(81-0)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9M}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3056ba5d-f7d1-55a3-8c91-5da94f695f90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426091} {:answer/id #uuid "32392a3b-31a7-5a44-8746-b4c85858d143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426092} {:answer/id #uuid "77d105e7-3ddf-576d-b7fd-6524428a349e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426108} {:answer/id #uuid "7faf887c-1e04-5044-ade7-d44593ea0768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426111} {:answer/id #uuid "d52f3d90-a693-584a-a56c-1a94156d2638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426136}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426136} :answer-field/id #uuid "1239f0fe-7d29-5b4f-895a-1d599c74b28e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "41c034f9-14b4-5bc4-9351-1214f69c2032" :question/math-academy-id "q-162990" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a85b0be5f8e5db2cf4d1716e4afe4a2780c500d94a1ce282a9d0044f93ba084.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{3}$ and the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{6}{x}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{6}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{6}{r}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}(\\frac{6}{r}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ){r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}6{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}6{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =2{r}^{3}{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2(3\\sqrt{3}-1)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =(3\\sqrt{3}-1)\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}-1) \\\\  & =10-4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129564 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165dd9be-8787-52e5-af21-883b13bcaa26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288182} {:answer/id #uuid "4d423f60-e247-5193-81e2-deec61035167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288568} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc87fcb-e311-53c6-a0b4-26ad31d76151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288800} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe9fb75-9a26-5f50-935f-5dab8b9ff80a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289495} {:answer/id #uuid "e7eaf9e2-a3f6-5edd-a709-9deef4b12842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289574}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288568} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1a5a4c7-f9f3-5318-9f7c-d8b90cf164b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "83599989-7e1a-5f2f-adf9-0de9e207ef83" :question/math-academy-id "q-24247" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c2644fc906d4588bc39aa92d887836ff8942f5eb4b28de1baf3dc1030d2dca67.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3},$ what is the value of $\\cos ⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/49/4942cec8983dd2bddddd0ba2f8f9332b1b82f3999a44adb138b905457d39c633.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos ⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\cos ⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos ⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\cos ⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\cos ⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033fe036-222d-5d10-957e-6471d19dc5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321179} {:answer/id #uuid "2c482aed-cfdd-5bf4-acda-648b685f18fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321489} {:answer/id #uuid "2d64e163-f5c4-5413-9570-dd5493a5c5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321500} {:answer/id #uuid "c7bd880a-2b37-5ebe-b42b-b4c304a5a933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322697} {:answer/id #uuid "e11d49f5-3c92-5fce-b032-36bf3f972d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322906}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322906} :answer-field/id #uuid "52e35bc9-d423-567e-8c0e-63b05f91b6ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4fce4c9f-341f-5617-8150-093fe040d196" :question/math-academy-id "q-37385" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $\\det (A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-2)(-2)) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=0.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06e1daaa-dc7f-57a9-81b8-22fbcb301a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321201} {:answer/id #uuid "81eb5789-543d-50e7-ad83-a4bfccc26cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322171} {:answer/id #uuid "948f60f4-5a9b-5003-8084-be16c06fae10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322328} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0190ae-90dd-5b97-b446-a58698208c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322370} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c111aa-65f0-573e-955f-760529a5afe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322531} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ab9650-e7f9-53e4-be63-104407f7ffee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0beb6dad-ec13-5ce0-a209-c7b3cfc3efa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-38078" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2u\\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ 3u\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$ and $y(u,v)=3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-(-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =6u({\\cos }^{2}⁡v+{\\cos }^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & =6u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129658 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08b45aef-de01-54a9-97af-2432a20f12ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324049} {:answer/id #uuid "7669bab5-d76e-5e61-aedf-e5ce81bd8e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324956} {:answer/id #uuid "76ff9d65-e880-5ea1-bb66-05a27a0097f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324960} {:answer/id #uuid "82e7c3df-51de-58ac-8741-a036eb7b0002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325073} {:answer/id #uuid "fb95e0a7-38fc-511f-81e0-a8348a5040a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326184} :answer-field/id #uuid "84f73a10-2514-50db-963e-cff84592339b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f665dff-8596-5242-8dd6-d19332732320" :question/math-academy-id "q-38506" :question/problem "Find the scale factor of the transformation represented by the rotation-scaling matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & -2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The scale factor $r$ of the rotation-scaling matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{bmatrix}$ can be found using the formula \n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{\\det (A)}=\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{\\det (A)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129664 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302641c7-2a38-5e74-ab70-6a73f29cc4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324355} {:answer/id #uuid "33ce560c-2bd3-570e-940d-f6a3d17f8eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324384} {:answer/id #uuid "58dd9826-4a88-5372-89a2-1ae654805212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324709} {:answer/id #uuid "716c287e-e73b-59c5-b606-885eb23928c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324906} {:answer/id #uuid "75d82695-8eb9-5223-bfda-fe002343cbea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324951}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324906} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dc28151-eed7-5ddf-86ea-98d2a88d62bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7d6641ce-39fa-5304-84ca-9071211edd5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38565" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +13 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=-2\\pm 3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+3\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1-3\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}{{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129683 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47e55c69-1866-588d-872a-b402386f3e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368516} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf80517-57f1-595e-a0c6-e931586cc9b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368517} {:answer/id #uuid "d3509644-e4ea-51d2-a471-d35c123da88d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368518} {:answer/id #uuid "00e54f49-e547-573b-a5d0-5831fa66ad14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368519} {:answer/id #uuid "6e481d77-a41c-5160-be87-d2c659c36170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368520}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368519} :answer-field/id #uuid "38d5c140-2980-57f5-9c02-49b92c04d215" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "58dcf2b4-e0ab-52d4-9f82-d78c5ef8a397" :question/math-academy-id "q-38655" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{array}\\right],\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )^{2}-1(-1)) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "832eaf1a-2aaa-5d69-b401-e53dab669612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c4/c41a72ee3c171c5e5058322a5c017eaa4d63f1df1be872000cd0031071c8da01.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127808} {:answer/id #uuid "dec73c62-2784-5513-9391-6631cf7d604a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/21/21c648df52c22bff4ffd772d268d72c7506ca470465133c067fb11e0f611c9d6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127809} {:answer/id #uuid "e3597aec-8606-5d77-9fc3-b7e915a782b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f4/f4e270fc087f663e64f93ca15adfebb3b591dd78fbcbc187f8b02c4899521bcd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127810} {:answer/id #uuid "e9717218-6bf9-5f64-8bf7-9a8fefc6bc39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e2/e2117c19adc490559adaeb7b5080469c244b56bedbf3850f1e2ea372c44e0e8d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127811} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca7c69c-6946-529c-a586-8665a33d366d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fe/fe26b7d100fb5e9b092531f759a4e9e82c136cd305fcf9fef53446efe2f7fa04.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127812}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186127812} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd42f8f0-d812-5a23-b53a-3d6e5909e169" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c6f0a65d-6910-53c6-b1a3-b177adcca755" :question/math-academy-id "q-39208" :question/problem "![](images/2c/2c835e071e972664ebe02d0046a1bd6652474ce92155709dcb000161ce8f5a2a.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a rotation-scaling matrix. So, we need to find its scale factor and the angle of rotation.\n\nWe can find the scale factor using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{\\det (A)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent $A$ as a product of a rotation and a scaling matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$  and scale the result by a scale factor of $2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/42/42d0455347222e21192aba7694f1b2eb3f2f9a10cc8a7c8112312003fb2eb9c9.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ec0914-5828-5c4a-81c9-ce52c1488ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323999} {:answer/id #uuid "350992e5-036c-535c-8a4b-6a5ab4c6fa16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324405} {:answer/id #uuid "81e74165-da06-5f93-ae92-aeef9db19857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325063} {:answer/id #uuid "a528987d-54c7-5615-ae6d-cbc6a9db0c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325376} {:answer/id #uuid "efd74025-7232-59ef-bfa0-45a2a9dfad28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326087}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325376} :answer-field/id #uuid "60477555-dd44-54e5-9698-cd175b7154c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "24830b7e-b0be-5ebb-9a57-2cd58c40cf8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-39299" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +v\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=v\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=w.\n$$\n\nYou may assume that $\\theta $ is a fixed constant." :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129758 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf20c60-05f2-5708-980b-fd1527c75409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481802} {:answer/id #uuid "1ebc1e7a-b1a7-5e33-ad02-11d00e19214e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481856} {:answer/id #uuid "79c2061c-f9aa-5de0-aba6-c75d63373665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482057} {:answer/id #uuid "89d7f971-7086-5fea-903e-93de814adaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482096} {:answer/id #uuid "a99f008d-ff7c-53f6-9542-fcd0523c8372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482173}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482096} :answer-field/id #uuid "069ecdc3-aac2-564e-8b8f-d73e6c94ad8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1a921fbf-9bfb-5cd0-be15-5730003e7ec3" :question/math-academy-id "q-40089" :question/problem "![](images/22/2206a1bc02acb9ddccc5991a4c8d36c9e5d3255e72239e8f7e81908e10faab48.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=18y$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $z\\ge 0,$ shown above. \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xy\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $3$ centered at $(0,3,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=18y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =18y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =18\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/57/578c7f81a0dae8339772dec9a9407fb380866e5dde5fdd18fee04feb6afe5749.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=xy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c5b75d4-b6ee-51d4-bbba-3978adc99049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 cos ⁡ θ}ρ 2 sin ⁡ θ sin 2 ⁡ ϕdρdθdϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128036} {:answer/id #uuid "99df22c1-627f-5da2-a594-fd1a4749125d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 cos ⁡ ϕ}ρ 2 sin 3 ⁡ ϕdρdθdϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128037} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a5b43d-3476-5d85-b63e-c2fa126b6a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4 sin ⁡ θ sin ⁡ ϕ}ρ 3 cos ⁡ θ sin 2 ⁡ ϕdρdθdϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128038} {:answer/id #uuid "f0cb97c4-383f-5a41-987a-2899845af08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 cos ⁡ θ}ρ 3 cos ⁡ θ sin 2 ⁡ ϕdρdθdϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128039} {:answer/id #uuid "6e691881-57e2-530e-a0d1-4ca867753f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4 sin ⁡ θ sin ⁡ ϕ}ρ 2 sin 3 ⁡ ϕdρdθdϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128040}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e9d80b8e-d353-54d6-a7f4-60be0f908b6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128035} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a03abacc-5882-5030-8479-541347fa4de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375143} {:answer/id #uuid "80951fc0-e6d1-5c44-9a51-16b64414443f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375144} {:answer/id #uuid "0f60d84d-845a-5beb-9d11-6f369ea6e39c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375145} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb209d7-a648-5399-ba3a-9150109ccb74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375146} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb0c55d-2333-58b0-900b-e47b7cebb208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375147}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375147} :answer-field/id #uuid "b045d2b1-9fa2-59d7-882d-91bdb84b2b83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "182369a1-0318-58db-8fc8-603690ab0d2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-40152" :question/problem "![](images/41/41b5ff62b86d0cc7ce341a013f1815b7bb203f5b93cb52c525844f9bb93839b2.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4y,$ shown above. \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/cc/cc029278b96456462e746450c8feeb9c9ec6169a9b36f56915758ffcc1f8dd70.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "956f961d-1d3f-556b-905b-28f1a7397dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}ρ 2 sin 3 ⁡ ϕdρdθdϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128042} {:answer/id #uuid "a15be279-159d-5d83-9c2f-55304e59f306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{π / 2}^{3 π / 3}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}ρ 4 cos 2 ⁡ ϕ sin ⁡ ϕdρdθdϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128043} {:answer/id #uuid "b2525949-8b50-5e22-8e3f-266dc4ccb77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}ρ 4 sin 3 ⁡ ϕdρdθdϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128044} {:answer/id #uuid "0a64c450-5aa8-5fdb-a66d-ecd140ac3877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}ρ 2 sin 3 ⁡ ϕdρdθdϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128045} {:answer/id #uuid "68975495-7d81-525c-b6fc-978e8cbf52fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{π / 2}^{3 π / 2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}ρ 4 sin 3 ⁡ ϕdρdθdϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128046}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b9e8cbfe-3067-5895-9897-29b4f1bc7160" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128041} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3173be2c-57d2-5935-8e9a-9e6ba5b61ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375149} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9f988c-c1cf-539f-9e97-2253899e4e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375150} {:answer/id #uuid "f278b853-ff03-5372-bbf4-435412255f28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375151} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b76952-6854-53da-854d-313559831a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375152} {:answer/id #uuid "03678bef-6673-5339-b887-d3f2a24c29c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375153}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375153} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b2fbb19-8d38-51b8-9607-b92099926e98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4dc06060-1fda-590b-bf44-a946ea904294" :question/math-academy-id "q-40154" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ for $x\\le 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/de/de9f30a4f54875e05a8cedb6c73af37ad7146c56ab62d0b2b2d3f3fa359160ca.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\rho }^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}{\\sin }^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61dc7c0-d3f8-5a52-a767-d5a5950ed61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128132} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3acfbd-450b-529e-9e81-f6898d0afc15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128133} {:answer/id #uuid "425120b8-9a7a-509b-b2b1-16f549aee0d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\tan θ = \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128134} {:answer/id #uuid "51495d9a-03f0-5f49-aeab-3c6ea5811fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128135} {:answer/id #uuid "681b517b-c0da-555d-9464-011b10f62e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\tan θ = \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128136}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a1f2f1dc-9d19-53e5-af38-cc9ea5f6ad94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128131} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2ab46d8-4b20-5cb0-bd03-d4843745957e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402771} {:answer/id #uuid "1281daa1-21d2-521b-ba7b-2f63be883923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402772} {:answer/id #uuid "cf49d4c9-aad2-5d7f-bca3-cd2582a8d854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402773} {:answer/id #uuid "1efc176a-2f08-5690-b21d-dbaa2aa9e6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402774} {:answer/id #uuid "9257c9b4-8a20-5a75-b76e-4b9c11a339a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402775}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402774} :answer-field/id #uuid "737f5b1b-3e66-5582-9362-63911dfedfe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "299c91a4-6289-5d24-ace1-495b52634a62" :question/math-academy-id "q-42043" :question/problem "![](images/91/919b9e8f2a9493fd0dda3e0d7bf66de9af054fc4ed9c0fcf7a68329fdbbaffdd.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2},$ what is the value of $\\tan ⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/94/948610f67267e6ec1b845d2b4e1bc4030b81a1080bcb9a44d9f974ff78c3f7b0.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos ⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\cos ⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos ⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\cos ⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\cos ⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\tan ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sin ⁡\\theta }{\\cos ⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd51e610-6f03-5601-be97-90a082867f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128804} {:answer/id #uuid "115c217e-8e0a-5c5b-8302-ae521adc07f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128805} {:answer/id #uuid "232b05d7-2819-5154-96f7-710edeff9ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128806} {:answer/id #uuid "4505c771-cef0-508f-89e7-d4849ae94ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128807} {:answer/id #uuid "6325e896-5085-50b5-ab25-23a0ebf6535e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128808}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128804} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8292c76-a1e0-5537-aaa6-9b5b73963ff1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "929376bc-8d45-5684-ae94-b99b8cb09d33" :question/math-academy-id "q-83968" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd1a5a140c3a2d430312d6f6ceeb21d6b7282119c8ec53365553ff845d8c64d5.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at \n$(0,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-{y}^{2}-y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{\\sin }^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({\\sin }^{5}⁡0-{\\sin }^{5}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130323 :tutorial/content "We've seen that we can write a rotation-scaling matrix $A$ is the product of a rotation and a scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nA\n\t= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{array} \\right]}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\cos   \\theta  & - \\sin   \\theta  \\\\ \\sin   \\theta  & \\cos   \\theta \\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nSo, the natural question now is, how does a rotation-scaling matrix \n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{blue}a} & - {\\color{red}b} \\\\ {\\color{red}b} & {\\color{blue}a}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nrelate to the individual rotation and scaling matrices?\n\nFirst, setting the two equal to each other, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{blue}a} & - {\\color{red}b} \\\\ {\\color{red}b} & {\\color{blue}a}\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{array} \\right]}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\cos   \\theta  & - \\sin   \\theta  \\\\ \\sin   \\theta  & \\cos   \\theta \\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nFrom this, we can find the scale factor and angle of rotation of a rotation-scaling matrix. First, notice that we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}a} = {\\color{blue}r\\cos\\theta}, \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad {\\color{red}b} = {\\color{red}r\\sin\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTo find $r$ in terms of $a$ and $b,$ we can square and then add the two equations above. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2} & ={r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the scale factor $r$ is given by\n\n$$\nr = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2} = \\sqrt{\\det{A}}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the rotation angle $\\theta$ in terms of $a$ and $b,$ we start with the equations\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}a} = {\\color{blue}r\\cos\\theta}, \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad {\\color{red}b} = {\\color{red}r\\sin\\theta}.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{b}{a}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\theta = \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac ba \\right).\n$$\n\nIt's also possible to recover both the original scaling *and* rotation matrix once the scale factor $r$ is known. Let's see how that works." :tutorial/id #uuid "1fb2c1d9-0856-55ca-945a-8e01f2e58902" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2014 :tutorial/title "Finding the Scale Factor and Rotation Angle Of a Rotation-Scaling Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130333 :tutorial/content "By the Pythagorean theorem, we know that the equation \n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = 1\n$$\n\nholds for any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle. \n\nNow, we also know that for any point on the unit circle, the coordinates $(x,y)$ are related to the central angle $\\theta$ via the formulas\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Pythagorean Identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equation is called the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity**, and it holds for *every* angle $\\theta.$ This includes negative angles and angles that are larger than one full rotation." :tutorial/id #uuid "2adb34b1-9f8d-5f02-bafd-029ac9251084" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2721 :tutorial/title "The Pythagorean Identity"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130457 :tutorial/content "Similar to transformations in the plane, we can also consider transformations of three-dimensional regions.\n\nFor example, the transformation\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{array} \\right]} = \\mathbf{T}\\left( {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{array} \\right]} \\right) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\sin   v \\cos   w \\\\ u \\sin   v \\sin   w \\\\ u \\cos   v\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ \n\nmaps a rectangular prism in Cartesian $uvw$-space onto one-eighth of a sphere in Cartesian $xyz$-space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3e1ee5a6116d57cc1c3419b55bf0a9701b9f5d4d93931d053ed4a05b83a5d8f0.png)\n\n \n\nIn general, if a transformation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v , w ) \\\\ y ( u , v , w ) \\\\ z ( u , v , w )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ \n\nis a $C^1$ transformation, meaning that it and its first derivatives are continuous, then the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFor example, to compute the Jacobian determinant of our transformation above, we first calculate the required partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\begin{vmatrix}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w\\end{vmatrix}+u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot {u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot ({\\cos }^{2}⁡w+{\\sin }^{2}⁡w)+u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot u{\\sin }^{2}⁡v\\cdot ({\\cos }^{2}⁡w+{\\sin }^{2}⁡w) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡v+{u}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot ({\\cos }^{2}⁡v+{\\sin }^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's get some practice at computing Jacobians of three-dimensional transformations." :tutorial/id #uuid "610a6348-ed80-507a-b390-560e9bc1e4ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7237 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130460 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$ \n\nWe'll assume that $\\mathbf T$ is a $\\boldsymbol{C}^1$ **transformation**, which means that  $x$ and $y$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on an open set $U$ contained in $\\Bbb R^2.$ \n\nThe **Jacobian determinant** of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}=\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe sometimes refer to the Jacobian determinant simply as the **Jacobian.**\n\nLet's compute the Jacobian determinant of the following transformation:\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} = \\mathbf{T}\\left( {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \\right) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\cos   v \\\\ u \\sin   v\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ \n\nTo compute the Jacobian determinant of this function, we first calculate the required partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)-(-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =u{\\cos }^{2}⁡v+u{\\sin }^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =u({\\cos }^{2}⁡v+{\\sin }^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe'll spend some time discussing the meaning behind the Jacobian shortly. But for now, let's get some practice at computing it." :tutorial/id #uuid "f0ea7366-d3d6-5645-99d8-72f7febb8d19" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7668 :tutorial/title "The Jacobian Determinant"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130466 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad\ny = \\sin{t}, \\qquad\nt \\in [0,\\pi].\n$$\n\nIf we rotate this curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we obtain a sphere of radius $1.$ \n\n![](images/d0/d0d323b764b70d3f2830d821636a9bc27b40e7f7e9f45df909c81dd279dc16e4.png)\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of our surface of revolution can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-2\\pi (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-2\\pi (-1-1) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f849fe7f-670f-5a7f-8dc7-dbbd33a876f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7707 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130468 :tutorial/content "Throughout this lesson, we've used the fact that \n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)} \\right| = \\rho^2 \\sin{\\phi}.\n$$\n\nLet's now prove this result.\n\nGiven the spherical coordinate transformations\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\n\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \n\nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi,\n$$\n\nwe first  calculate the required partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂\\rho } & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta } & =-\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂ϕ} & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂\\rho } & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂ϕ} & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂\\rho } & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂ϕ} & =-\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & -\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ & 0 & -\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\begin{vmatrix}-\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\end{vmatrix}-\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & -\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot (-{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta ))-\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot ({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ-{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =-{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ({\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =-{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)} \\right| = \\rho^2 \\sin{\\phi}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "86bd6cae-b4e6-5da9-80b3-b884c320bda3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7720 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Jacobian for Spherical Polar Coordinates"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133712 :question/id #uuid "74c9ecfb-5005-53ff-b7a9-db798488587b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9554" :question/problem "Let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2 \\cos t \\rangle$ for $t \\in \\left [0, 2\\pi \\right).$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D) \n= \\iint\\limits_D  \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} -  \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right) \\mathrm{d}A\n= \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A}}}\\: \\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y) = -y$ and $Q(x,y) = 0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*\n\n![](images/a5/a5eeb5db90f9ee0d86a49e5bbf02629c7886fa019db7cff3c4d0e1eb43e8fd75.png)" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y) = -y$ and $Q(x,y) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n- \\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = \\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'(t) = \\left\\langle  2\\cos t ,\\:  -2 \\sin t \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4{\\cos }^{2}⁡t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $4\\cos^2 t.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a97740-bf4e-5245-a223-1105ad76868c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238086} {:answer/id #uuid "0ccb496a-1f3e-5f9c-9be5-7756bb97a385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238169} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbfd16e-b04d-5762-91cf-d69f6af7923d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239432} {:answer/id #uuid "a5578604-9539-5565-8bbb-579ff2d23c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239490} {:answer/id #uuid "ff74f82e-113d-5ba7-9d63-962e019ecfd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238086} :answer-field/id #uuid "50e2f23b-8197-5ffd-805f-f3ecff761a73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e7a8c44b-b300-5e66-9428-e03f400ebf5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-105416" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,g(t)={\\sin }^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)\\}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{2}(t)\\}$ equals $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{1}(t)\\}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{1}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({\\sin }^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({\\sin }^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while ${\\sin }^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({\\sin }^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({\\sin }^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{0}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d95993-6fce-50a6-9da0-c38e0b9621ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130961} {:answer/id #uuid "5b48ccdf-94f7-5b30-b9e9-c2b9d97a02fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130965} {:answer/id #uuid "6eea3a9c-148b-5bbc-b698-c42d3d976666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521016} {:answer/id #uuid "81f2f5fe-c2fa-5a14-a755-d561c640ccb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521062} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce79bec-631d-5e6e-9ada-b94ec4a0d8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521119}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521062} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfbd2886-ea55-5ec0-b21b-dda63331dd6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "008f670b-b77a-5939-9c4f-8602d138cc7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162377" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-0\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {\\cos }^{2}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {\\cos }^{2}⁡(\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(\\pi t)}{2}+\\frac{\\pi t}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0-(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ce7e4c-f358-59e1-83ec-c12d185581ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326996} {:answer/id #uuid "4c81f4db-d861-547d-a06f-42826d808a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327598} {:answer/id #uuid "ae66382d-5670-5d2c-88f6-a6e2c5123b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328448} {:answer/id #uuid "b25772cd-af85-5333-aa05-8ae7885a5e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328481} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a08351-6bdd-5434-861e-a5f6e60e14fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328448} :answer-field/id #uuid "033cf0fb-22d2-5315-bb5f-00c4ceeb328a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "663f08e4-70a8-54d4-a465-2c24f8e60288" :question/math-academy-id "q-42254" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Find $⟨{\\sin }^{2}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨{\\sin }^{2}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}]-[\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4731d43a-1037-5467-97a5-e798a96ef9d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327555} {:answer/id #uuid "658a8d30-d9af-5e26-83da-9719b1081d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327818} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1056e6-0216-54a8-a348-f7fd16cd261c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327879} {:answer/id #uuid "cb4f71df-9ae0-539d-bc37-b9dfa1a2efd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328715} {:answer/id #uuid "f61627d1-d9a7-5fa0-8601-a797fc2e5d46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329093} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ecac406-0aef-584c-9d48-0a6c47d439e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a804e446-7433-50c7-a8ce-2769d7a93237" :question/math-academy-id "q-42459" :question/problem "Find $4\\det (A)+4\\det (B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- For the matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\det (A)=2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-1\\cdot 1=3.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has proportional columns, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}1} & \\sqrt{2} & {\\color{blue}2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2} & 1 & {\\color{blue}4} \\\\ {\\color{blue}3} & 6 & {\\color{blue}6}\\end{bmatrix}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\det (B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4\\det (A)+4\\det (B)=4\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 0=12.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c225eb90-e2e0-5316-bf2c-433fb3c69768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132410} {:answer/id #uuid "320e4a8c-eedf-5588-98e8-c145e0ace715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132411} {:answer/id #uuid "43de3423-2ffd-5930-9ee0-ea5001c9a16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132412} {:answer/id #uuid "53f34a5f-dc76-5b22-9b1f-04217732f9e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132413} {:answer/id #uuid "796055cc-72c1-5b68-9805-3a39d9b06647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132414}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186132410} :answer-field/id #uuid "33879ed2-4ff8-5792-966a-daa49f7bc975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dcb38d39-eeab-5f94-bc9b-9cb1d6df2c04" :question/math-academy-id "q-42461" :question/problem "Find $2\\det (A)+4\\det (B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ is a triangular matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal: \n\n$$\n\\det (A)=1\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=4\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has two identical rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\det (B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\det (A)+4\\det (B)=2\\cdot 4+4\\cdot 0=8.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b168093-06ee-59d2-a609-623e2ec4e449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327451} {:answer/id #uuid "4588a6ee-dc15-5f53-86b2-b586c559c781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327542} {:answer/id #uuid "4f67b91c-0f60-548d-8a33-48f8a99a74b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3\\pi }{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327627} {:answer/id #uuid "59ccd062-fdab-5575-acd3-736c657f5624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327721} {:answer/id #uuid "ebca9d1f-42e9-5b29-8145-61126df47e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328998}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328998} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fca567e-a930-5cf5-8fe9-bab4358653b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f27dcc08-cb74-544e-b846-2c5aa548a3f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-42734" :question/problem "Let $V=C[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[\\pi ,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,{\\cos }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\left(\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{t}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{4}){|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi }{4})-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =(\\pi +0)-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡t=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135151 :tutorial/content "If an $n\\!\\times \\!n$ matrix $A$ has an entire row or column of zeros, then its determinant is equal to zero.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix $A$ given below:\n\n$$\nA=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe second row consists of all zeros, and the asterisks ($\\ast$) may represent any number.\n\nLet's compute the determinant using the Laplace expansion across the second row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2+1}({\\color{red}0})\\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)^{2+2}({\\color{red}0})\\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)^{2+3}({\\color{red}0})\\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0+0+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that it doesn't matter what numbers are represented by the asterisks ($\\ast$). Each cofactor is multiplied by zero, so the final result must be zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "cee0a552-60f2-5785-a506-63bd053364c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6888 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ba1c77-7947-50fa-9bf1-7b1374d40c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{m}{\\min ^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186137556} {:answer/id #uuid "687385c3-7a8d-58d5-b147-ba9261548880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{m^{2}}{\\min ^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186137558} {:answer/id #uuid "699eb470-cd2b-5ea6-8de7-eb6a87b032e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{m^{3}}{\\min ^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186137559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143185 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "f6e19794-83d2-526b-bdeb-8a73d4af34a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140347}] :question/id #uuid "b1b71e03-911f-56bf-ac9f-038f62d9623f" :question/math-academy-id "q-172902" :question/problem "$9hr =$ {{field-1}}\n$\\min $"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147344 :question/id #uuid "cb472181-da8e-5252-9c80-4d336e2ccf9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6615" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + 9y = 6\\cos x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\cos(3x)$ and $y_2 = \\sin(3x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x) y_1 + u_2(x) y_2\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle u_2(x) = \\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x) = 6\\cos x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos(3x), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =3{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3x)+3{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))}^{′} &=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) &=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for $u_2.$ We start by eliminating $u_1'$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\nu_1' = -\\dfrac{u_2'\\sin(3x)}{\\cos(3x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -3\\left(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x) = 2\\cos x \\cos(3x).$"} {:answer/id #uuid "310dd514-af1c-5159-a13d-b3cc2654b52b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\bmod " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149413} {:answer/id #uuid "8c54055d-4179-5e99-a522-79a2c01f2105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8(\\bmod 9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149474} {:answer/id #uuid "9eea33e2-da4b-5559-bedb-29d840768325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7(\\bmod 9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149475} {:answer/id #uuid "be5f78f9-dc9a-5861-bb14-4d36f15fded4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4(\\bmod 9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149476} {:answer/id #uuid "d7cb3727-66a3-5975-a033-2512aaa85472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6(\\bmod 9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149477} {:answer/id #uuid "248c7a91-2f3a-530c-bc55-64bc4f4d438d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(\\bmod 9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149478} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f2a3ce-9559-5e3e-9c17-927e41d5edaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(\\bmod 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149480} {:answer/id #uuid "bbbca7d0-4a38-5105-88f0-132b4a2b6e07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0(\\bmod 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149481} {:answer/id #uuid "ddfa2a17-58f9-553f-82e4-23ce301d07cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(\\bmod 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149482} {:answer/id #uuid "f90f4163-6771-573f-b549-8c214392863c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4(\\bmod 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149483} {:answer/id #uuid "2625dc13-9394-5af8-8764-25343bd1d2c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1(\\bmod 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149484} {:answer/id #uuid "ab582d0a-9222-5585-b384-1f384339f739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\bmod " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149536} {:answer/id #uuid "9b9d0e71-367f-5deb-ab38-5a6d6cd519ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(\\bmod 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151002} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d3c795-e7f5-5ab6-8c0c-0903e6c51f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1(\\bmod 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151003} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7b1941-b443-5246-a66f-35337e719044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(\\bmod 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151004} {:answer/id #uuid "362059c0-29a6-53a3-8427-64a8241c9d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4(\\bmod 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151005} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb739d4-68fc-5242-873d-ec37a7c7679e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5(\\bmod 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151006} {:answer/id #uuid "aacad3a2-3e07-554e-9871-c38b908236fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x≡2, 5$ only $(\\bmod 8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151194} {:answer/id #uuid "b022bb9b-3174-513b-92ba-c85e056f64a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x≡1, 4$ only $(\\bmod 8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151195} {:answer/id #uuid "dcbda960-e1a2-52ef-9bcb-6effb4dbef12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x≡3, 5$ only $(\\bmod 8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151196} {:answer/id #uuid "10f12ce8-f644-538b-a3bb-a6b0783dde38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x≡2, 4$ only $(\\bmod 8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151197} {:answer/id #uuid "a58c1c66-4225-59ed-ac62-43d745b36911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x≡1, 4, 7$ only $(\\bmod 9)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151201} {:answer/id #uuid "caaf3b53-6e58-51c6-88bb-c3d440356f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x≡0, 2, 4, 8$ only $(\\bmod 9)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151202} {:answer/id #uuid "2e09c2e0-29e9-58a2-bbb1-05b62282f49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x≡1, 4$ only $(\\bmod 9)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151203} {:answer/id #uuid "69bba69d-efbd-5854-8033-8baf7d4742ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x≡1, 3, 5$ only $(\\bmod 9)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151853 :question/id #uuid "28182e82-b660-59f1-a01e-90c9fc7db452" :question/math-academy-id "e-14607" :question/problem "Use trial and error to find the solutions, if any, to the congruence $2x \\equiv 4 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:6)$." :question/worked-solution "Since the modulus $n=6$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx = 0,1,2,3,4,5\n$$\n\ninto the equation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot 0 & ≡0≢4 & (\\bmod 6) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 1 & ≡2≢4 & (\\bmod 6) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 2 & ≡4 & (\\bmod 6) & ✓ \\\\ 2 \\cdot 3 & ≡0≢4 & (\\bmod 6) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 4 & ≡2≢4 & (\\bmod 6) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 5 & ≡4 & (\\bmod 6) & ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x\\equiv 2, 5$ only $(\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151855 :question/id #uuid "576db46e-0a13-5f50-ae52-7a1d973b3239" :question/math-academy-id "e-14609" :question/problem "Find the sum modulo $20$ of the solutions to the congruence $8x \\equiv 4 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:20)$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" :question/worked-solution "In the given congruence, we have $a=8$ and $n=20$, so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(8,20)=4\n$$\n\nSince $b=4$ is divisible by $4$, our congruence has $4$ solutions.\n\nFirst, we reduce the congruence using the division property of congruences:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle 8 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  4 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 20 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 4 \\cdot  2 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  4 \\cdot  1 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 4 \\cdot  5 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\cancel{4} \\cdot  2 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  \\cancel{4} \\cdot  1 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} \\cancel{4} \\cdot  5 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 2 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  1 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 5 )\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNotice that in the new congruence, we have $a_1=2$ and $n_1=5$, so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a_1,n_1)=\\text{gcd}(2,5)=1\n$$\n\nThis means our congruence has a unique solution modulo $5$.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $2\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$. To find the inverse of $2\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$, we compute the exponents of $2\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$ until we get a result of $1 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2^{2} & ≡4 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ 2^{3} & ≡4 \\cdot 2≡8≡1 \\cdot 5 + 3≡3 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ 2^{4} & ≡3 \\cdot 2≡6≡1 \\cdot 5 + 1≡1 & (\\bmod 5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, $2 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$, which means that $3$ is the multiplicative inverse of $2$ (modulo $5$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $3$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x & ≡1 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 2x & ≡3 \\cdot 1 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ (3 \\cdot 2)x & ≡3 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ x & ≡3 & (\\bmod 5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we get one solution, $x \\equiv 3 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:5)$.\n\nBut our original congruence was modulo $20$ (not modulo $5$). To find all the solutions modulo $20$, we repeatedly add $5$ modulo $20$ until we reach the number $3$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & ≡3 & (\\bmod 20) \\\\ x_{2} & ≡3 + 5≡8 & (\\bmod 20) \\\\ x_{3} & ≡8 + 5≡13 & (\\bmod 20) \\\\ x_{4} & ≡13 + 5≡18 & (\\bmod 20) \\\\ x_{5} & ≡18 + 5≡23≡1 \\cdot 20 + 3≡3≡x_{1} & (\\bmod 20)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x\\equiv 3, 8, 13, 18 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:20)$.\n\nFinally,\n$3+8+13+18 \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:20)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151869 :question/id #uuid "0c2b236d-1f39-59a1-9458-4cfb9424bc1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14663" :question/problem "Find the solutions of $50x \\equiv 120 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:298)$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $\\text{gcd}(50,298) =2$.*" :question/worked-solution "In the given congruence, we have $a=50$ and $n=298$, and we're given that\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(50,298)=2\n$$\n\nSince $b=120$ is divisible by $2$, our congruence has $2$ solutions.\n\nNext, we use the division property of congruences:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle 50 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  120 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 298 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 2 \\cdot  25 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  2 \\cdot  60 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 2 \\cdot  149 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\cancel{2} \\cdot  25 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  \\cancel{2} \\cdot  60 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} \\cancel{2} \\cdot  149 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 25 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  60 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 149 )\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNotice that in this new congruence, we have $a_1=25$ and $n_1=149$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a_1,n_1)=\\text{gcd}(25,149)=1\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence has a unique solution modulo $149$.\n\nTo find the solution, we must multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $25\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:149)$. To find the inverse of $25\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:149)$, we apply the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{ccccccccc}\\displaystyle 149 & \\displaystyle = & \\displaystyle 25 \\cdot  5 & \\displaystyle + & \\displaystyle 24 \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle \\Leftrightarrow  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} 24 & \\displaystyle = & \\displaystyle 149 - 25 \\cdot  5 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle ↙ & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle ↙ & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  &  &  \\\\ \\displaystyle 25 & \\displaystyle = & \\displaystyle 24 \\cdot  1 & \\displaystyle + & \\displaystyle 1 \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle \\Leftrightarrow  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} 1 & \\displaystyle = & \\displaystyle 25 - 24 \\cdot  1 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle ↙ & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle ↙ & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  &  &  \\\\ \\displaystyle 24 & \\displaystyle = & \\displaystyle 24 \\cdot  1 & \\displaystyle + & \\displaystyle 0 \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle \\phantom{\\Leftrightarrow} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} \\phantom{0} & \\displaystyle \\phantom{=} & \\displaystyle \\phantom{14 - 14 \\cdot  1}\\end{array}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNext, back-substituting in the usual way gives the following:\n\n$$\n1 = 6\\cdot 25 -149\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $149$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\cdot 25 - 149 & ≡1 & (\\bmod 149) \\\\ 6 \\cdot 25 & ≡1 & (\\bmod 149)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse of $25$ modulo $149$ is $6$.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $6$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}25x & ≡60 & (\\bmod 149) \\\\ 6 \\cdot 25x & ≡6 \\cdot 60 & (\\bmod 149) \\\\ x & ≡360 & (\\bmod 149) \\\\ x & ≡149 \\cdot 2 + 62 & (\\bmod 149) \\\\ x & ≡62 & (\\bmod 149)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the solution $x \\equiv 62 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:149)$.\n\nBut our original congruence was modulo $298$ (not modulo $149$). To find all the solutions modulo $298$, we repeatedly add $149$ to $62$ modulo $298$ until we reach the number $62$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & ≡62 & (\\bmod 298) \\\\ x_{2} & ≡62 + 149≡211 & (\\bmod 298) \\\\ x_{3} & ≡211 + 149≡360≡149 \\cdot 2 + 62≡x_{1} & (\\bmod 298)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx_{1}=62\\: (\\text{mod}\\:298) \\quad\\text{and}\\quad x_{2}= 211 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:298)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151949 :question/id #uuid "287a493f-e988-5cef-b0d0-4205d1eaf74e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6313" :question/problem "Find a definite integral with respect to $\\theta$ that is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} (y^2-x) \\, \\textrm ds,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r = \\cos{\\theta}\\:$ for $0 \\le \\theta \\le\\dfrac{ \\pi}4.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos^2\\theta$ and $y=\\cos\\theta \\sin{\\theta}$, where $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}4.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) =y^2-x$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{ }{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta ({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -1) \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta (-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}-{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151950 :question/id #uuid "63aa09fb-9839-5f73-9f98-2eb9784ead43" :question/math-academy-id "e-6314" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}$ along the polar curve $r=1$ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos\\theta$ and $y=\\sin\\theta$, where $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}2.\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-(0-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151951 :question/id #uuid "00b29cf2-6a73-5fd6-b8bd-04ba8c5f1a46" :question/math-academy-id "e-6315" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C} xy(1-x^2) \\,\\textrm{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the polar curve $r=\\cos{\\theta}\\:$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos^2\\theta$ and $y=\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta$, where $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) = xy(1-x^2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\cos\\theta,$ $\\textrm d u=-\\sin\\theta\\,\\textrm d \\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}-{u}^{3}(1-{u}^{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{3}-{u}^{7})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{u}^{8}}{8}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{8})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151952 :question/id #uuid "c1e6833e-efac-5bd9-a6ff-e6ca401cdf9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6466" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\n- $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $f(x) = 1-x^2$\n\n- $g: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $g(x) = \\sin x$\n\n- $h: \\mathbb{R} \\to (0, \\infty)$ defined by $h(x) = 3^x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f: X \\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. Notice that $f(x)=1-x^2$ has two roots, $x=\\pm 1.$ So, we have $f(-1)=0=f(1),$ while $-1 \\neq 1.$\n\n- II is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\sin x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $g(x) = 2.$\n\n- III is a bijection. First of all, $h$ is a surjection because $\\forall\\, y \\in (0, \\infty),$ we can choose an $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $h(x)=y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(x) & =y \\\\ {3}^{x} & =y \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{x}) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(y) \\\\ x & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify the above formula:\n\n$$\nh(x) = h \\left(\\log_3\\left(y\\right) \\right) = 3^{\\log_3\\left(y\\right)}  = y\n$$\n\nSecond, $h$ is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h({x}_{1}) & =h({x}_{2}) \\\\ {3}^{{x}_{1}} & ={3}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{{x}_{1}}) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{{x}_{2}}) \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $h$ is both surjective and injective, we conclude that $h$ is bijective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151979 :question/id #uuid "44ab89b2-3453-5a38-9968-7b17a6c04bd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6747" :question/problem "Suppose we encrypt the five-letter English word $\\text{ALBUM}$ to ciphertext using the Caesar cipher with key $k=16$. Assuming that we use the usual English alphabet (shown below) during the encryption, what ciphertext word results from the encryption?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" :question/worked-solution "To encrypt a word using the Caesar cipher with key $k=16$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the word in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}12$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Add $k=16$ to each index modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0 + 16≡16 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 11 + 16≡27≡1 + 26≡1 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 1 + 16≡17 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 20 + 16≡36≡10 + 26≡10 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 12 + 16≡28≡2 + 26≡2 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\text{QBRKC}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151980 :question/id #uuid "6195da75-d0ea-5ee4-b52a-1e03521f4784" :question/math-academy-id "e-6748" :question/problem "Suppose we encrypt a $4$-letter English word using the Caesar cipher with key $k=14$ and get the ciphertext $\\text{QCBS}$ as a result. Assuming that during the encryption we used the usual English alphabet (shown below), what was the original (plain text) word?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" :question/worked-solution "To decipher a message encrypted using the Caesar cipher with key $k=14$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}18$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Subtract $k=14$ from each index modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}16 - 14≡2 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 2 - 14≡ - 12≡ - 12 + 26≡14 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 1 - 14≡ - 13≡ - 13 + 26≡13 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 18 - 14≡4 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\text{CONE}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151987 :question/id #uuid "69feaab1-0c57-50dc-aeff-21c798e1859b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6812" :question/problem "Suppose we encrypt the four-letter English word $\\text{TALK}$ to ciphertext using the linear cipher with key $a=7$. Assuming that we use the usual English alphabet (shown below) during the encryption, what ciphertext word results from the encryption?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" :question/worked-solution "To encrypt a word using the linear cipher with key $a=7$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the word in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}10$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Multiply each index by $a=7$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\cdot 19≡133≡3 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 0≡0 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 11≡77≡25 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 10≡70≡18 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}18$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\text{DAZS}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151988 :question/id #uuid "b6e53039-e0b7-591f-95fb-92fddf49ed46" :question/math-academy-id "e-6813" :question/problem "Suppose we encrypt a $4$-letter English word using the linear cipher with key $a=15$ and get the ciphertext $\\text{ZAJU}$ as a result. Assuming that during the encryption we used the usual English alphabet (shown below), what was the original (plain text) word?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" :question/worked-solution "To decipher a message encrypted using the linear cipher with key $a=15$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Find the inverse to $a=15$ modulo $26$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}26 & = & 15\\cdot 1 & + & 11 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 11\\cdot 1 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 11 & = & 4\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 4 & = & 3\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =26-15 \\\\ 4 & =15-11 \\\\ 3 & =11-4\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =4-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThen, we back-substitute:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =4-3 \\\\  & =4-(11-4\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3-11 \\\\  & =(15-11)\\cdot 3-11 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-11\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-(26-15)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 7-26\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 7+26\\cdot (-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe can write this result in modulo $26$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}15 \\cdot 7 + 26 \\cdot (-4) & ≡1 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 15 \\cdot 7 & ≡1 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $15 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$, which means that the inverse of $a=15$ modulo $26$ is $a^{-1}=7$.\n- Multiply each index by $a^{-1}=7$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\cdot 25≡175≡19 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 0≡0≡0 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 9≡63≡11 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 20≡140≡10 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\text{TALK}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151990 :question/id #uuid "8f1ee01d-29ac-5fb8-a5c9-e0995afa269c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6815" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152015 :question/id #uuid "6a776760-7cfc-5d85-ba45-2810ad8cb78f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7338" :question/problem "Calculate the value of $4^{\\large 7^{100}} \\: \\text{mod} \\: 13$?" :question/worked-solution "The special case of Fermat's little theorem states the following:\n\n> $a^{p-1} \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:p)$ for any prime $p$ and any integer $a$ that is coprime with $p$.\n\nFirst, notice that $13$ is prime and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(4,13)=1\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the special case of Fermat's little theorem:\n\n$$\n4^{13-1} \\equiv 4^{\\color{blue}12} \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:13)\n$$\n\nLet's now find $7^{100}$ modulo ${\\color{blue}12}$. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 7^{1}\\begin{vmatrix}≡7 & (\\bmod 12) \\\\ 7^{2} & ≡1\\end{vmatrix}(\\bmod 12); 7^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}≡7 & (\\bmod 12) \\\\ 7^{4} & ≡1\\end{vmatrix}(\\bmod 12); ⋮\n$$\nThis pattern will continue. So, $7$ to the power of any even integer (like $100$) will give us $1$ modulo $12$, and we can write\n\n$$\n7^{100} = 12k+1\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$.\n\nNow, re-writing the given exponent, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{{7}^{100}} & ≡{4}^{12k+1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}13) \\\\  & ≡{({4}^{12})}^{k}\\cdot {4}^{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}13) \\\\  & ≡{(1)}^{k}\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}13) \\\\  & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}13)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152017 :question/id #uuid "996eaa5c-120c-559c-b05c-54172332a875" :question/math-academy-id "e-7465" :question/problem "Consider the following congruence, which is either true or false:\n\n$5^{24} \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:39)$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\text{gcd}(5,39)=1$\n2. $\\phi(39) = 24$\n3. The congruence is true by Euler's theorem" :question/worked-solution "Euler's theorem states that if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, then\n\n$$\na^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:n)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\phi$ is Euler's totient function.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{gcd}(5,39)=1$.\n- Statement II is true. First, notice that\n$39 = 3 \\cdot 13$,\nwhere $3$ and $13$ are distinct prime numbers. As a result,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ(39) & =ϕ(3)\\cdot ϕ(13) \\\\  & =(3-1)\\cdot (13-1) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Statement III is true. By Euler's theorem, we get the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}a^{ϕ(n)} & ≡1 & (modn) \\\\ 5^{ϕ(39)} & ≡1 & (\\bmod 39) \\\\ 5^{24} & ≡1 & (\\bmod 39)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6f5e336-5ff4-56ca-818f-1d119ed81495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148968} {:answer/id #uuid "af99e30e-a9da-5f77-bc0e-2076652d7fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148969} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6bbb3d-49e7-5223-8254-71d7461addf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148970} {:answer/id #uuid "d9be9dcb-fa90-5f5b-8218-f5b4865a1d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148971} {:answer/id #uuid "e030f946-6bad-5b53-b725-4e5472d82ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148972}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ea6fe5-e9fd-56de-82eb-af23d85324c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f75b05f-5844-5552-8410-66e825fbf4dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132398" :question/problem "Which of the following congruences have *precisely three* solutions modulo $24$?\n\n1. $18x≡12(\\bmod 24)$\n2. $9x≡5(\\bmod 24)$\n3. $15x≡18(\\bmod 24)$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b10b9c7d-8f97-5f00-8e32-ae6e26f9735f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148974} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c5a556-62f5-5ff7-b6e6-78a82c24ccb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148975} {:answer/id #uuid "ed703634-d279-5a95-9600-ee11a12622ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148976} {:answer/id #uuid "3bbf8b2c-cd62-5abf-8b80-3d3562efa95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148977} {:answer/id #uuid "62510874-d8ba-5014-ac93-02c01040e53f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175