field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149582} {:answer-field/id #uuid "56f2fae7-49e0-5d81-ba49-e30f34502d62" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6cd8d075-9d25-5a81-9a9f-2b159dddaebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-214208" :question/problem "Find the solutions of $92x≡24(\\bmod 284)$. Express your answers as nonnegative integers smaller than $284$, and in ascending order.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $\\gcd (92, 284) = 4$.*\n\n$x_{1}≡$\n{{field-1}}\n$, x_{2}≡$\n{{field-2}}\n$, x_{3}≡$\n{{field-3}}\n$, x_{4}≡$\n{{field-4}}\n$(\\bmod 284)$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152598 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83c436d7-43de-5fc3-8ce9-ffee3522b4e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150096} {:answer/id #uuid "878ee604-c103-5053-accd-021c977abe86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150097} {:answer/id #uuid "36a1f7e7-07d8-5e7b-b2d8-10439e7fbca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150098} {:answer/id #uuid "6b2f32a0-d9c2-5fff-92ef-17aaeb98ad1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150099} {:answer/id #uuid "6ddd25dc-12e2-58d9-ad7b-7e3c37c2d227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150100}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2e2f3bae-5cbf-5593-a8d7-5b3a83d8175d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9b11f59e-fd24-5b44-aac3-13bb2025249f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38396" :question/problem "If $\\Re (x) = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\Im (x) = \\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ d\\end{bmatrix}$ are the real and imaginary parts of the vector $x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 - 5\\textrm{i} \\\\ 6 - 3\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of $ab + cd$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8728c7da-720f-5e61-97d6-b8a39df3c314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325123} {:answer/id #uuid "905f342b-d00a-53b3-af8d-38cbcb119916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325199} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8466cb-bf8d-5e2d-8d3b-c1cb24e50608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325326} {:answer/id #uuid "b9dcdd31-8037-5ab6-8919-b83b9fff4507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325566} {:answer/id #uuid "c1007dfa-49fb-5a7e-92a1-3f8618ba8b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325630}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325199} :answer-field/id #uuid "98c8848c-c6e4-5d33-845e-aec52077a72c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e836460c-d649-52c9-8ad9-0a85310c1949" :question/math-academy-id "q-41188" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }$ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }$, where $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\cos }^{2}⁡2\\theta +4{\\sin }^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4({\\cos }^{2}⁡2\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4([\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]-[-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e7d0b49-a86d-5dac-9980-5a31f3551190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333419} {:answer/id #uuid "8094e70b-c370-5011-a1fa-b202845cf12f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334132} {:answer/id #uuid "a772a1dd-6c85-59e3-9ae1-db65d223e432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334456} {:answer/id #uuid "ad4207fe-16d7-587f-9134-5cb8b7f5cc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334518} {:answer/id #uuid "d56b2bc6-e730-5565-a0d6-c383d13ce2a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334860}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334456} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b6e66ab-9753-5c19-896c-fc7be08555c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "be3693e3-b291-55cc-9e6f-d294ae6aac43" :question/math-academy-id "q-6881" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce23f03c-32be-576f-b060-ea74e6f090fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477419} {:answer/id #uuid "95c018bb-a80b-54fc-af45-bef9a800aa6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477420}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477420} :answer-field/id #uuid "807cf1ab-8d42-5a68-ba3b-58e1293a4d47" :answer-field/key "$f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477679} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c73e61da-f236-522d-a83f-180053be0d94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477421} {:answer/id #uuid "73bef137-63b7-5b4d-885b-e636dd0af2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477422}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477421} :answer-field/id #uuid "da627b86-eb71-5917-ad11-3aa68e6d2771" :answer-field/key "$g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477680} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85c9ed27-d7c8-5ae2-aba1-099c3f60cf33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477423} {:answer/id #uuid "acdf2aa1-86d9-5db4-b85c-1752a695bf14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477424}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477423} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bc7e146-01f9-59d3-8a9a-3934e62a5feb" :answer-field/key "$h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e0a2fe09-79e7-541d-8328-5d8abf9635ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-76236" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- “$f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$” is a surjection. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ choose $x={\\log }_{3}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f({\\log }_{3}⁡y)={3}^{{\\log }_{3}⁡y}=y.$\n\n- “$g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$” is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in Q.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only real solutions of $g(x)={x}^{2}=2$ are $x=\\pm\\sqrt{2}$, and neither belongs to $Q$.\n\n- “$h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={x}^{2}$” is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={x}^{2}=-1.$  In fact, ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in R$, with equality at $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90da3ca8-37af-554f-8d91-e57306848905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150942} {:answer/id #uuid "de4056d5-7ff6-55aa-af85-aaa30b96ed06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150943} {:answer/id #uuid "ee13be1c-1128-5cce-998b-e98cd7d59b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150944} {:answer/id #uuid "f52ea6ad-1753-56f5-98f8-95a2393d4afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150945} {:answer/id #uuid "78cdb22c-fa67-56b1-8f35-50e08de970e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150946}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186150946} :answer-field/id #uuid "61d6f4e9-3b02-5c82-a9e2-09f0904ced68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "64562ab0-7f9a-5e97-a09e-6e014afb5d25" :question/math-academy-id "q-76339" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=5{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 5{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =25{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}25{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =125{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}125{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =125{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =125\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{u}^{4}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{125}{4}({1}^{4}-{0}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{125}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d386b2-29ca-55a0-a4f9-2285709e1710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342276} {:answer/id #uuid "0724c421-8073-517d-93f3-1620e786e609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342301} {:answer/id #uuid "39b44baa-ac93-5399-be44-580cc1caf867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342714} {:answer/id #uuid "5e406d1b-7ff9-563d-ad72-bf188c813dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343029} {:answer/id #uuid "e4798884-9668-5922-995d-443c2be3e374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344244}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342301} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ab5eb9-1fc8-5190-8bd2-5881ace73b08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aefe264e-7dab-56be-82be-12f2dc01af9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76345" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +4{\\sin }^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c31779c1-61a9-5ddf-9ce4-388acac151e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393613} {:answer/id #uuid "b35b8c73-b6e1-55f6-a3a8-1f84f618409d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393614} {:answer/id #uuid "d14288db-cd96-5f69-9f29-fa682d61a4a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393615} {:answer/id #uuid "f83c51d2-6855-5059-8910-c179f8d7f6c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393616} {:answer/id #uuid "865a2778-efa4-5860-8c94-4fab14e1e597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393617}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393614} :answer-field/id #uuid "8116cea9-3c1b-5e81-a58c-b2147c4e208d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fda4f6af-c917-5961-9062-323f1cdf3aeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-76355" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152748 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d435e76a-c6c1-5adf-8afa-a55c3f25bd40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150996} {:answer/id #uuid "d482455e-6da3-5ea9-af93-f5d036e7b546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150997} {:answer/id #uuid "e8952c28-26f6-5ffc-8edd-4c928428f9cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150998} {:answer/id #uuid "3678df20-2cee-57ce-bf31-c385b8e6d965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref