/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151215} {:answer/id #uuid "6e022540-1fb2-53fc-8776-6b831b0ae829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151216}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a8c6f791-d8f0-5181-b9aa-8c07a2e13270" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151211}] :question/id #uuid "e180e6e2-1772-5d92-937f-001d2f6c4e3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-78154" :question/problem "Find the sum modulo $30$ of the solutions to the congruence $9x≡6(\\bmod 30)$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3754726d-eb07-5177-bde1-2e130a929394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345826} {:answer/id #uuid "41933836-9e91-5567-ae93-cdca54f3119c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+(y+1)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345910} {:answer/id #uuid "634f6c56-30af-534a-9acf-909105673bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346215} {:answer/id #uuid "85b84dc3-77f6-5317-bc63-cba7718f74d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+(y+1)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346513} {:answer/id #uuid "97060e97-47b0-5626-85ea-a37318a871be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346673}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346673} :answer-field/id #uuid "16df9bb4-f58f-542f-8d1a-7a5f96d7b152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ddcf2b55-d913-5f98-8f2e-d38863acbf2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79375" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 \\\\ y-1 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87ddde79-2216-55f9-a241-4e3672b694c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460233} {:answer/id #uuid "0b7a88de-d9d2-5ec0-a2fb-a186b71ec20e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460234}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460233} :answer-field/id #uuid "e844d229-6b8b-51c9-b888-0ba89abf3b5b" :answer-field/key "$y(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460983} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eeab3a09-b1a6-5339-9628-ec71bce3f490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460235} {:answer/id #uuid "c845b3e9-cf61-58a4-9310-d63f07f4f147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460236} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f15e339-1689-5c7f-8cf5-50895397afc6" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460984} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7904cf0f-78cb-5a70-8792-6d23e6d0e9de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460237} {:answer/id #uuid "d97c94c7-45b3-50e5-a408-e6bc7d86f4ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460238}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460237} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9b85a7e-07f0-5732-a416-1ae1d52c3ca4" :answer-field/key "$y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f6e0c318-7db3-5a86-afe7-c1d5265937a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-79730" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b1c3ace-b4ee-5b77-b91a-166f5f76d833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151542} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a20bd5-2716-5f04-a81e-063a924e5384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151543} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c187e6-f4c3-5b2f-bffc-5bf1f43dff98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151544} {:answer/id #uuid "e999ca3f-8975-5313-9c48-20964d87bf71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151545} {:answer/id #uuid "00aa87ba-3230-5c8d-b385-f21422c5e166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151546}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2a783bb8-9153-5afe-a752-23097faadc15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79e7e9ce-308f-5b22-afba-e54f547724ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-81931" :question/problem "Find $\\Im (k)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle v = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle kv^(―) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\textrm{i} + 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152843 :question/answer-fie