34f152d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-86318" :question/problem "Given that $6a≡6b(\\bmod 12)$, which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n1. $a≡b(\\bmod 4)$\n2. $a≡b(\\bmod 2)$\n3. $3a≡3b(\\bmod 6)$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bab2ac61-19dc-5ace-9cf9-6fa2d34b33dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151782} {:answer/id #uuid "be2bbec4-a1b0-5e70-a4c2-8c35006b4970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151783} {:answer/id #uuid "cbded075-23ec-50bd-ad8a-f8d0aba56903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151784} {:answer/id #uuid "debb8264-165e-59c1-b252-a2a2c538d408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151785} {:answer/id #uuid "6595dce4-6f3d-5886-b84d-83bc5ca51fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151786}] :answer-field/id #uuid "71438bd3-caef-5a25-a107-f52b6c328d7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e27ffe5a-804d-5c58-adb6-3b3c042c4ffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-98404" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\gcd (888, 205) = 7$\n2. $\\gcd (888, 205) = 1$\n3. $888$ and $205$ are coprime"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "feae585c-9952-5333-86aa-cb161eb18f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151788} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3bac92-bcac-5e6c-b795-59b6ee2e90d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151789} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc7366e-2567-5552-87d8-96a03cb4fb31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151790} {:answer/id #uuid "3e47a2ac-392e-5895-a4f1-9813ededf16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151791} {:answer/id #uuid "74557ce3-17f2-584b-bc33-ca5cf0fc9059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151792}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ad38f955-52f0-514d-8b58-9e6b8881a332" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151787}] :question/id #uuid "85245392-be53-5f7f-893a-92db6f88521c" :question/math-academy-id "q-98471" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\gcd (6021, 4023) = 1$\n2. $\\gcd (6021, 4023) = 27$\n3. $6021$ and $4023$ are not coprime"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153385 :tutorial/content "The **linear cipher** is similar to the Caesar cipher. However, instead of adding the key, we multiply by the key. If the alphabet consists of $n$ letters, and the key is $a$, then the plain text index $i_\\textrm{plain}$ is translated into the ciphertext index $i_\\textrm{cipher}$ as follows:\n\n$$\na \\cdot i_\\textrm{plain} \\equiv i_\\textrm{cipher} \\: (\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nFor example, let's encrypt the message $\\textrm{HEY}$ using the linear cipher with key $a=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters. First, we recall the indices of letters in the English alphabet:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the word in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}24$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Multiply each index by $a=3$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 7≡21 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 4≡12 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 24≡72≡20 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indexes:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the encrypted message is $\\text{VMU}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "656ce173-c650-58bf-bde8-4cf40d4feaec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4206 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153386 :tutorial/content "A **cipher** is a procedure for transforming or **encrypting** a message to conceal its meaning. The original message is said to be written in **plain text**, while the encrypted message is said to be written in **ciphertext**.\n\nCiphers often work by\n\n1. translating each letter into a number called an **index**,\n2. performing an operation on the index, and then\n3. translating the number back into a letter.\n\nThe letters in the English alphabet are indexed as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nOne of the simplest ciphers, the **Caesar cipher**, involves replacing each letter in a message with a different one $k$ places down the alphabet. If the alphabet consists of $n$ letters, then the plain text index $i_\\textrm{plain}$ is translated into the ciphertext index $i_\\textrm{cipher}$ as follows:\n\n$$\ni_\\textrm{plain} + k \\equiv i_\\textrm{cipher} \\: (\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nFor example, to encrypt the message $\\textrm{HEY}$ using the Caesar cipher with the **key** $k=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the word in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}24$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Add $k=3$ to each index modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 + 3≡10 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 4 + 3≡7 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 24 + 3≡27≡1 + 26≡1 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the encrypted message is $\\text{KHB}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ddd6b61-3c36-5f76-aa73-e46f3c0780ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4207 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153387 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we encrypted a message using the Caesar cipher with key $k=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters. The resulting encrypted message was $\\textrm{KHB}$.\n\nHowever, suppose we send this message to a friend. How can they **decipher** or **decrypt** this message to reveal the original message?\n\nDecrypting is the opposite of encrypting. Remember that to encrypt a message, we add the key to the indices of the letters. So, to *decrypt* a message, all we have to do is *subtract* the key:\n\n$$\ni_\\textrm{cipher} - k \\equiv i_\\textrm{plain} \\: (\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nLet's recall the indices of the English alphabet and then decipher the message.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo decipher a message encrypted using the Caesar cipher with key $k=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Subtract $k=3$ from each index modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}10 - 3≡7 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 7 - 3≡4 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 1 - 3≡ - 2≡ - 2 + 26≡24 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}24$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nWe successfully recovered the original message, which was $\\text{HEY}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "be3ab00b-27dd-5750-b723-e4d5ac527431" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4208 :tutorial/title "Decrypting a Message Using the Caesar Cipher"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153388 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we encrypted a message using the linear cipher with key $a=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters. The resulting encrypted message was $\\textrm{VMU}$.\n\nTo decrypt this message, all we have to do is multiply by the inverse of the key:\n\n$$\na^{-1} \\cdot i_\\textrm{cipher} \\equiv i_\\textrm{plain} \\: (\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nLet's recall the indices of the English alphabet and then decipher the message.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo decipher a message encrypted using the linear cipher with key $a=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Find the inverse of $a=3$ modulo $26$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}26 & = & 3\\cdot 8 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 3 & = & 2\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =26-3\\cdot 8 \\\\ 1 & =3-2\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThen, we back-substitute:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =3-2 \\\\  & =3-(26-3\\cdot 8) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 9-26 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 9+26\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe can write this result in modulo $26$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 9 + 26 \\cdot (-1) & ≡1 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 9 & ≡1 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $3 \\cdot 9 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$, which means that the inverse of $a=3$ modulo $26$ is $a^{-1}=9$.\n- Multiply each index by $a^{-1}=9$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\cdot 21≡189≡7 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 9 \\cdot 12≡108≡4 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 9 \\cdot 20≡180≡24 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}24$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nWe successfully recovered the original message, which was $\\text{HEY}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "23c831bf-8efa-553b-9d5b-3542af84877e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4209 :tutorial/title "Decrypting a Message Using the Linear Cipher"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153390 :tutorial/content "How do we determine whether a function $f$ is an injection without using a graph or mapping diagram? \n\nTo do this, we take $x_1$ and $x_2$ (arbitrary but fixed) in the domain of $f,$ and we construct the equation $f(x_1)=f(x_2).$ Using algebraic manipulation, we aim to get some relation between the variables $x_1$ and $x_2.$ If we get $x_1=x_2,$ this implies that $f$ is an injection. \n\nLet's use this idea to prove that $f:\\mathbb{R}\\rightarrow (0,\\infty)$ defined by $f(x)=2^x$ is an injection.\n\nFirst, we construct the equation $f(x_1) = f(x_2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using algebraic manipulation, we reduce this relationship to $x_1=x_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{1}}) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{2}}) \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for any $x_1, x_2 \\in\\mathbb{R},$ if $f(x_1)=f(x_2)$ we have $x_1=x_2.$ In other words, $f$ is an injection." :tutorial/id #uuid "6592b1a8-cfe6-5f38-be26-79e350169bf6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4229 :tutorial/title "Identifying Injections Defined on Arbitrary Sets"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153462 :tutorial/content "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\nax \\equiv b \\qquad (\\text{mod}\\:n)\n$$\n\nIn cases where $\\textrm{gcd}(a,n) > 1$ and $\\textrm{gcd}(a,n) \\mid b$, it's possible to reduce the congruence to a simpler congruence with a unique solution. However, the solution to the simpler congruence is only *part* of the solution to the original congruence. An extra step is needed to find the complete solution.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find all the solutions to the following congruence:\n\n$$\n2x \\equiv 6 \\qquad (\\text{mod}\\:10)\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=2$ and $n=10$, so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(2,10)=2\n$$\n\nSince $b=6$ is divisible by $2$, our congruence has $2$ solutions modulo $10$.\n\nNotice that we can simplify the congruence using the division property of congruences:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle 2 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  6 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 10 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 2 \\cdot  x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  2 \\cdot  3 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 2 \\cdot  5 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\cancel{2} \\cdot  x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  \\cancel{2} \\cdot  3 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} \\cancel{2} \\cdot  5 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  {\\color{blue}3} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 5 )\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThus, our simplified congruence has the unique solution, $x \\equiv {\\color{blue}3} \\: (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{red}5})$.\n\nBut remember, we expect $2$ solutions modulo $10$. To find all the solutions, we repeatedly add the reduced modulus (${\\color{red}5}$) to our reduced solution (${\\color{blue}3}$) modulo $10$ until we reach our reduced solution (${\\color{blue}3}$) again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & ≡3 & (\\bmod 10) \\\\ x_{2} & ≡3 + 5≡8 & (\\bmod 10) \\\\ x_{3} & ≡8 + 5≡13≡10 + 3≡3 & (\\bmod 10)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x\\equiv {\\color{blue}3} \\: (\\text{mod}\\:10)$ and $x\\equiv {\\color{purple}{8}} \\: (\\text{mod}\\:10)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "654a9c4c-6266-562f-8e65-2bad594817c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9034 :tutorial/title "Solving Linear Congruences Using the Division Property"} {:answer/id #uuid "f27ada8b-a71f-51fe-a90a-ce99dbd8c94d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(37 \\cdot 71)\\bmod 73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153629} {:answer/id #uuid "30c08b1e-7c6b-551a-98d7-039e1e4f24d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(55 \\cdot 71)\\bmod 73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153630} {:answer/id #uuid "471ddeca-b2db-57c4-b263-24f848186d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(55 \\cdot 37)\\bmod 73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153631} {:answer/id #uuid "481d0556-7f5c-5ce1-95df-777498280e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(37 \\cdot 16)\\bmod 73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153632} {:answer/id #uuid "758762ed-0847-5c74-8bb7-765712e8f3e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(55 \\cdot 16)\\bmod 73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153633} {:answer/id #uuid "285fd9a7-8a71-53e2-b200-329f19a9a948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\gcd (5, 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154591} {:answer/id #uuid "6777d810-fab8-5ec4-b7c6-bb6fd25237a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{lcm} (5, 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154592} {:answer/id #uuid "a8eb9140-5069-521a-b617-e2778480f8ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(12 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9)\\bmod 37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154894} {:answer/id #uuid "d44fc1d9-1194-5f58-9988-7bbe036b71d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(7 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 15)\\bmod 37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154895} {:answer/id #uuid "23d713af-93c2-5162-b95d-5fdf2d098c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(33 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9)\\bmod 37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154896} {:answer/id #uuid "2d310bd4-c55b-5849-9faa-87805d26593f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(12 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 3)\\bmod 37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154897} {:answer/id #uuid "7695bb32-8cfc-5200-82b3-60c63da3333f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(33 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 3)\\bmod 37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154898} {:answer/id #uuid "d0782507-ab77-5bc3-b471-aea370492241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}\\min ^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156940} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd0379a-fc7b-54b3-878e-c1c039cf7e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\min ^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156941} {:answer/id #uuid "0f0ee3ef-8f1a-5074-a226-1b41367d1e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}\\min ^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156942} {:answer/id #uuid "428ca9f2-cdef-5739-9282-70ea7effdd4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\min ^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156943} {:answer/id #uuid "48812556-f3c3-5283-8341-9f2b68f4033a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{5}\\min ^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157029 :question/id #uuid "f0bb7d00-780d-5393-a143-b258435a78a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-15553" :question/problem "Suppose we encrypt the $3$-letter English word $\\text{BIT}$ to ciphertext using the RSA cipher with the public key $(e,n)=(19,65)$. Assuming that we use the usual English alphabet (shown below) during the encryption and each letter is encrypted separately, what ciphertext results from the encryption?\n\n*Hint: Use the `mod` function on your calculator or a software package to compute the required powers modulo $n$.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" :question/worked-solution "To encrypt a word using the RSA cipher with the public key\n\n$$\n(e,n)=(19,65)\n$$\n\nover the English alphabet, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the word in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Raise each index to the power of $e=19$ modulo $n=65$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1^{19}≡1 & (\\bmod 65) \\\\ 8^{19}≡57 & (\\bmod 65) \\\\ 19^{19}≡59 & (\\bmod 65)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ciphertext is\n$01 \\:\\: 57 \\:\\: 59$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157030 :question/id #uuid "9092817d-e00d-56fa-a43f-eddb6c1f90b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-15554" :question/problem "Suppose we encrypt a $3$-letter English word using the RSA cipher with the private key $(d,n)=(13,55)$ and get the ciphertext $52 \\:\\: 49 \\:\\: 53$ as a result. Assuming that during the encryption, we used the usual English alphabet (shown below) and each letter was encrypted separately, what was the original (plain text) word?\n\n*Hint: Use the `mod` function on your calculator or a software package to compute the required powers modulo $n$.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" :question/worked-solution "To decrypt a word using the RSA cipher with the private key\n\n$$\n(d,n)=(13,55)\n$$\n\nover the English alphabet, we do the following:\n\n- Raise each index to the power of $d=13$ modulo $n=55$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}52^{13}≡17 & (\\bmod 55) \\\\ 49^{13}≡4 & (\\bmod 55) \\\\ 53^{13}≡3 & (\\bmod 55)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indexes:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the plaintext was\n$R \\:\\: E \\:\\: D$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157031 :question/id #uuid "78074adf-2db8-5ec9-a618-58efb4b8e66d" :question/math-academy-id "e-15555" :question/problem "Suppose we encrypt a $3$-letter English word using the RSA cipher with the public key $(e,n)=(7,33)$ and get the ciphertext $15 \\:\\: 16 \\:\\: 13$ as a result. Assuming that during the encryption, we used the usual English alphabet (shown below) and each letter was encrypted separately, what was the original (plain text) word?\n\n*Hint: Use the `mod` function on your calculator or a software package to compute the required powers modulo $n$.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" :question/worked-solution "To decrypt a message using the RSA cipher, we need to know its corresponding private key.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nn=33 = 3 \\times 11\n$$\n\nLet's denote $p=3$ and $q=11$. Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ(n) &= (p - 1)(q - 1) \\\\\n&= (3 - 1)(11 - 1) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the RSA cipher with the private key $(d,n)$, the first component $d$ is the multiplicative inverse of $e=7$ (the first component of the public key) modulo\n\n$$\n\\phi(n)=20{:}\n$$\n\n$$\nd = e^{-1} \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 20)\n$$\n\nSo, let's find the inverse of $e=7$ modulo $\\phi(n)=20$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}20 & = & 7\\cdot 2 & + & 6 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 7 & = & 6\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 &= 20 - 7 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n1 &= 7 - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 7 - 6 \\\\\n&= 7 - (20 - 7 \\cdot 2) \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 3 - 20 \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 3 + 20 \\cdot (-1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $20$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\cdot 3 + 20 \\cdot (-1) & ≡1 & (\\bmod 20) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 3 & ≡1 & (\\bmod 20)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $7 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:20)$, which means that the inverse of $e=7$ modulo $20$ is $e^{-1}=3$.\n\nThus, our private key is\n\n$$\n(d,n) = (3,33)\n$$\n\nNow, to decrypt a word using the RSA cipher with the private key\n\n$$\n(d,n)=(3,33)\n$$\n\nover the English alphabet, we do the following:\n\n- Raise each index to the power of $d=3$ modulo $n=33$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}15^{3}≡09 & (\\bmod 33) \\\\ 16^{3}≡04 & (\\bmod 33) \\\\ 13^{3}≡19 & (\\bmod 33)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indexes:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}09$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}04$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the plaintext was\n$J \\:\\: E \\:\\: T$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157042 :question/id #uuid "b54dd07c-5e48-5d06-a454-c7dd907804d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-16634" :question/problem "Suppose $\\mathbf a=[-2,\\, 6,\\, -7]^T$ and $\\mathbf b=[8,\\, -9,\\, -9]^T.$ Find the Chebyshev distance $d_\\infty$ between $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\nd_\\infty(\\mathbf a,\\mathbf b) =\\|\\mathbf a-\\mathbf b\\|_\\infty\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $\\mathbf v,$\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|_\\infty = \\operatorname{max}(|v_1|,|v_2|,\\ldots, |v_N|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\max (|-2-8|,|6-(-9)|,|-7-(-9)|) \\\\  & =\\max (10,15,2) \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157065 :question/id #uuid "89275b98-c720-586a-8b31-6a187aeeb5c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-6908" :question/problem "Let $a$ and $b$ be integers. Prove that if $a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)$, then $4a \\not\\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12)$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional statement $P \\Rightarrow Q$ and its contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nare logically equivalent. Moreover, it's sometimes easier to prove the contrapositive.\n\nIn this case, we need to show that\n$\\underbrace{a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)}_{P} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{4a \\not\\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12)}_{Q}$.\nThe contrapositive of the statement is\n$\\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(4a \\not\\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12) \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, Q} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3) \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, P}$,\nwhich is equivalent to\n$4a \\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)$.\nSo, we proceed as follows:\n\n> The contrapositive of the given statement is\n> $4a \\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)$.\n> We will prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the definition of modular congruence. Recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n \\mid (a-b) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad a-b = nk\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$. So, we can reason as follows:\n\n> By the definition of modular congruence,\n> $4a≡4b(\\bmod 12) \\mid ⇔12∣(4a - 4b); ⇔12∣4(a - b)$\n\nNext, we use the definition of divisibility:\n\n> By the definition of divisibility in $\\mathbb{Z}$, $12 \\mid 4(a-b)$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that\n> $4(a-b) = 12k$.\n\nNow, we can cancel out the factor of $4{:}$\n\n> Dividing this equation by $4$, we obtain\n> $a-b = 3k$,\n> which in turn implies\n> $3 \\mid (a-b)$.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of modular congruence again:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, we have\n> $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)$.\n> By proving the contrapositive, we proved the initial statement, as required."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157096 :question/id #uuid "97c9cecd-d87a-5db4-ba6c-18fc8508e85e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7280" :question/problem "Find the smallest natural number solution to the system of linear congruences below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}x \\equiv  1 & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 2 ) \\\\ x \\equiv  5 & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 7 )\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $2$ and $7$ are coprime, then according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 7 = 14\n$$\n\nFrom the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:2) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 1 + 2t, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & ≡5 & (\\bmod 7) \\\\ 1 + 2t & ≡5 & (\\bmod 7) \\\\ 2t & ≡4 & (\\bmod 7)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $7$ is a relatively small modulus, by trial and error, we have that $4 \\cdot 2 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:7)$. So, we multiply both sides by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2t & ≡4 & (\\bmod 7) \\\\ 4 \\cdot 2t & ≡16 & (\\bmod 7) \\\\ t & ≡2 & (\\bmod 7)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt = 2 + 7s\n$$\n\nwhere $s \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Substituting this into the expression for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 1 + 2t \\\\\n&= 1 + 2(2 + 7s) \\\\\n&= 5 + 14s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x \\equiv 5 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:14)$ is the unique solution modulo $14$, and $x=5$ is the smallest natural number solution to the system."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157098 :question/id #uuid "dd18e86e-81ff-54cd-970c-73ec8144b66e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7282" :question/problem "Find the smallest natural number solution to the system of linear congruences below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}x \\equiv  1 & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 2 ) \\\\ x \\equiv  2 & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 3 ) \\\\ x \\equiv  3 & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 5 )\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $2$, $3$, and $5$ are coprime, then according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 = 30\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system in two steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the common solution for the first and second congruences of the system.\n\nFrom the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:2) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 1 + 2t, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & ≡2 & (\\bmod 3) \\\\ 1 + 2t & ≡2 & (\\bmod 3) \\\\ 2t & ≡1 & (\\bmod 3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $3$ is a relatively small modulus, we can solve it by trial and error. We have that $2 \\cdot 2 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:3)$. So, we multiply both sides by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2t & ≡1 & (\\bmod 3) \\\\ 2 \\cdot 2t & ≡2 & (\\bmod 3) \\\\ t & ≡2 & (\\bmod 3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt = 2 + 3s\n$$\n\nwhere $s \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Substituting this into the expression for $x$, we get the common solution for the first and second congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 1 + 2t \\\\\n&= 1 + 2(2 + 3s) \\\\\n&= 5 + 6s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Find the common solution of all three congruences.\n\nNow, substituting the common solution of the first two congruences into the third congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & ≡3 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ 5 + 6s & ≡3 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ 6s & ≡ - 2 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ s & ≡3 & (\\bmod 5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\ns = 3 + 5u\n$$\n\nwhere $u \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Substituting this into the expression for the common solution of the first two congruences, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 5 + 6s \\\\\n&= 5 + 6(3 + 5u) \\\\\n&= 23 + 30u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x \\equiv 23 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:30)$ is the unique solution modulo $30$, and $x=23$ is the smallest natural number solution to the system."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157100 :question/id #uuid "5caa3fbc-f232-5e40-b6d2-c34fa552d6b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-7367" :question/problem "Find the total area bounded by the polar curve $r=\\sin(7\\theta),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d9aa4ff23010048536fbcbadc9cff5dc2fc094895c00814dd9efccdc9750682a.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta$ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sin(7\\theta) = 0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $7\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n7\\theta = 0, \\pi \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{7}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{7}.$\n\n![](images/71/712d56708d2346f502a229c20596e7aa24ce4be13564e9461784c9114c55065a.png)\n\nSince there are $7$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =7\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14\\theta ))\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\frac{7}{4}(\\theta -\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(14\\theta ))|}_{0}^{\\pi /7} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{7}-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi ))-(0-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{7}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157102 :question/id #uuid "189d117b-b510-59dd-8340-a84bd1c08472" :question/math-academy-id "e-7409" :question/problem "![](images/4e/4e47cfcf3da66707e6bc898d099868bfbd6d5163dcd38d47e4c79b2180e89ec6.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+1)^2+y^2=1$ and the line $x=-1,$ as shown above. Express the following double integral in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D xy \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(-1,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^2+y^2=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = -2\\cos\\theta.$\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\pi,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/76/769c13bea2dfc91aa3a507df1b7a763ae0da7591e0d6ba5face12268efb88240.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: -\\sec\\theta \\leq r \\leq -2\\cos\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq {\\pi} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157103 :question/id #uuid "859e1d71-21cb-5e30-9d60-a4b21358fe1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-7410" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa730804a15decac8450255e714ca1e0697f4f2326eb615d4d035a2870a49dce.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{2y}{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ centered at $\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\!.$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $$\n\\displaystyle x^2+\\left( y-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)^2=\\dfrac14,\n$$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = \\sin\\theta.$\n\n- For $y=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/12/1284a8b4b3f1634b4f7586d40ab8adf9cf93c69484b7ed069f138c8055e88892.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}\\csc\\theta \\leq r \\leq \\sin\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157104 :question/id #uuid "ac36f233-1f32-5cba-a35e-aa8e48207a18" :question/math-academy-id "e-7411" :question/problem "![](images/94/940f547f886f1b680cbb37ef950438e6a4f33bd3c84df28a5e00480631c97711.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between the quarter-circles of radii $3$ and $4,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq r \\leq 4, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{r}{{r}^{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157175 :question/id #uuid "01791465-d1dc-5f4a-ac9a-72b822ab08b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7887" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x =2\\tan{t}, \\, y =3\\sec{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={\\tan }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={\\tan }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\tan }^{2}⁡t+1 & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157206 :question/id #uuid "75c34118-ac32-5a29-a6c2-6750918c4f2b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9234" :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}\\left(\\dfrac{20}{9}\\right)\\!$, what is $\\textrm{SD}[X]$?" :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Po}\\left(\\lambda\\right)\n$$\n\nis a Poisson random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SD}[X] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[X]} = \\sqrt{\\lambda}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Po}\\left(\\dfrac{20}{9}\\right)\\!\n$$\n\nwe have the following standard deviation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSD[X] &= \\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var} [X]} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{20}{9}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{20}}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157210 :question/id #uuid "85fe7118-b8ef-5b80-85ea-3f838a668c1d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9241" :question/problem "A fair $8$-sided die is rolled until it shows a score of $2$. What is the variance in the number of rolls that are required?" :question/worked-solution "We interpret a \"success\" as the event that a $2$ is shown and a \"failure\" when some other number is displayed. So, the success probability is\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ represent the number of rolls we must do until a 2 is obtained. So, we can model $X$ using a geometric distribution:\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\right)\n$$\n\nRecall that, if\n\n$$\nX \\sim\\textrm{Geom}\\left(p\\right)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{1-p}{p^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\right)\n$$\n\nwe have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Var} [X] &= (1 - \\frac{1}{8})/(\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right))/(\\left(\\frac{1}{64}\\right)) \\\\\n&= 56\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157214 :question/id #uuid "01cea63b-0792-50dd-bd23-f001118300d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9295" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $11^{49} \\: \\text{mod}\\: 73$?\n\n1. $(16 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 11) \\: \\text{mod} \\:73$\n2. $(4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 48) \\: \\text{mod} \\: 73$\n3. $(16 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 48) \\: \\text{mod} \\: 73$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following table containing some powers of $11$ modulo $73$.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$a$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$32$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$11^a \\: \\text{mod} \\: 73$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$48$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$41$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $49$ as a sum of powers of $2{:}$\n\n$$\n49 =2^5 + 2^4 + 2^0 = 32+16+1\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n11^{49} = 11^{32 + 16 + 1} = 11^{32} \\cdot 11^{16} \\cdot 11^{1}\n$$\n\nNow, we can take the corresponding values from the given table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$a$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$32$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$11^a \\: \\text{mod} \\: 73$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{11}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$48$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$41$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{16}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n11^{49}\\begin{vmatrix}≡11^{32} \\cdot 11^{16} \\cdot 11^{1} & (\\bmod 73) \\\\ ≡16 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 11 & (\\bmod 73)\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157216 :question/id #uuid "abdbe14b-6dc1-5ca3-8207-11ffa48ebc90" :question/math-academy-id "e-9301" :question/problem "Compute $9^{22} \\:\\text{mod}\\: 37$." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $22$ as a sum of powers of $2{:}$\n\n$$\n22 = 2^4+2^2+2^1 = 16 + 4 + 2\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n9^{22} = 9^{16 + 4 + 2} =9^{16} \\cdot 9^{4} \\cdot 9^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the exponents modulo $37$ using consecutive squaring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9^{2} & ≡81≡7 & (\\bmod 37) \\\\ 9^{4} & ≡7^{2}≡49≡12 & (\\bmod 37) \\\\ 9^{8} & ≡12^{2}≡144≡33 & (\\bmod 37) \\\\ 9^{16} & ≡33^{2}≡1089≡16 & (\\bmod 37) \\\\ 9^{32} & ≡16^{2}≡256≡34 & (\\bmod 37)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, by multiplying the obtained exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{22} & ≡{9}^{16}\\cdot {9}^{4}\\cdot {9}^{2} & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡16\\cdot 12\\cdot 7 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡1\\hspace{0.167em}344 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡36\\cdot 37+12 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡12 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157555 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9f49ac0-0901-5841-98cf-2