ef 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155258} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4d0b791-662a-50d7-9a51-777bd93033b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548844}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548844} :answer-field/id #uuid "32f00e3e-0ee2-5b85-a1c5-2b6e6779f01e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4a7e522c-a79d-5bda-94b9-530843463aa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-276944" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $2\\cdot {8}^{2x}<\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {8}^{2x} & <\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {8}^{2x} & <\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $8$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{8}⁡({8}^{2x}) & <{\\log }_{8}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\ 2x & <{\\log }_{8}⁡({6}^{-1}) \\\\ 2x & <-{\\log }_{8}⁡(6) \\\\ x & <-\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{8}⁡(6).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157761 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a13e1663-bebc-5560-82e4-145f1e1d160b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155485} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a5df08-f356-517f-ac99-36b527550fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155486} {:answer/id #uuid "bb012bca-f064-5363-b15b-296a929ed8c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155487} {:answer/id #uuid "733980e1-1277-5b37-8ee1-d88944801e50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155488}] :answer-field/id #uuid "68eb03bf-92fc-50ac-a8f0-d48d107cbf19" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155484}] :question/id #uuid "ff47f6cb-e2bc-560b-ac8d-a4fd2b7bc55b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337920" :question/problem "Let $a ∈ Z$. Prove that if $3∣a$ and $8∣a$, then $24∣a$.\n\nSince $3$ and $8$ are coprime, we have $\\gcd (3, 8) =$\n{{field-1}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fac2aac-15fd-5b1a-8c19-cdbaaa605b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155674} {:answer/id #uuid "af2543c6-e121-548c-9ce8-bd9e83f27959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155675} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1bd87c-c87b-5317-bf18-04917ad67fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155676} {:answer/id #uuid "1fd64df2-e5b8-5aaa-a2df-8978d2413927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155677} {:answer/id #uuid "540f5941-edc3-564d-ab1c-16a630b65ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186155677} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f08daa-f6b4-563b-b34a-95db72823c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "56bfdc28-d81a-5ee4-a9f9-1fab676af72b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80944" :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{3} & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=\\det ({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{3} & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{3})=2{y}^{3}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{3})=6x{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{3} & 6x{y}^{2} \\\\ 6x{y}^{2} & 6{x}^{2}y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=2\\cdot 6-6\\cdot 6=-24.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45626ab5-23ff-5b54-ab30-6536c8857200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544239} {:answer/id #uuid "5227f4d9-586f-5a87-96f1-2d29829a5051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544266} {:answer/id #uuid "53f41015-0e83-5163-bbaa-f1bb9ea38cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544272} {:answer/id #uuid "6d0b241e-150f-5225-9356-c59f965a081e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544327} {:answer/id #uuid "e116c89d-0721-51ca-8376-6b9644097c9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544574}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544266} :answer-field/id #uuid "277f62b5-5b05-5cc1-9927-317efd86b1d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "34c5bc2e-d431-58fd-be86-bcbb846410a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-83365" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd3af53e4d4f02c5978e8b4bc8508fb0455a0a09e7222377df68a2297bc30dae.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=4\\cdot A=8{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157829 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a603b1-e7b9-5111-9287-7aa72828eb6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544089} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca96453-2812-54b1-ac9f-2bc70296b399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544147} {:answer/id #uuid "527f5534-b9ff-58e8-814b-8cf2eb2ab2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544267} {:answer/id #uuid "5f450753-49cb-5b4a-a620-d8dc65d78d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544293} {:answer/id #uuid "a418680f-7ad8-563a-ace3-88e406955935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544442}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544147} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba35f265-6454-54f3-bbaa-3ac2948921cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "527ee65c-de13-51b9-8ea3-2fa79ecc1df6" :question/math-academy-id "q-83369" :question/problem "![](images/47/47eb2f1073f62129ba2fbf7ae154b9733ff2d5bf09af30053f7b708d7a8d21b0.png)\n\nFind the total area bounded by the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n2\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\n![](images/22/220bd5e2559f7e3ef483b1315d1dd98771cf81d8d181a161b87d8084773259e6.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =4\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={(\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ebc643b-565a-59ad-a7f3-6eb56c2a2256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544117} {:answer/id #uuid "1279ff3f-5245-521f-ba84-156f57eb4ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{5\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544125} {:answer/id #uuid "604b7cdb-42b3-51c3-b697-9adc283c8c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{3\\pi /10}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544296} {:answer/id #uuid "c0242d69-b07a-5c78-ba50-a2001826e357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544498} {:answer/id #uuid "f1473918-1ba6-5e3e-bb10-5a77e78b1483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544610}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544610} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eb918fe-8d8a-558f-9906-4308e3a6fe4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b62e1087-3b4a-589d-abc5-58ac8a8c24f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-83521" :question/problem "![](images/09/09bc06001905956e32a4ff8cfff25a875a6933e19c365b2f9b159d8711a05048.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/09/09bc06001905956e32a4ff8cfff25a875a6933e19c365b2f9b159d8711a05048.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=3\\cdot A=\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ff8ea5a-73f9-53b1-aa2b-d89a1b062fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{11\\pi /6}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544160} {:answer/id #uuid "567b1d6d-20c9-5e7e-ad5c-e9bbd06e3774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544278} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a93243-035f-54e5-a2ed-ba2740b32501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{11\\pi /6}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544481} {:answer/id #uuid "df677947-f1f2-54d0-b9ed-85515727e9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544570} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bc3e69-6223-5ab4-87cd-e8f77504d1c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{4\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544481} :answer-field/id #uuid "676f7b35-5434-55eb-ad55-37dab460907b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c7ae6625-2462-5c24-ab37-c40b68057a97" :question/math-academy-id "q-83527" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3aa82e021a1871bd17abfd72570452bc6aefe358a8fddd3cb8e92bb6ff91c5a9.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/3a/3aa82e021a1871bd17abfd72570452bc6aefe358a8fddd3cb8e92bb6ff91c5a9.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $4$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]}_{1\\text{st}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}]}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}]}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}]}_{4\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{11\\pi /6}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63ff2307-bdea-53b3-88e5-97b96d5ad36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489749} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4cd776-9de6-538f-9d94-7a3efcb04fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489772} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4aa5b4-4bf8-52ba-bb0c-de13688b61cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489774} {:answer/id #uuid "c761b339-ab83-5bb8-80db-4cc0d6bebede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489946} {:answer/id #uuid "cce7b078-805e-5d09-a2f3-9ad87e8072c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489969} :answer-field/id #uuid "d25f125e-94ad-5225-b5e5-f24049a7a3e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5d182d5c-4098-5732-b594-84e4e6e5bea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-83789" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b694a0907a5cb1c33d44c921b01b8e351059ce3e695485d79b72051f7ed764a.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and the line $y=1,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/68/68f70968b0dd256159dbfdcbfde4f2f63578237db1770e019c8fb19a5a638eaa.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c6185ac-3ff7-5ad9-8a7a-c63bebc17ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541579} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f7a9d3-6a06-59d9-ace8-7b473aee0897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x>6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541805} {:answer/id #uuid "c26870c9-271c-5ec8-ba0d-55e750e2893c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0<x<6+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541831} {:answer/id #uuid "ceace2e3-3334-557a-a6f8-644ee4f2d6b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6<x<6+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541859} {:answer/id #uuid "d840e75c-9bea-5129-8361-3aaab6aed04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0<x<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541886}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541859} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e6b5db-0c42-53f8-93d9-a05a77c02160" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e045f62d-3ea7-50f9-adfb-812aba109913" :question/math-academy-id "q-87744" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $10{\\log }_{2}⁡(x-6)-3<2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{\\log }_{2}⁡(x-6)-3 & <2 \\\\ 10{\\log }_{2}⁡(x-6) & <5 \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡(x-6) & <\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{\\log }_{2}⁡(x-6)} & <{2}^{1/2} \\\\ x-6 & <\\sqrt{2} \\\\ x & <6+\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx-6>0\\,⇒\\,x>6\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<6+\\sqrt{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,6+\\sqrt{2}).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>6,$ which can be expressed as $(6,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,6+\\sqrt{2})\\cap (6,\\infty )=(6,6+\\sqrt{2})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (6,6+\\sqrt{2}),$ which can also be written as $6<x<6+\\sqrt{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d574a5c6-6e85-5840-a6c9-47afbd64f5f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460359} {:answer/id #uuid "fb406f8b-7d21-583e-8970-b28bc7c03256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460360}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460359} :answer-field/id #uuid "d633ac8f-3e41-5a25-a8d2-0ce35f4524f7" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461046} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eff13e4a-c513-5d39-b7d9-0fce67dd8e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460361} {:answer/id #uuid "6523295b-2f68-5504-809c-9dba8cade993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460362}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460362} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3d6943f-992e-54df-ae46-78c7fa8cb08b" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461047} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daab89ef-f2be-5132-82ab-a30bb6edea6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460363} {:answer/id #uuid "956bd643-178a-547c-9724-cd203246ad92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460364}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460363} :answer-field/id #uuid "65ea0ed9-3e06-545b-9915-b7abb6939075" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-2}^{4}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "96edbb01-df4a-527f-b87f-1a45a2a9187b" :question/math-academy-id "q-88686" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={\\sin }^{2}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{4}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is not of the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157992 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a42fbefb-e7a0-5c8c-811e-7172dd932bfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156874} {:answer/id #uuid "f619aa98-fa13-535b-ade0-8ace52426b46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156875} {:answer/id #uuid "f69f7dae-f477-54fc-9219-3cb40d573182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156876} {:answer/id #uuid "172cda24-675f-5351-9c0c-145d82be49fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156877} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4f898a-59fc-5e98-a0cd-7807a4ac8955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156878}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4eb95309-e90e-5054-a05f-5086b9b0a7f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156873}] :question/id #uuid "5ccc2140-04de-522e-b708-847944ab8291" :question/math-academy-id "q-90308" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $E[X] = \\frac{3}{2}$, compute $\\operatorname{Var} [X]$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{ {x}^{4}}} , \\hspace{1em} & x \\ge  1 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{2em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157994 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a87439a9-416c-5b3e-b783-e472e7c213a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156886} {:answer/id #uuid "f1386606-1964-5913-a800-1f49406a4e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156887} {:answer/id #uuid "1c60de31-2baa-514e-b665-926d370ff53e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156888} {:answer/id #uuid "5102fa65-8149-5d77-8bc1-9dc0634a8468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156889} {:answer/id #uuid "72d4456d-28c2-52d6-8b44-a0e1ae553713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156890}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fe5b0188-9342-5bd5-9bf8-75d1d03da45f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156885}] :question/id #uuid "da29ebf6-6f5c-522d-9eb0-1d67aefc6e44" :question/math-academy-id "q-90533" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $E[X] = \\frac{3}{2}$, compute $\\operatorname{Var} [X]$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{3 {x}^{2}}{8}} , \\hspace{1em} & 0 \\le  x \\le  2 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{2em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157995 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "912fda05-50dd-5a85-9915-db844ea828d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156892} {:answer/id #uuid "464877b4-3f14-5e05-a93b-c9f41ba846c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156893} {:answer/id #uuid "4936caba-3e5b-5359-b7bf-623ece1a7185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156894} {:answer/id #uuid "5abb586a-daa6-5273-afd8-122f111885fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156895} {:answer/id #uuid "658227ee-1f2d-56c7-b55d-bcee83c13217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156896}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b23082ac-b43f-5e7f-a7e2-2819f3c51ad3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156891}] :question/id #uuid "6c515cfc-5bd5-5a01-98a2-bca3d6630d4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-90538" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $E[X] = 3$, compute $\\operatorname{Var} [X]$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{x}{9}} , & 0 < x < 3 , \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{9}} ( 6 - x ) , & 3 \\le  x < 6 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{2em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158483 :tutorial/content "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers $n_1,n_2,\\ldots,n_k$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(n_i,n_j)=1\n$$\n\nwhenever\n\n$$\ni \\neq j\n$$\n\nthen for any integers $a_1,a_2,\\ldots,a_k$ the system\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}x \\equiv  {a}_{1} & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} {n}_{1} ) \\\\ x \\equiv  {a}_{2} & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} {n}_{2} ) \\\\ \\hspace{1em} \\vdots  &  \\\\ x \\equiv  {a}_{k} & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} {n}_{k} )\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo $n_1 n_2 \\cdots n_k$.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the smallest natural number solution to the following system of linear congruences:\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}x \\equiv  4 & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 5 ) \\\\ x \\equiv  7 & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 9 )\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThis system can be interpreted as follows. Suppose we have $x$ identical \"bricks\" and we know that:\n\n- if we stack them in piles with $5$ bricks each, then the last remaining pile will contain $4$ bricks.\n- if we stack them in piles with $9$ bricks each, then the last remaining pile will contain $7$ bricks.\n\nHow can we find the smallest possible number $x$?\n\n![](images/dd/dd89c8b56cd455b7aafa41cd8d305a7455246141851c49d26ba5cf955f87de7a.png)\n\nFirst, we note that $5$ and $9$ are coprime. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo\n\n$$\n5 \\cdot 9 = 45\n$$\n\nFrom the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 4 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:5) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 4 + 5t, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & ≡7 & (\\bmod 9) \\\\ 4 + 5t & ≡7 & (\\bmod 9) \\\\ 5t & ≡3 & (\\bmod 9)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $9$ is a relatively small modulus, we can find the multiplicative inverse of $5$ by trial and error. We find that $2 \\cdot 5 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:9)$. So, we multiply both sides of the above congruence by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5t & ≡3 & (\\bmod 9) \\\\ 2 \\cdot 5t & ≡2 \\cdot 3 & (\\bmod 9) \\\\ t & ≡6 & (\\bmod 9)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt = 6 + 9s\n$$\n\nwhere $s \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Substituting this into the expression for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 4 + 5t \\\\\n&= 4 + 5(6 + 9s) \\\\\n&= 34 + 45s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x \\equiv 34 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:45)$ is the unique solution modulo $45$. The solution $x=34$ is the smallest natural number solution to the system, and all other solutions are obtained by adding or subtracting multiples of $45$ to this solution.\n\nFinally, let's check that this is indeed correct. Substituting $x=34$ into the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}34 & ≡4 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ 34 - 6(5) & ≡4 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ 4 & ≡4 & (\\bmod 5), ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=34$ into the second congruence, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}34 & ≡7 & (\\bmod 9) \\\\ 34 - 3(9) & ≡7 & (\\bmod 9) \\\\ 7 & ≡7 & (\\bmod 9).✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3f9c314d-11ff-55e0-9e5a-7cb04af14b8e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4399 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158507 :tutorial/content "When we have an exponential function in an inequality and the base of the exponential is greater than $1,$ we can take the logarithm of both sides of the inequality.\n\nFor example, to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n2^x < 3,\n$$\n\nwe can take the logarithm (base $2$) of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{x}) & <{\\log }_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ x & <{\\log }_{2}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reason why we're allowed to take a logarithm of both sides of an inequality is that taking a logarithm preserves the order of numbers. That is to say, if $a < b,$ then $\\log_n (a) < \\log_n (b),$ provided that $n > 1$ and $a,b>0.$\n\nTo see this concretely, consider the following order of numbers:\n\n$$\n0.5 < 1 < 2 < 3\n$$\n\nIf we take the logarithm (base $2$) of all the numbers above, they stay in the same order:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-1}_{ \\log_2(0.5) } < \\underbrace{0}_{ \\log_2(1) } < \\underbrace{1}_{ \\log_2(2) } < \\underbrace{ \\,\\, 1.585 \\,\\, }_{ \\log_2(3) }\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "43d19387-94b0-5f0d-bbfb-94bd473a944a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4656 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158511 :tutorial/content "When we have a logarithm in an inequality with base $n > 1,$ we can use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $n.$\n\nHowever, there is a catch: when we solve the resulting inequality, we must remember to remove extraneous solutions. We can do this by keeping only the solutions that are in the domain of the original logarithm.\n\nFor example, to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\log_2 (x-1) < 4,\n$$\n\nwe write each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{\\log }_{2}⁡(x-1)} & <{2}^{4} \\\\ x-1 & <16 \\\\ x & <17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx-1 > 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x > 1\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x < 17,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty, 17).$ But the domain of the logarithm is $x > 1,$ which can be expressed as $(1, \\infty).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 17) \\cap (1, \\infty) = (1, 17)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in (1,17),$ which can also be written as $1 < x < 17.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5f8d2a28-784d-5ec0-b07f-a448d69b2d84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4665 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158532 :tutorial/content "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\tan{t},\\quad y=a\\sec{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. The domain of the parameter $t$ is\n\n$$\nt\\in[-\\pi,\\pi],\\qquad t\\neq \\pm \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe branches corresponding to different values of $t$ are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f787b25a9ed78b01a3a4d7a8c88c43649662384fd55df2bb690449704dc7697.png)\n\nThis parametrization contains discontinuities at $t=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ One consequence of this is that the curve appears to \"jump\" between different quadrants at the singular points.\n\nFor the most part, we won't worry too much about the domain of $t.$ However, it does become important when we're parametrizing part of a hyperbola, which we'll deal with later.\n\n**Note:** To understand where this parametrization comes from, recall the trigonometric identity\n\n$$\n1 + \\tan^2 t = \\sec^2 t.\n$$\n\nRearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\sec^2 t - \\tan^2 t  =1,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sec }^{2}⁡t & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ {\\tan }^{2}⁡t & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,x=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0cd62814-be59-57f8-b26e-e22e038ae18a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4745 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158540 :tutorial/content "We've used the fact that the parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=b\\tan{t},\\quad y=a\\sec{t}.\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants. Now, we'll demonstrate why this is true.\n\nFirst, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t & ⟹ &  & \\operatorname{tan}⁡t & =\\frac{x}{b}, \\\\ y & =a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t & ⟹ &  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡t & =\\frac{y}{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the secant-tangent identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\tan }^{2}⁡t+1 & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{x}{b}\\right)}^{2}+1 & ={\\left(\\frac{y}{a}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the Cartesian equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin. Therefore, the parametric equations do indeed describe a vertical hyperbola." :tutorial/id #uuid "5cba73ca-1b8b-5f75-bd4e-d67eb65ea890" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4829 :tutorial/title "Proof That the Parametric Equations Describe a Vertical Hyperbola"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158552 :tutorial/content "The **Diffie-Hellman** shared secret exchange protocol is a **public key cryptographic algorithm** that allows two parties to share a secret message over an insecure communication channel. It uses modular exponentiation to derive the shared secret without directly transmitting it.\n\nThe discovery of the Diffie-Hellman protocol laid the foundations for many modern cryptographic protocols for securely transferring online information. Before considering the Diffie-Hellman algorithm in detail, let's review its mathematical foundations.\n\nSuppose that we need to compute $8^{24} \\:\\text{mod}\\: 59$. To do that efficiently, we proceed as follows:\n\nFirst, we write $24$ as a sum of powers of $2{:}$\n\n$$\n24 = 2^4+2^3 = 16+8\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n8^{24} = 8^{16+8} = 8^{16} \\cdot 8^{8}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the exponents modulo $59$ using consecutive squaring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8^{2} & ≡64≡5 & (\\bmod 59) \\\\ 8^{4} & ≡5^{2}≡25 & (\\bmod 59) \\\\ 8^{8} & ≡25^{2}≡625≡35 & (\\bmod 59) \\\\ 8^{16} & ≡35^{2}≡1225≡45 & (\\bmod 59)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, by multiplying the highlighted exponents, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{24} & ≡{8}^{16}\\cdot {8}^{8} & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}59) \\\\  & ≡45\\cdot 35 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}59) \\\\  & ≡1\\hspace{0.167em}575 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}59) \\\\  & ≡26\\cdot 59+41 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}59) \\\\  & ≡41 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}59) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ec2bae3c-ada5-563a-8e49-9718a4c7119a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5665 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158609 :tutorial/content "We'll now justify why the Jacobian determinant gives the (local) area scale factor of a transformation.\n\nConsider a rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane of length $\\delta u,$ width $\\delta v,$ whose bottom-left corner is at the point $(u_0,v_0),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/9a/9a7d700c343fd2807e70b43d688c2f47ed3724e88fc9849e23aa20ee0be10b15.png)\n\nFurthermore, we assume the following:\n\n- $\\mathbf T: \\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow \\mathbb R^2$ is a $C^1$ transformation throughout $\\Delta,$ and contains no critical points.\n\n- The image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the region $D.$\n\n- The image of $(u_0,v_0)$ is $(x_0,y_0).$\n\n- The image of $S_1$ is $S_1',$ and its endpoints are $(x_0,y_0)$ and $A.$\n\n- The image of $S_2$ is $S_2',$ and its endpoints are $(x_0,y_0)$ and $B.$\n\nSince $S_1'$ is the image of $S_1,$ it can be parametrized by varying $u$ only. Similarly, since $S_2'$ is the image of $S_2,$ it can be parametrized by varying $v$ only. So, the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a\n= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} + \\delta  u , {v}_{0} ) \\\\ y \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} + \\delta  u , {v}_{0} )\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\n\\qquad \n\n\\mathbf b\n= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} , {v}_{0} + \\delta  v ) \\\\ y \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} , {v}_{0} + \\delta  v )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\delta u$ is small, $S_1'$ can be approximated by a line segment. Similarly, since $\\delta v$ is small, $S_2'$ can also be approximated by a line segment. Let's construct two vectors $\\mathbf{s}_1$ and $\\mathbf{s}_2$ that span our two segments, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1ff10cdcde237698a28c6f76053de50745ef12c67dfc5290ac6e06893d201972.png)\n\nWe can find the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ by subtracting the position vectors of its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1}=a-\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that when $\\delta u$ is small, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{∂x}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u} \\\\ {\\frac{∂y}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ can be approximated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf s_1 \\approx\n\\delta u \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  x}{\\partial  u}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  y}{\\partial  u}}\\end{array} \\right]}_{(u_0,v_0)}\n$$\n\nUsing similar arguments, we have the following approximation for $\\mathbf s_2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf s_2 \\approx\n\\delta v \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  x}{\\partial  v}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  y}{\\partial  v}}\\end{array} \\right]}_{(u_0,v_0)}\n$$\n\nThe area of $D$ is approximately equal to the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf s_1$ and $\\mathbf s_2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D)\n\\approx\n\\left|\n\\det {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} | & | \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\\\ \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{s}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{s}}_{2} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\\\ \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} | & | \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em}\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\right|.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our approximations for $\\mathbf s_1$ and $\\mathbf s_2,$ we arrive at the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ≈{\\left|\\det \\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,\\delta u\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\,\\,\\delta v\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\delta u\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\delta v\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}\\right|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} \\\\  & ={|\\delta u\\delta v\\cdot (\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\frac{∂y}{∂u})|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} \\\\  & =\\delta u\\delta v\\cdot {|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "07200f56-2408-51a2-9e3d-6b5d727ef5ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9453 :tutorial/title "The Jacobian as a Local Area Scale Factor"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158610 :tutorial/content "Suppose we encrypt the $3$-letter English word $\\text{DOG}$ to ciphertext using the RSA cipher with the public key\n\n$$\n(e,n)=(13,51)\n$$\n\nWe'll assume that we use the usual English alphabet (shown above) during the encryption, where each letter is encrypted separately. Let's determine the ciphertext that results from the encryption.\n\n*Hint: Use the `mod` function on your calculator or a software package to compute the required powers modulo $n$.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo encrypt a word using the RSA cipher with the public key\n\n$$\n(e,n)=(13,51)\n$$\n\nover the English alphabet, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the word in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Raise each index to the power of $e=13$ modulo $n=51$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}3^{13}≡12 & (\\bmod 51) \\\\ 14^{13}≡5 & (\\bmod 51) \\\\ 6^{13}≡27 & (\\bmod 51)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ciphertext is\n$12 \\:\\: 05 \\:\\: 27$." :tutorial/id #uuid "f4c29142-6b32-52a0-b5c5-9029a89a7001" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9632 :tutorial/title "Encrypting Messages Using RSA"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158611 :tutorial/content "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSuppose we encrypt a $3$-letter English word using the RSA cipher with the public key\n\n$$\n(e,n)=(19,65)\n$$\n\nand get the ciphertext $41 \\:\\: 14 \\:\\: 59$. Assuming we used the usual English alphabet (shown above) during the encryption and each letter was encrypted separately, how can we decrypt this and arrive at the original plain text word?\n\n*Hint: Use the `mod` function on your calculator or a software package to compute the required powers modulo $n$.*\n\nTo decrypt a message using the RSA cipher, we need to know the private key.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nn=65 = 5 \\times 13\n$$\n\nLet's denote $p=5$ and $q=13$. Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ(n) &= (p - 1)(q - 1) \\\\\n&= (5 - 1)(13 - 1) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 12 \\\\\n&= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the RSA cipher with private key $(d,n)$, the first component $d$ is the multiplicative inverse of $e=19$ (the first component of the public key) modulo\n\n$$\n\\phi(n)=48{:}\n$$\n\n$$\nd = e^{-1} \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 48)\n$$\n\nSo, let's find the inverse of $e=19$ modulo $\\phi(n)=48$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}48 & = & 19\\cdot 2 & + & 10 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 19 & = & 10\\cdot 1 & + & 9 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 10 & = & 9\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 &= 48 - 19 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n9 &= 19 - 10 \\\\\n1 &= 10 - 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 10 - 9 \\\\\n&= 10 - (19 - 10) \\\\\n&= 10 \\cdot 2 - 19 \\\\\n&= (48 - 19 \\cdot 2) \\cdot 2 - 19 \\\\\n&= 48 \\cdot 2 + 19 \\cdot (-5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $48$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}48 \\cdot 2 + 19 \\cdot (-5) & ≡1 & (\\bmod 48) \\\\ 19 \\cdot 43 & ≡1 & (\\bmod 48)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $19 \\cdot 43 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:48)$, which means that the inverse of $e=19$ modulo $48$ is $e^{-1}=43$.\n\nThus, our private key is\n\n$$\n(d,n) = (43,65)\n$$\n\nNow, to decrypt a word using the RSA cipher with the private key\n\n$$\n(d,n)=(43,65)\n$$\n\nover the English alphabet, we do the following:\n\n- Raise each index to the power of $d=43$ modulo $n=65$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}41^{43}≡11 & (\\bmod 65) \\\\ 14^{43}≡14 & (\\bmod 65) \\\\ 59^{43}≡19 & (\\bmod 65)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indexes:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the plaintext was\n$L \\:\\: O \\:\\: T$." :tutorial/id #uuid "b57ae47c-2e45-505f-be2f-f7a974bfb74f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9633 :tutorial/title "Decrypting Messages Using RSA"} {:answer/id #uuid "a950e4e7-15c6-5d67-b822-d815f13a51fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "E[\\widehat{p}] = 0.45, \\operatorname{Var} [\\widehat{p}] = 0.0124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159789} {:answer/id #uuid "bd56ba8b-0c04-5716-93ab-02934427d9e4" :answer/typ