7592186487260} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "183c7bd1-c965-51f8-b1ac-7a7863aba7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486643} {:answer/id #uuid "1930fe5c-7248-5f8e-9be2-f6fdc0db788e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486646}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486643} :answer-field/id #uuid "fadb4645-7f64-5b96-9f43-b05796106137" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6094a958-deb9-5dcb-b85d-1d2a1044d120" :question/math-academy-id "q-253511" :question/problem "Consider the set $A=[0,2].$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $\\inf (A).$\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le 0$ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,0]$ contains only lower bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>0$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>0.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,0]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n\\inf (A)=\\max (-\\infty ,0]=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $0$ is also the minimum of $A.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ bound of $A.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\inf (A)=0\\in A.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8929061-a36b-574c-a4a0-2a9230b65f4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488919}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488919} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c762596-2608-5e67-889c-627985c34945" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2c1d7015-20c9-5785-8dde-ce877756d0c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-253549" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}+1}:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the supremum of $A?$\n\n$\\sup (A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. \n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(-1)^{1}}{(1)^{2}+1} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{(-1)^{2}}{(2)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{(-1)^{3}}{(3)^{2}+1} & =-\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{(-1)^{4}}{(4)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{5},\\,-\\frac{1}{10},\\,\\frac{1}{17}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sup (A) & =\\max \\{-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{5},\\,-\\frac{1}{10},\\,\\frac{1}{17}\\}=\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162530 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b722803a-370b-5cc6-8dec-952c663984fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sup (A)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160503} {:answer/id #uuid "128ee1a2-ad98-5075-846f-b8cbe5627a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{7}{4}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487604} {:answer/id #uuid "7d320ab4-e7ea-5b1a-9b1e-fcd8c46760b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "sup(A)=1\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487808} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2c2649-8169-5a93-bf6e-55c2f61519ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{7}{4}\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488011} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb9e03b-cb3d-5a8d-9ae0-14d4a6eba10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "sup(A)=1\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488041}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487604} :answer-field/id #uuid "fce77323-ea42-5087-8e83-d953a7ffc6f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4afd4c3a-6ec4-5cdc-b8ec-af8373f2b829" :question/math-academy-id "q-253840" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{1+3{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=1+3{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/19/198c3d6737439fcfa204926394b317aae0026502b5eaacb9e2f0429941f23db3.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $1.$ The maximum of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\max (A)={a}_{1}=1+3{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\ge {a}_{1}$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x<{a}_{1}$ cannot be an upper bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\frac{7}{4},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\n\\sup (A)=\\min [\\frac{7}{4},\\infty )=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{7}{4}$ is the maximum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sup (A)=\\frac{7}{4}\\in A.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43d3c0fd-5ae1-5420-a4ca-ca1916926180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487701}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487701} :answer-field/id #uuid "113dae54-601d-51b2-b64c-2c10aa2e3a1d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "63e6c2b8-b883-58eb-b129-69256fecf5f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-253853" :question/problem "![](images/73/73d739c3d2e4c6bc9d296136ef6f17e7fa0bf565aa4c8c4712e54df4e952935f.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-1,3].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -1$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $A$ because $-1,$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=\\inf (A)=\\max (-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8957db0d-2788-5a97-accb-1b58cd4b66b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160516} {:answer/id #