86166320} {:answer/id #uuid "59dab537-0f75-5b5b-899a-9aaad44d9a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{arsech}  \\left(\\frac{x - 1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166321} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a22d4a-b7fe-5a71-94cd-a1afd9eede6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{arsech}  (x + \\frac{1}{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166323} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4b266c-90ba-52e9-9490-59f9211854b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{arcsch}  (x + \\frac{1}{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166324} {:answer/id #uuid "ce603a3b-9e88-5419-84c3-cec1a9367058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcsch}  (x + \\frac{1}{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166325} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a4d961-d74f-5bc3-aae3-db9a6f686d9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsch}  (x + \\frac{1}{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166326} {:answer/id #uuid "47da47fc-953e-5c4a-b864-60e1fda3b91c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arsech}  (x + \\frac{1}{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166490 :question/id #uuid "13800660-08b4-50af-93b4-1013bbf41e66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12492" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y) = 15xy^2$ on the portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin, where $x\\geq 0,$ and $y \\leq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region $D\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n![](images/a3/a381fede820f9fc5ba08bb9569906d41a3e8f779de38b446c4f271ceef3a5ccd.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = \\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot \\pi \\cdot 1^2 = \\dfrac\\pi 4.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}15x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}15r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}15{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{5}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ({1}^{5}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =({\\sin }^{3}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} = \\dfrac{4}{\\pi} \\cdot 1 = \\dfrac{4}{\\pi}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166517 :question/id #uuid "68a1f42e-a8c7-5be3-b051-68bfd4707347" :question/math-academy-id "e-21429" :question/problem "$$\ny''' + 4y'  =  6,  \\qquad \\big\\{ y_1 = 1, \\: y_2 = \\cos(2x), \\: y_3 = \\sin(2x) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny(x) = A + B \\cos(2x) + C \\sin(2x) + u_1(x) + u_2(x) \\cos(2x) + u_3(x) \\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $u_3(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y_p} = {u_1}{y_1} + {u_2}{y_2} + {u_3}{y_3},\n\\qquad\n{u_i} = (-1)^{3-i} \\int \\frac{g(x){W_i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x, \n\\qquad\ni=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of $y_1,y_2,y_3,$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x) = 6.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1= 1, \\qquad y_2= \\cos(2x), \\qquad y_3= \\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1,$ $y_2,$ and $y_3$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 0 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 0 & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x)+8{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1,$ $y_2,$ and $y_3$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find $u_3,$ we compute the determinant $W_3(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{3}(x) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 0 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3}(x) & =(-1)^{3-3}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{3}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{6(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166564 :question/id #uuid "d2127a8e-14bb-5574-a542-852333fe4cca" :question/math-academy-id "e-21789" :question/problem "Let $F(x) = 2x^2+5$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ to $x\\in \\mathbb R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n \\cos(nx) + b_n \\sin(nx)\\right).\n$$\n\nDetermine the integral expression for the coefficient $a_4.$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left( a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $a_0,$ $a_n,$ and $b_n$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\cos(4x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ &= ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ &= \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1, \\sin(x), \\cos(x), \\sin(2x), \\cos(2x), \\ldots$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\na_4 \\left\\langle \\cos(4x), \\cos(4x) \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={a}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2{x}^{2}+5)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166587 :question/id #uuid "1491963b-fbd8-57fa-bdde-f2d6705fbb83" :question/math-academy-id "e-8258" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2,$ where $x = s\\cos{t}$ and $y = t\\sin{s}.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y = y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s) \\\\  & =-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2t{\\sin }^{2}⁡s \\\\  & =2t{\\sin }^{2}⁡s-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2t{\\sin }^{2}⁡s-{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the identity $\\sin 2t = 2\\sin t\\cos t$ in the above simplification."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166592 :question/id #uuid "181b2a5e-7caf-5527-8602-e67a945affde" :question/math-academy-id "e-8337" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIf $y = 2\\operatorname{arsinh} {x^2},$ then find $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}  x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{({x}^{2})}^{2}+1}}\\cdot {({x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+1}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166593 :question/id #uuid "ce19d882-3c04-5ef1-8393-7de582df188d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8338" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIf $y =\\operatorname{arcosh} (x+1),$ then find $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}  x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(x+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\cdot (x+1)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+2x+1)-1}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x(x+2)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166594 :question/id #uuid "d5eeb5a1-4bfd-5f1e-b261-548c91eec64b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8339" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIf $y = \\operatorname{artanh} {4x},$ then find $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}  x) = \\dfrac{1}{1-x^2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡4x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{(4x)}^{2}}\\cdot {(4x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-16{x}^{2}}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{1-16{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166595 :question/id #uuid "7cd3958b-aa1c-5c9a-b2ef-a21002aa7ff1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8340" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIf $w(x) = e^{2x}\\operatorname{artanh} {x},$ then $w'(x)=2e^{2x}\\operatorname{artanh} {x} + y(x).$ What is $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nHere, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = e^{2x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{artanh} {x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(x) & ={({e}^{2x})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{artanh}⁡x+{e}^{2x}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\cdot \\operatorname{artanh}⁡x+{e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x+\\underbrace{\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{1-{x}^{2}}}_{y(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{2x}}{1 - x^2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166596 :question/id #uuid "fb41f298-ad49-5dce-819b-5ed8fab7f962" :question/math-academy-id "e-8345" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{-3}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}\\,\\textrm d x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}  x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=-3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=-3\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166597 :question/id #uuid "43e9b2da-1e6d-52b5-9312-c07d2d7a682b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8346" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\ln{\\pi}}{\\sqrt{x^2-1}}\\,\\textrm d x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}  x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2-1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi }{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166598 :question/id #uuid "528e7b19-3de0-5c93-8b7f-82383486eafc" :question/math-academy-id "e-8347" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{2(1-x^2)}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$ for $|x|< 1.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}  x) = \\dfrac{1}{1-x^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{2(1-{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166599 :question/id #uuid "3817c58a-1176-5347-aa96-2846969cae32" :question/math-academy-id "e-8349" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFind the derivative of $y = 3\\operatorname{sech} {x}+\\coth{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFirst, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic reciprocal hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n( \\operatorname{sech}  x )' = -\\operatorname{sech}  x\\tanh x,\n\\quad \n( \\operatorname{csch}  x )' = -\\operatorname{csch}  x \\coth x,\n\\quad \n( \\coth x )' = -\\operatorname{csch} ^2 x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results and the rules of differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =3(-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)+(-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166600 :question/id #uuid "b14f0558-456d-51a9-85ac-0b36158bc52e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8350" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x) =\\coth(5x - 2).$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf =\\coth{g}, \\qquad g = 5x - 2\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g}  = -\\operatorname{csch} ^2{g},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 5.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡g\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-5{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡g \\\\  & =-5{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡(5x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166601 :question/id #uuid "4baa8799-f8ce-521e-833b-8b66c747e701" :question/math-academy-id "e-8351" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x) = x^2\\coth{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nHere, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x) = \\coth{x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{2})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{coth}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{coth}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\cdot (-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =x(2\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166602 :question/id #uuid "bec9b472-31ce-5892-8a17-c4aa3c33ef12" :question/math-academy-id "e-8352" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x) = \\dfrac{\\operatorname{sech} {x}}{4x}.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIn this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\dfrac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x) = \\operatorname{sech} {x}$ and $v(x) = 4x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)}^{′}\\cdot (4x)-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\cdot {(4x)}^{′}}{{(4x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\cdot (4x)-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\cdot 4}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+1)}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+1)}{4{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166603 :question/id #uuid "6e692dca-1bbc-59e7-999f-4acb806ddd38" :question/math-academy-id "e-8369" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIf $y =7\\operatorname{arcsch} (1-x),$ then $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}  x) = -\\dfrac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+x^2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(1-x)) \\\\  & =7\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(1-x)) \\\\  & =7\\left(-\\frac{1}{|1-x|\\sqrt{1+(1-x)^{2}}}\\right)\\cdot (1-x)^{′} \\\\  & =7\\left(-\\frac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{1+1-2x+{x}^{2}}}\\right)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{|x-1|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2x+2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166604 :question/id #uuid "b4dd5d1a-eeae-58f5-b7a1-a6a20fb02df9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8370" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIf $y =  5\\operatorname{arsech} {2x},$ then $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}  x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-x^2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\operatorname{arsech}⁡2x) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡2x) \\\\  & =5\\left(-\\frac{1}{(2x)\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\right)\\cdot (2x)^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2x\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{x\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166605 :question/id #uuid "83b6c061-a8fc-5628-a199-60196b904407" :question/math-academy-id "e-8371" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIf $y = \\operatorname{arcoth} {e^x},$ then $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cotangent function is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}  x) = \\dfrac{1}{1-x^2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-({e}^{x})^{2}}\\cdot ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{e}^{2x}}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166606 :question/id #uuid "8a1806e8-d99d-5107-aa63-0bf841cb44c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8372" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIf $f(x) = \\sin{x} \\cdot \\operatorname{arcsch} {x},$ then $f'(x) = \\cos{x} \\operatorname{arcsch} {x} + g(x).$ What is $g(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIn this case, the expression for $f(x)$  is a product $u(x) \\cdot v(x)$ with $u(x) = \\sin{x}$ and $v(x) = \\operatorname{arcsch} {x}.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the product rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x+\\underbrace{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\right)}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x) = -\\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{|x|\\sqrt{1+x^2}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166607 :question/id #uuid "3d0b8fbe-a118-5ec7-83d4-863d605bed74" :question/math-academy-id "e-8375" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{3|x|\\sqrt{x^2+1}}\\,\\textrm d x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}  x) = -\\dfrac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+ x^2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\int -\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166608 :question/id #uuid "b72eee89-1abb-5081-b89e-6cc8735b7f54" :question/math-academy-id "e-8376" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3}{x\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\,\\textrm d x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}  x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-x^2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=-3\\int -\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=-3\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166609 :question/id #uuid "35322f22-4526-53dd-9839-83dd4c286776" :question/math-academy-id "e-8377" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{8}{1-x^2}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$ for $|x|>1.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cotangent function is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}  x) = \\dfrac{1}{1-x^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=8\\int \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=8\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166610 :question/id #uuid "40513c35-cdde-57b5-be6e-0e19facd138e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8393" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\sqrt{2x^2+1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2+1}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\operatorname{arsinh}  u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $2x^2 = \\left(\\sqrt{2}\\,x\\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u =\\sqrt{2}\\,x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\sqrt{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2}\\,x)}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{\\arsinh}u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{\\arsinh}(\\sqrt{2}\\,x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166611 :question/id #uuid "34f929f2-63c4-5e9b-bd63-c42839a78ea6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8394" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x^2-4}} \\,\\textrm{d}x   =\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nLet's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x^2-4}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{3}{2} \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2-1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\operatorname{arcosh}  u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u = \\dfrac x 2.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x =2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =3\\text{\\arcosh}u+C \\\\  & =3\\text{\\arcosh}\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166612 :question/id #uuid "36f704ba-2263-5351-9a1e-b792b38481bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-8395" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{10}{1 - 4x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x   =\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u =\\operatorname{artanh}  u +C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $4x^2 = \\left(2x\\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10}{1-4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{1-(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\text{\\artanh}u+C \\\\  & =5\\text{\\artanh}2x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $\\left|2x \\right| <    1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166613 :question/id #uuid "b2d41cac-2c65-5d2d-b847-0c1f9bfdf48a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8396" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nEvaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac {x}{2} \\right)^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}u = \\operatorname{arcosh} {u}+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac {x}{2} \\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u = \\dfrac {x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = 2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $2\\sqrt{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac {x}{2} \\right)^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2 - 1}} \\cdot (2 \\, \\textrm{d}u) \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\int_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\sqrt{u^2 - 1}} \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\operatorname{arcosh}  u \\Big|_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\operatorname{arcosh} {\\sqrt 5} - \\operatorname{arcosh} {2} \\\\[5pt]\n\n&=  \\ln\\left(\\sqrt 5 + \\sqrt{\\left(\\sqrt 5\\right)^2 - 1}\\right) - \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{\\left(2\\right)^2 - 1}\\right)  \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{5}\\right) -  \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)  \\\\[5pt]\n\n& = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{2 + \\sqrt{5}}{2 + \\sqrt{3}}  \\right)\n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166614 :question/id #uuid "26ac89a4-2124-5c3b-a84a-d46a2913ca52" :question/math-academy-id "e-8397" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\mid 3x \\mid \\sqrt{1+9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x =\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic cosecant,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\mid u \\mid \\sqrt{1+u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = -\\operatorname{arcsch}  u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n9x^2 = (3x)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u =3x$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{|3x|\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|3x|\\sqrt{1+(3x)^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}3x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166615 :question/id #uuid "46e8bad9-1604-5e94-adaa-bff893dc3d9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8398" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{4-x^2}} \\,\\textrm{d}x =\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nLet's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{4-x^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\frac{x}{2}\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic secant,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = -\\operatorname{arsech}  u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac x 2\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x =2\\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}}dx &= \\frac{1}{4}\\int(1)/(\\frac{x}{2}\\sqrt{1 - \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^{2}})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\int\\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}} \\cdot 2du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int\\frac{du}{u\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}arsech u + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arsech}  \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166616 :question/id #uuid "59ca6986-a1b4-5a77-8d35-269d4e454dcf" :question/math-academy-id "e-8399" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5}{1 - 4x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x =\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cotangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-u^2}\\, \\textrm{d}u =\\operatorname{arcoth}  u +C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n4x^2 = \\left(2x\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{5}{1 - 4x^{2}}dx &= 5\\int(1)/(1 - (2x)^{2})dx \\\\\n&= 5\\int\\frac{1}{1 - u^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}\\int\\frac{1}{1 - u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}arcoth u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}arcoth 2x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition\n\n$$\n\\left|2x \\right| > 1\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166617 :question/id #uuid "3e1768f7-105c-5b22-b980-6d54563bd9b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8400" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nEvaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle{\\displaystyle \\int_{1/9}^{1/6} \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-9x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_{1/9}^{1/6} \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x = 3\\int_{1/9}^{1/6} \\dfrac{1}{3x\\sqrt{1-9x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic secant,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-u^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}u =-\\operatorname{arsech}  u +C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n9x^2 = \\left(3x\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 3 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x =\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int_{1/9}^{1/6} \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{1 - 9x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= 3 \\int_{1/9}^{1/6} \\dfrac{1}{3x\\sqrt{1 - \\left(3x\\right)^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= 3 \\int_{1/3}^{1/2} \\dfrac{1}{u\\sqrt{1 - u^2}}\\, \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{d}u \\\\[5pt] &= \\int_{1/3}^{1/2} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{u \\sqrt{1-u^2}} \\\\[5pt] & = \\left.- \\operatorname{arsech} {u}\\right|_{1/3}^{1/2} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\left(\\operatorname{arsech} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) - \\operatorname{arsech} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right) \\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\operatorname{arsech} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right) - \\operatorname{arsech} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166619 :question/id #uuid "89aeeda2-86ec-5ed2-9ae2-ad68f1b1c599" :question/math-academy-id "e-8402" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x^2-4x+3}} \\textrm{d}x=}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4x + 3 &= x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 2x + 2^{2} - 2^{2} + 3 \\\\\n&= (x - 2)^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x^2-4x+3}} \\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt {(x-2)^2-1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x-2$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x^{2} - 4x + 3}}dx &= \\int(2)/(\\sqrt{(x - 2)^{2} - 1})dx \\\\\n&= 2\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} - 1}}du \\\\\n&= 2arcosh u + C \\\\\n&= 2\\operatorname{arcosh}  (x - 2) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166622 :question/id #uuid "ff40db23-6bc1-539b-9f67-3c72c6f448d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8405" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{1} \\sinh {\\left(2x+1\\right)} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nu=2x+1\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x=\\dfrac 1 2 \\, \\textrm d u\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\\hline x & - 2 & 1 \\\\ \\hline u & - 3 & 3 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{-2}^{1}\\sinh (2x + 1)dx &= \\int_{-3}^{3}\\sinh u \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-3}^{3}\\sinh udu \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{2}\\cosh u\\right|_{-3}^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cosh 3 - \\cosh (-3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cosh 3 - \\cosh 3) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166623 :question/id #uuid "94ad0100-80d0-5fd5-9cda-039d9a8bcc47" :question/math-academy-id "e-8406" :question/problem "Calculate the integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{csch} (3x+1)\\coth(3x+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nu=3x+1\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x =\\dfrac{1}{3} \\, \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intcsch (3x + 1)\\coth (3x + 1)dx &= \\intcsch u\\coth u \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\intcsch u\\coth udu \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(-csch u) + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csch}  (3x + 1) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166624 :question/id #uuid "adec9fbb-c33d-5557-b1cd-dfd42d6c594d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8407" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} {\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2} x^2\\sinh{x^3}}\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=x^3$. Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{3}\\textrm{d} u = x^2\\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\\hline x & - 2 & 2 \\\\ \\hline u & - 8 & 8 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\int_{-2}^{2} x^2\\sinh{x^3}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n&=\\int_{-2}^{2} \\sinh{x^3}\\cdot x^2\\,\\textrm{d}x\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\int_{-8}^{8} \\sinh{u}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{3}\\, \\textrm{d}u\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\cosh u\\Big|_{-8}^{8}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\cosh{8} - \\cosh{(-8)}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\cosh{8} - \\cosh{8}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\cdot 0\\\\[5pt]\n&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166625 :question/id #uuid "1c7e6096-bb20-53a3-a605-42b72948fe98" :question/math-academy-id "e-8408" :question/problem "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x\\sinh{x^2}}{\\cosh{x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\nu= \\cosh{x^2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2x\\sinh{x^2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{2} \\, \\textrm{d}u = x\\sinh{x^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x\\sinh{x^2}}{\\cosh{x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\cosh{x^2}}\\cdot x\\sinh{x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= \\int \\dfrac{1}{u}\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} \\, \\textrm d u \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int \\dfrac{1}{u}\\, \\textrm{d}u \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid u \\mid +C \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\cosh{x^2}\\right)+ C \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166629 :question/id #uuid "b6135ab8-d329-5646-aeb7-dea8b38e7606" :question/math-academy-id "e-8420" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}$\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int _{-1}^{1} \\coth{x}\\sinh{x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{\\sin{x}}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\coth{x}=\\dfrac{\\cosh{x}}{\\sinh{x}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}-{e}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-1}-{e}^{-(-1)}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}-{e}^{-1}-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2e-2{e}^{-1}}{2} \\\\  & =e-{e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166630 :question/id #uuid "555cf939-5320-5230-bcef-4828210fbc34" :question/math-academy-id "e-8421" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\tanh}{\\mathop{\\rm tanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int _{-1}^{1} \\dfrac{\\tanh^2{x}}{2\\sinh^2 x} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\tanh}{\\mathop{\\rm tanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$First, we write our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int _{-1}^{1} \\dfrac{\\tanh^2{x}}{2\\sinh^2 x}\\textrm{d}x =\n\t\t\\dfrac{1}{2}\\int _{-1}^{1} \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}}\\cdot \\tanh^2{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\tan{x}=\\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{\\cos{x}}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is  \n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{\\sinh{x}}{\\cosh{x}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {\\tanh }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{e-{e}^{-1}}{e+{e}^{-1}}-\\frac{{e}^{-1}-e}{{e}^{-1}+e}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{e}^{2}+1}-\\frac{1-{e}^{2}}{1+{e}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{e}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{e}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166631 :question/id #uuid "27ef207b-1337-5a3b-92cc-0d7a8b6bb13e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8422" :question/problem "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\ln 3} \\tanh x   \\, \\textrm{d}x  }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\tan{x} = \\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{\\cos{x}}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{\\sinh{x}}{\\cosh{x}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u = \\cosh{x}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\sinh{x} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\textrm{d}u = \\sinh{x} \\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration according to the rule $u = \\cosh{x}.$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\ln 3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{5}{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5/3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{1}^{5/3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166633 :question/id #uuid "2281e86d-4f21-53da-b6fa-1f178d4862a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8433" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle{\\int} \\dfrac{1}{1+\\sinh^2 x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x= 1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n\\cosh^2x -\\sinh^2x=1\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n1+\\sinh^2x=\\cosh^2x.\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}  x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cosh x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166634 :question/id #uuid "9b63d35a-f1c5-554f-ac4c-68d18afa153a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8434" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\ln 2} \\dfrac{\\tanh{x}-\\tanh^3{x}}{\\operatorname{sech} {x}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1 + \\tan^2 x= \\sec^2{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-\\tanh^2x=\\operatorname{sech} ^2{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-{\\tanh }^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{(1-{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x)\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+\\operatorname{sech}⁡0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}+\\frac{2}{{e}^{0}+{e}^{0}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(2+\\frac{1}{2})}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166635 :question/id #uuid "7af9f5f6-1e14-55ea-8a09-25d211cb40d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8435" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{\\ln 3}^{\\ln 6} \\dfrac{\\operatorname{csch} {x}}{\\coth^2{x} - 1} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cosecant-cotangent identity \n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n1 + \\cot^2 x= \\csc^2{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic cosecant-cotangent identity\n\n$$\n\\coth^2x - 1=\\operatorname{csch} ^2{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t\\int_{\\ln 3}^{\\ln 6} \\dfrac{\\operatorname{csch} {x}}{\\coth^2{x} - 1} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n\t\t\t&= \\int_{\\ln 3}^{\\ln 6} \\dfrac{\\operatorname{csch} {x}}{\\operatorname{csch} ^2{x}} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\int_{\\ln 3}^{\\ln 6} \\sinh{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\cosh{x} \\Big|_{\\ln 3}^{\\ln 6}\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\cosh(\\ln 6) - \\cosh(\\ln 3)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left(\\dfrac{e^{\\ln 6}+e^{-\\ln 6}}{2}\\right) - \\left(\\dfrac{e^{\\ln 3}+e^{-\\ln 3}}{2}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left(\\dfrac{6 + \\dfrac{1}{6}}{2}\\right) - \\left(\\dfrac{3 + \\dfrac{1}{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{37}{12} - \\dfrac{5}{3}\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{37}{12} - \\dfrac{20}{12} \\\\[5pt]\n            &= \\dfrac{17}{12}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166636 :question/id #uuid "14733493-2ad9-5fc2-9afe-26384f6ce9ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-8436" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{2}{\\cosh^2(2x+1)-1} \\, \\textrm{d}x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n\\cosh^2 \\theta - \\sinh^2 \\theta = 1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta = 2x + 1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\cosh^2 (2x+1) - \\sinh^2(2x+1) = 1\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\n\\cosh^2 (2x+1) -1 = \\sinh^2(2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(2x+1)-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(2x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int {\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(2x+1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166641 :question/id #uuid "b6c11fbd-00db-5506-baf0-2b6a94bfa6e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8441" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\cosh^2{x} + \\sinh^2{x}\\ \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\cos{2x}= \\cos^2{x}-\\sin^2{x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cosh 2x = \\cosh^2{x} + \\sinh^2{x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166642 :question/id #uuid "c91d7451-c9b5-57a1-9284-ec3fb617220c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8442" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2\\cosh^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} =2\\cos^2 x -1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cosh{2x} =2\\cosh^2 x -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\cosh^2 x = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\left(\\cosh 2x +1 \\right).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166643 :question/id #uuid "5d3a47d6-5a07-5fb6-9f57-23e74a7ba24f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8443" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\ln 2} 8\\sinh^2{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\cos {2x}=1-2\\sin^2x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cosh{2x} = 1+ 2\\sinh^2{x}\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\sinh^2x = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cosh {2x} -1\\right ).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}8{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x-4x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-{2}^{-2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166648 :question/id #uuid "c0e85144-6e01-5311-84d4-1042856272da" :question/math-academy-id "e-8456" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{sech}   \\left( -t \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{sech} {x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech} {x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cosh x} = \\dfrac{2}{e^{x}+e^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(-t) & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-(-t)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{-t}+{e}^{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166649 :question/id #uuid "65b73c5e-690f-5ed6-b245-c96f2b61662a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8457" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{csch}  (-9).$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{csch}  x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}  x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh x} = \\dfrac {2}{e^{x} - e^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}  (-9) =\\dfrac{2}{e^{-9}-e^{-(-9)}}=\\dfrac{2}{e^{-9}-e^{9}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166650 :question/id #uuid "40c32ead-4f17-57d2-acd3-f65f6de1fe80" :question/math-academy-id "e-8458" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nEvaluate $\\coth  (-\\ln 3).$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe definition of $\\coth x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}+1}{{e}^{2x}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{e}^{-2x}}{1-{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fourth definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =\\frac{1+{e}^{-2(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}}{1-{e}^{-2(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}{1-{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{3}^{2}}}{1-{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{3}^{2}}{1-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+9}{1-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(-8)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166674 :question/id #uuid "1b55924d-42e6-5a8e-b5d2-a18a581882ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-8591" :question/problem "The velocity $\\mathbf v$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by $\\mathbf v(t)= \\left\\langle -4, \\, \\cos{(2t)},\\, \\sin{(2t)} \\right\\rangle.$ Find the normal component of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The normal component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\na_N = \\dfrac{||\\mathbf v \\times \\mathbf a||}{||\\mathbf v||}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $\\mathbf v \\times \\mathbf a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\times a & =⟨-4,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -4 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 0 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 0 & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 0 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2t)+2{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2t),8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $||\\mathbf v||$ and $||\\mathbf v \\times \\mathbf a||\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v|| & =\\sqrt{{(-4)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2t)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}, \\\\ ||v\\times a|| & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2t)+64{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64({\\cos }^{2}⁡(2t)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2t))} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{68} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the normal component of the acceleration, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{17}}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166679 :question/id #uuid "e805dcee-247b-5aa3-9856-f7bd48bd1a78" :question/math-academy-id "e-8597" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,2,\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf B(t) = \\mathbf T(t) \\times \\mathbf N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $\\mathbf N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $\\mathbf{N}(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,0,\\,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{16{\\cos }^{2}⁡4t+16{\\sin }^{2}⁡4t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{16({\\cos }^{2}⁡4t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡4t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩}{\\left(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,2,\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t⟩\\times ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡4t & 2 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}2 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t & 2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t-0,-(-{\\sin }^{2}⁡4t-{\\cos }^{2}⁡4t),0-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,\\,1,\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166722 :question/id #uuid "509f1e7c-9c35-54db-8f73-efffc3080a85" :question/math-academy-id "e-9856" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}y=\\operatorname{sech} {x}\n$$?\n\n1. The function is increasing over $(0, \\infty)$.\n2. The function has a vertical asymptote.\n3. The domain of the function is $(-\\infty, 0) \\cup (0, \\infty)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}y=\\operatorname{sech} {x}\n$$\n\n![](images/e6/e68bcc4e3c81b645bc57046f633f1f2cce8e0a550aca7ce9ece42f778db31f44.png)\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $y=\\operatorname{sech}  x$ is decreasing over $(0, \\infty)$.\n- Statement II is false. The function has no vertical asymptotes.\n- Statement III is false. The domain of the function is $(-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, none of the statements are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166723 :question/id #uuid "7f7fa860-a630-58f0-a5ce-c28cf2fd4a3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9857" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}y=\\operatorname{csch} {x}\n$$?\n\n1. The function has no zeros.\n2. $\\operatorname{csch} {x} \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to 0^-$.\n3. The function is even." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}y=\\operatorname{csch} {x}\n$$\n\n![](images/2e/2e67e1e48ecad04a570bc17b4b4b6a63479c75cd9c2685bfc43a7739de10f0a4.png)\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $y=\\operatorname{csch} {x}$ has no zeros.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have that $\\operatorname{csch} {x} \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to 0^-$.\n- Statement III is false. The function is odd (the graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin).\n\nTherefore, only I and II are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166724 :question/id #uuid "793aaa69-b7c0-56b4-aa88-fd9b4c76e6fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-9858" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}y=\\coth{x}\n$$?\n\n1. The function has a zero at $x=0$.\n2. $\\coth{x} \\to 1$ as $x \\to \\infty$.\n3. The range of the function is $(0, \\infty)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}y=\\coth{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/00/00b3a3a57f32b0ed97ab4ccbba0d6c90be16d7dfa85bbb4d25703a8e2967d26b.png)\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function does not have zeros in its domain.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have that $\\coth{x} \\to 1$ as $x \\to \\infty$.\n- Statement III is false. The range of the function is $(-\\infty, -1) \\cup (1, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166725 :question/id #uuid "e05932ad-3ee7-5b8a-a0c9-8e8ac50990d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9881" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n       \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}$Find a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity $1 + \\cot^2{x} = \\csc^2{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n       \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}$Osborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2{x} = \\csc^2{x}\n$$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\cot^2{x} = \\dfrac{\\cos^2{x}}{\\sin^2{x}} \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\csc^2{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2{x}}\n$$\n\nand thus, we have two products of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is \n\n$$\n1 \\,{\\mathbf{\\color{red}{-}}}\\, \\coth^2{x} = {\\mathbf{\\color{red}{-}}}\\,\\operatorname{csch} ^2{x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eeb6e75e-3620-530f-9bd9-dc1adb5ceca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163523} {:answer/id #uuid "1952e981-b41c-587c-b28b-3d6a8739e7e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163524} {:answer/id #uuid "4d9b96ca-6ad8-560f-a733-77f89f10e777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163525} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5f99dc-4d02-512a-9e6a-e3ccf460a46a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163526} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1a0a90-f8ca-5ef9-8118-4689c1a93112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163527}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b1f53edf-9799-5e7d-ac36-de062e7ac83f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163522}] :question/id #uuid "75450c8a-a957-5e05-b27f-93c3757b29b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-112754" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\operatorname{Exp} \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$, what is $E[X]$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d82e6b52-f5bc-5b37-b8dd-76f82200e935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163529} {:answer/id #uuid "de025df1-12c2-518d-bc5e-8694f14ffcdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163530} {:answer/id #uuid "ee50ef7c-4a76-5cfe-9e6b-5a39dfbd6ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163531} {:answer/id #uuid "233967d3-ad34-5523-aa62-2db67fc9a556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163532} {:answer/id #uuid "43d480f6-154b-5067-9