ield/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56933511-7bac-56df-b79c-e43f47de1424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378686} {:answer/id #uuid "af549cc0-3f08-5b96-911b-8266acb72440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378687} {:answer/id #uuid "1ae28847-626b-5c4b-84eb-5668493198b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378688} {:answer/id #uuid "6616269d-d8b0-5e02-b6cc-b20f0191ea43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378689} {:answer/id #uuid "4d29e524-5968-5c11-92b5-924629837ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378690}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378686} :answer-field/id #uuid "693f5701-baa5-5230-8d27-8fbfe6ab9c1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cdc523d0-2005-5617-9a5b-100488432c13" :question/math-academy-id "q-136825" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\max \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d57b4db-6d97-50c9-9610-473062de51c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266545} {:answer/id #uuid "381a1650-88e7-5895-92a4-54d80c6f41ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266630} {:answer/id #uuid "a43a4fd4-4cb6-534b-b19e-d93a34bf16ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267623} {:answer/id #uuid "ce902b47-557a-5a8c-88e4-f101e42dce23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267989} {:answer/id #uuid "d68c4f89-2fe1-5fa4-a817-0703ba8e0fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268055}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268055} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a4e85e1-14c0-5a34-8b71-adb11fa6ae6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6524f432-8e1b-53f9-ae30-319b701fe5f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-136826" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\max {Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\min {Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}8-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )((8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36) & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,7,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\min {Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}=4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc93f948-9547-58c0-a155-a69aee9ac321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163895} {:answer/id #uuid "17b0043f-faf7-5df0-80ff-21f619c0ffe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163896} {:answer/id #uuid "17ef995e-5470-58f0-b118-500bc50d3e41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163897} {:answer/id #uuid "2883a17c-abfa-56dd-b96e-66c703d60d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163898} {:answer/id #uuid "786825d6-5ced-5c26-8c33-0bed4c666b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163899}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0a599d2d-ad37-5032-a0d6-30196f574856" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163894} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d331bafb-09e2-5bfb-9aa2-74edd028eaaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374215} {:answer/id #uuid "81ce1fc8-7154-54d9-94c5-36a16730fff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374216} {:answer/id #uuid "36575c97-125a-5020-96a4-b3b902aeea63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374217} {:answer/id #uuid "702f6174-9178-5c01-872d-41f7d3ff1d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374218} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb49f89-28f1-5275-bab0-bd9e76e1ed0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374219}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374216} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0942e08-0923-5429-93af-30e2cac7b4f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "49e12370-95b6-55a6-adbd-181ce8b9aebf" :question/math-academy-id "q-163067" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ on the disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}=9\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2{r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{81}{4}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{9\\pi }\\cdot 81\\pi =9.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19a8f019-8506-58c7-abfe-902043687deb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300074} {:answer/id #uuid "2a575606-d150-50bb-aa1d-0a0ba4aec497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300166} {:answer/id #uuid "4e7f614f-ac71-54ca-8274-eef4f70dbaad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300371} {:answer/id #uuid "56b77128-5d04-5662-847a-b54f38da6a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300414} {:answer/id #uuid "cac90675-8760-57fb-80dc-674c12351e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300974}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300371} :answer-field/id #uuid "5587f6b4-9459-5ce8-9831-279933bdb957" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "716b0900-6b2a-535c-9122-4199af0eaf40" :question/math-academy-id "q-327781" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{‴}+{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,\\,{{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny(x)=A+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+{u}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+{u}_{3}(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{3}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x).$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡(x)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(x) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{3},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{3}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{3}(x) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3}(x) & =(-1)^{3-3}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{3}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43785bed-6354-5520-9544-515eb3f47955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312514} {:answer/id #uuid "73a37284-23f3-5418-992b-555b44531652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312891} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a906103-9969-5dec-8e2d-71d03c4cc319" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314040} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2534ce9c-b049-551d-a7e3-68e08b499839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312258} {:answer/id #uuid "2eba7fa0-cdf8-5b6c-84f5-6252bbc05561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312345} {:answer/id #uuid "363665b7-fab1-5111-805b-48107890efd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312412} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecab699-9c2c-5d02-8af2-cf07ae896cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312481} {:answer/id #uuid "85e2d662-c721-53c0-ac55-ccfaedc98bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313048}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312481} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd5b36bc-ff84-53e3-b504-b0efcc87ced7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "beb5a495-580f-541a-b622-3503782168cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-340367" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the half-angle identity\n\n$$\n{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x)=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ $\\text{are not orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2236311d-1dcf-5814-8621-6ca58ac136e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397400} {:answer/id #uuid "80766049-c81a-5e17-9add-d24c5e9a61f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397401} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2a205e-e06c-5302-98bb-33dd8ef28f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397402} {:answer/id #uuid "74af64a7-831f-50b9-989b-da92fb9efa80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397403} {:answer/id #uuid "47abc435-93f1-5e56-be4e-2906875645c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397404} {:answer/id #uuid "84fdd6c1-2fa1-53ec-9f4e-f2c79cf04198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397405}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397400} :answer-field/id #uuid "41c0a54b-4090-5079-ad72-a367200f1bb8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397399} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "576de101-4a4e-5d10-93f1-4794297dd084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397407} {:answer/id #uuid "8be2ea60-7536-54c6-8c40-2b3ca7f53e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397408} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd3c9f2-b034-51ed-b959-1917ee861c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397409} {:answer/id #uuid "a0938cb1-fa06-51a3-9682-a27947461754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397410} {:answer/id #uuid "16682102-e4e9-5e8e-b234-f5f85ae7a7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397409} :answer-field/id #uuid "acd80bdd-4dbe-5452-b9e2-03bec982eb3d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397406} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6896852-8238-5500-8785-a40ba1143f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397413} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0541cc-7371-58fe-8dca-eb545f614b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397414} {:answer/id #uuid "610883b1-a3e9-5606-b9ae-cf0dd1218917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397415} {:answer/id #uuid "2c3717c9-e1f6-51ef-9d7f-0c5c74af7ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397416} {:answer/id #uuid "23137492-fa85-5ebf-bdf6-f3faffc43cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397417}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397417} :answer-field/id #uuid "d466e0c2-5e9a-5545-9677-019c4fb5c4a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aa20d8e3-c4d9-5112-bd9c-ec30f6aaa6fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-340418" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{5}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ &= ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ \\\\ &= \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{a}_{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-10\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={a}_{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{5} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{5} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "538e6ce3-322e-5099-990d-d24025a8c722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315596} {:answer/id #uuid "572ef5dc-2cb6-5c37-b950-0bd1382f24fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315623} {:answer/id #uuid "8511607d-a129-55ac-b7bd-0d120414fbc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315858} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6d0636-208d-529a-89ac-f0f021da43c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315896} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b8756d-9773-5b2b-aa07-cbee2fc74bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316037}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315596} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31e794b-7cb4-5f86-95b9-19dd8cbf85dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0b0ccf32-6f43-56b1-ac5d-39e4b594e1be" :question/math-academy-id "q-344824" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{‴}+{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,{{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny(x)=A+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+{u}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+{u}_{3}(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡(x)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(x) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{1},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡(x)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡(x) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =(-1)^{3-1}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{1}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot 1}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a31a5f2e-8c8b-5630-84d8-e9578576e72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165136} {:answer/id #uuid "2185bba9-af09-575e-b5ef-ea141d98b88e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165137} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf6a12e-df88-5d22-909c-96d9e780108d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165138} {:answer/id #uuid "4dadeca4-7da5-572d-b3c9-9cdb2c969887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165139} {:answer/id #uuid "65f73450-ec0d-5f0b-82cc-ebeac09da6a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165140}] :answer-field/id #uuid "740ebe09-d12f-50f9-b047-11879daa05c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8255bb48-7300-520c-a3ed-7877366101ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-90317" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a random variable such that $X∼U[- 1, 9]$, find $\\operatorname{Var} [X]$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80a7c0e1-5a6b-5cea-8598-3b9a96e046c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165148} {:answer/id #uuid "8e38100b-5d0c-5191-b96c-aaec8c3095a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165149} {:answer/id #uuid "d70ffbdf-1809-525e-8078-dfd68143ccdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165150} {:answer/id #uuid "0fcb3b03-0879-55c4-b921-febe23a538b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165151} {:answer/id #uuid "1b0c0f68-1149-59aa-bcbc-58e29df82bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165152}] :answer-field/id #uuid "559d4452-5f53-5f3d-a8e3-be2e40831180" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "352cf0ba-47a0-5076-a3b8-c1985801699a" :question/math-academy-id "q-90664" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a random variable such that $X∼U[- 12,-8]$, find $\\operatorname{Var} [X]$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c174169-aa47-5034-8eb6-f7f3f6a67204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+3{csch}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514898} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab17e12-16fb-560a-9043-5efb3ec8c7c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515036} {:answer/id #uuid "9e902e56-a500-5a45-80bd-107225b4bc77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x-3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515208} {:answer/id #uuid "d88b228f-0bb7-5bcc-ba0a-47bf3064e252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{coth}⁡x(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x-3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515329} {:answer/id #uuid "dca96d3f-da77-5807-8f9d-d795652b78cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-{sech}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515337}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515208} :answer-field/id #uuid "49a21963-b209-5ca1-abae-2d86c7c8ded5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea9f8f07-a38b-5320-8a01-10ad61981f4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95282" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic reciprocal hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)^{′}=-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x,\\,(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)^{′}=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x,\\,(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)^{′}=-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-(-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x-3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5b266ab-b7a9-5eca-9727-c1401397bb47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{\\ln^{2} x + 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165334} {:answer/id #uuid "d54bd814-17a0-5dd5-9ed3-858dd06a8c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2\\ln x + 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165335} {:answer/id #uuid "221a8193-c9d7-5e96-9749-ff7d51f4f242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{\\ln^{2} x - 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165336} {:answer/id #uuid "4881c426-93aa-52de-8607-d0291d124e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{1 - \\ln^{2} x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165337} {:answer/id #uuid "49b07f31-d7c2-5a5f-b482-3a4b079d4f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\ln x^{2} + 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165338}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6b34bd8e-03b9-595e-8ba5-af5a932f7519" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165333}] :question/id #uuid "3b02f68b-58d4-5a70-bcc3-655de2624883" :question/math-academy-id "q-95426" :question/problem "If $y = 2\\operatorname{arsinh}  (\\ln x)$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx} =$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a0450e8-6fb9-5c06-a492-b2d7b234798f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+2{csch}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514929} {:answer/id #uuid "43babeff-1414-59a6-a743-e2ee441fdb19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515016} {:answer/id #uuid "6d949e4f-b4ed-5bc7-9540-4068520fb9fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+2\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515100} {:answer/id #uuid "73ac46a2-08ba-5ab9-a955-921da4e67be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+2\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515109} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf636ca-0581-5e6c-b5fd-6de80891eeb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-2{csch}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515401}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515109} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0a3ff9d-4c25-52f6-a5ce-1978a3171c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "36aad765-1c48-5148-a5cb-bc60296086fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-95451" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\operatorname{sech}⁡x-2\\operatorname{csch}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic reciprocal hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)^{′}=-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x,\\,(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)^{′}=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x,\\,(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)^{′}=-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x-2\\operatorname{csch}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-2(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+2\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4f48948-2221-5574-9a09-320c6b463acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sech}  (2x - 1)\\tanh (2x - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165549} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1c5cde-aa14-536b-975a-aa80643ad6d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{csch}  (2x - 1)\\tanh (2x - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165550} {:answer/id #uuid "247f7ea2-a4bf-56ba-b2ec-2936b1b0055d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sech}  (2x - 1)\\tanh (2x - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165551} {:answer/id #uuid "310f0423-384a-5fc5-8c89-5e21351dd11b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sech}  (2x - 1)\\coth (2x - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165552} {:answer/id #uuid "637536a8-3f60-5d70-9b0f-e450bb3ae107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\tanh^{2} (2x - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165553}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b87d93a7-2618-5bad-957a-3f67a632cecb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165548}] :question/id #uuid "53325a1f-060f-5563-8089-8adc0f47a0e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-95574" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x) =-3\\operatorname{sech}  (2x - 1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "421b7a92-fb82-5fac-b1cf-0b831b760fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537365} {:answer/id #uuid "744490d1-9896-5ac5-9b5a-97aeb15a3238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537457} {:answer/id #uuid "84be6896-bcea-56cf-8b32-6d72991af814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537487} {:answer/id #uuid "94e81827-f57f-549f-8221-d7c1a9b3c0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537509} {:answer/id #uuid "cc34c6ea-57ba-59bc-8e55-253f0cdf1126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537628}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537457} :answer-field/id #uuid "526dd014-553b-5336-80a3-305b2a62ee6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "73150534-b7bb-5cf7-bc82-812c547a0e7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-95837" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x-1+1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\left(\\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\right)}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30277ee3-2cae-553d-a54d-a6b8aa645caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537331} {:answer/id #uuid "40870649-16d6-5360-bf14-6f836b129bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537362} {:answer/id #uuid "ac790c7b-fa48-5ef7-bfd9-5febf5087850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537554} {:answer/id #uuid "af873597-e301-5597-826f-dfb57e5af612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537561} {:answer/id #uuid "c68a61fd-7366-5723-97a4-3ca62cbf1471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537614}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537362} :answer-field/id #uuid "799a9089-2afe-5f59-9bb0-cb37a38513ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8a28f839-bb5d-5966-bf02-cc456e917b3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95873" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (3{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+3{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x={\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x={\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+3{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3({\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int ({\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3ae801-57c5-5791-9c38-7c7bf1661954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡1-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537251} {:answer/id #uuid "89fa846f-c2dc-59c0-bbf7-8bcd7e2a6c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡1-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537492} {:answer/id #uuid "9872ca05-e4a0-5b63-a020-31c95419fd24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡1-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537518} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e43ea6-56f9-53d9-be2b-45a9e8207c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡1+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537609} {:answer/id #uuid "d34fba1f-7a19-51e8-9fc1-a8d8f2c1ef38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡1+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537609} :answer-field/id #uuid "e609e883-1558-50e2-a2ac-f01133148f75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f84738b9-eab0-5de7-ad81-984eef57a947" :question/math-academy-id "q-95874" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta ={\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x={\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+{x}^{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sinh}⁡1+{1}^{2}]-[\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0+{0}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡1+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dd8e5fb-866c-5c4f-bbe1-505f111e2e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537357} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc22d87-d8ca-5a08-812f-2e78dbb4b559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537361} {:answer/id #uuid "6220da56-2689-5ea4-b737-5a5f2d525da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537424} {:answer/id #uuid "a03198e4-2954-5f90-98d3-8f9c6bf9d6b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537531} {:answer/id #uuid "ba135342-aa32-522f-91d3-c61532567d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537592}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537531} :answer-field/id #uuid "47e4c0ba-9e7f-5fb9-9608-805ed6d49159" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8bb7121c-56d4-5c48-ba76-846c281e39fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-95876" :question/problem "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\cosh }^{2}⁡2x+x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡2x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\cosh }^{2}⁡2x+x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x+1)+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x+\\frac{1}{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}]-[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0+0-\\frac{1}{2}{0}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0546a58c-c189-5300-98ab-59e9dfa06285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{csch}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523262} {:answer/id #uuid "20d8011e-02b2-51c3-977c-1f56b7e9e7b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523319} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5366df-ea2b-5885-a020-a71b3a496864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{csch}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523346} {:answer/id #uuid "8b14f7f2-bbd6-5875-adfd-d43889b7fb7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523541} {:answer/id #uuid "d65566be-873f-5b60-b8d3-57f1caa7ee23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523704}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523346} :answer-field/id #uuid "0223c3cc-e13e-53e5-9f9c-5759402c0960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c093bf1b-024d-5e9e-8cfa-be2b2599bfe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-95891" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{coth}⁡x$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)}^{′}\\cdot {e}^{x}-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\cdot {({e}^{x})}^{′}}{{({e}^{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x)\\cdot {e}^{x}-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\cdot ({e}^{x})}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}({\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c4f1c2a-fbbd-568a-974f-babbee1bc55a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{t^{2}}{t^{2} - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165777} {:answer/id #uuid "cda839d8-ec65-5029-9e04-f2d948de1236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2t^{2}}{t^{2} - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165778} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4bc3ab-bd7b-529a-a692-40fd762c0349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2t^{2}}{t^{4} - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165779} {:answer/id #uuid "1b898eb5-3220-5911-80c9-58fdde9caaa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2t^{2}}{t^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165780} {:answer/id #uuid "693888dc-5b46-5913-bfd4-4f38398d10a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{t^{4} - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165781}] :answer-field/id #uuid "83724983-2701-576d-9226-327ed90883cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165776}] :question/id #uuid "c31a71a6-e1d5-551e-b361-d99634e3a691" :question/math-academy-id "q-95946" :question/problem "Given that $t > 0$ and $t \\ne 1$, which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{csch}  (2\\ln t)$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6c38df1-c80c-5ff9-ab95-1ec76624f021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e^{-2} + e^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165783} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a93555-f13b-5d80-bf77-94871da9a258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e^{-\\frac{1}{2}} + e^{\\frac{1}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165784} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd90d62-e86b-53b9-8a51-80b916a338e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e^{2} - e^{-2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165785} {:answer/id #uuid "e01d402d-ae6d-52e2-9493-9049be3554cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e^{-2} - e^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165786} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb8a5ba-d431-5c25-98ed-f3d5e740e710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e^{-\\frac{1}{2}} - e^{\\frac{1}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165787}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9a82828d-abcc-5f61-afd5-414e06ef727a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165782}] :question/id #uuid "ff58e89a-8a73-5dbf-bc8e-5369290ad60b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95947" :question/problem "What is $\\operatorname{sech}  (-2)$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6256621-6a9a-543c-99ae-09d402fa9a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e^{2t^{3}} + e^{-2t^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165789} {:answer/id #uuid "d388df83-4cb4-5c0f-87e1-f276c605e23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e^{-2t^{3}} - e^{2t^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165790} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c444fb-78a2-57b5-84f1-0630117241f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e^{t^{3}} + e^{-t^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165791} {:answer/id #uuid "12feae11-633c-5b33-aa75-22b6118a23db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e^{2t^{3}} - e^{-2t^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165792} {:answer/id #uuid "4f563897-e65c-5c4f-8548-4c212634be58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e^{\\frac{t^{3}}{2}} + e^{-\\frac{t^{3}}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165793}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0e6eaeb1-313f-5347-98c2-d07626463b89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165788}] :question/id #uuid "649db741-7339-53df-961d-b12714233129" :question/math-academy-id "q-95948" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{sech}  (2t^{3})$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44e24fff-6edb-58dd-b6c8-7731cb263263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536388} {:answer/id #uuid "454d23bc-df8f-5fb0-9ac4-bbc97aefba45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536390} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3e4413-d9ee-532c-ae52-619bdac2ce6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x=e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536415} {:answer/id #uuid "5985a81f-c958-57c9-9d4b-af828a400130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536442} {:answer/id #uuid "9c651855-a335-5bd9-bf70-edb22dff84f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536536}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536388} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbdfdfa3-9b3a-546a-977e-1d072d1016bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "09b00f02-3f58-5541-80d9-f11db2b14004" :question/math-academy-id "q-9628" :question/problem "Find a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity ${\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "$$\nOsborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a product of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is \n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x\\,-\\,{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b4344f-0e6c-5645-9574-a4c4a3c9788b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358122} {:answer/id #uuid "39037208-51e5-5863-918d-3b38c718efe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358255} {:answer/id #uuid "5862a07a-00c2-5fcb-8b00-8455797aff2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358517} {:answer/id #uuid "7c71711f-2a1a-589c-970a-8f9a25f4171d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358829} {:answer/id #uuid "97071b96-0058-5668-ab53-95d833ab5cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359073}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358517} :answer-field/id #uuid "60925d35-5561-5000-bd1c-d7c04cb2463d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d801e9d7-50c3-5a8d-84f4-42fa2bf6b19c" :question/math-academy-id "q-96957" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 4 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 4 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 4 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨(0-(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)),-(0-(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)),(3{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+3{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bba9888-420b-5177-9749-b2a2df95fa99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357850} {:answer/id #uuid "5321b4d9-3b60-50aa-86d2-67d71264b404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨2,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358469} {:answer/id #uuid "6169e31e-bcf7-5263-9af6-da6e185aa542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358599} {:answer/id #uuid "96846c65-c6eb-5c00-b01c-0fde168b7d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359068} {:answer/id #uuid "d7aee3fc-9b7d-5c2c-938d-0b42025a181c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357850} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc6b7c4a-1590-5bf4-bd80-56a591de4f1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "553afed7-053a-5b06-8cb8-cd7b8f67d1b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96959" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨({\\sin }^{2}⁡t-(-{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)),-(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-0),(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167526 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b895f2a-59bd-569f-9915-52c2178da152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "87.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166365} {:answer/id #uuid "dd09f057-1f07-5868-95b7-cb7aaf01f64d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "86.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166366} {:answer/id #uuid "e488ee90-d7ff-590c-9cb3-0d8691d103e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "93.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166367} {:answer/id #uuid "1910a920-4530-5692-b8de-b740cc300521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "103.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166368} {:answer/id #uuid "539920b0-e158-590b-a640-1c2fd9637f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "86.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166369}] :answer-field/id #uuid "63594bd0-53a1-5581-8520-a9c6d9182f65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166364}] :question/id #uuid "eb57beb1-0ccc-5b67-b6a0-ba27ca16b237" :question/math-academy-id "q-98146" :question/problem "Given that $X∼NB(12, 0.3)$, what is $\\operatorname{Var} [X]$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167976 :tutorial/content "The variance of $X$ is determined by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{r(1-p)}{p^2}\n$$\n\nAgain, it's worth comparing this to the variance of $X_{\\textrm G}\\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p)$. Recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_{\\textrm G}] = \\dfrac{(1-p)}{p^2}\n$$\n\nIt's easy to see how the two rules are related. First, we can think of $X$ as a sum of $r$ independent geometric random variables:\n\n$$\nX = \\underbrace{X_{\\textrm{G}_1} + X_{\\textrm{G}_2} +\\cdots + X_{\\textrm{G}_r}}_{r \\textrm{times}}\n$$\n\nwhere $X_{\\textrm{G}_1}, X_{\\textrm{G}_2}, \\ldots, X_{\\textrm{G}_r} \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p)$. By the properties of variance for sums of independent random variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Var} [X] &= \\operatorname{Var} [X_{G_{1}}] + \\operatorname{Var} [X_{G_{2}}] + ⋯ + \\operatorname{Var} [X_{G_{r}}] \\\\\n&= \\underbrace{((1 - p))/(p^{2}) + ((1 - p))/(p^{2}) + ⋯ + ((1 - p))/(p^{2})}_{r\\text{ times}} \\\\\n&= (r(1 - p))/(p^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f508f06b-7636-5a90-b8a0-11f133c91c49" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10731 :tutorial/title "The Variance of a Negative Binomial Random Variable"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168090 :tutorial/content "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe derivatives of the reciprocal hyperbolic functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x) & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x) & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) & =-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use these results to find the derivative of \n\n$$\ny= \\operatorname{sech}  x - 2\\operatorname{csch}  x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating term-by-term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x-2\\operatorname{csch}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\operatorname{csch}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-2(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+2\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9f94990a-0da5-522a-9d5f-7e0b269b50ac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5208 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168091 :tutorial/content "The derivatives of the base inverse hyperbolic functions are shown below:\n$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}, &  & \\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}, &  & \\,x\\in (1,\\infty ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x) & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}, &  & \\,x\\in (-1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the derivative of the base inverse hyperbolic functions, we can differentiate combinations of these functions, by using the properties of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of \n\n$$\ny = \\operatorname{arcosh} (x^2).\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2})^{2}-1}}\\cdot {({x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{4}-1}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{4}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "30ebb598-5737-5a10-9f96-b450c6c56039" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5209 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168092 :tutorial/content "The derivatives of the inverse reciprocal hyperbolic functions are shown below:\n$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x) & =-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x) & =-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}, &  & x\\in (0,1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x) & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the derivative of the base inverse reciprocal hyperbolic functions, we can differentiate combinations of these functions, by using the properties of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of\n\n$$\ny = \\operatorname{arsech} {(-x^2)}\n$$\n\nUsing the formulas above together with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(-{x}^{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1-{(-{x}^{2})}^{2}}}\\cdot {(-{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{4}}}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dc2da505-a8d0-5680-b8a0-ddeb66c32295" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5210 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168093 :tutorial/content "Recall the following derivatives of the inverse hyperbolic functions:\n$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}, &  & \\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}, &  & \\,x\\in (1,\\infty ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x) & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}, &  & \\,x\\in (-1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we arrive at the following basic integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x+C, &  & \\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x+C, &  & \\,x\\in (1,\\infty ) \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x+C, &  & \\,x\\in (-1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use these formulas for finding integrals. For example, let's find the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\left(\\dfrac{5}{2\\sqrt{x^2-1}} + 2x \\right) \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of integration and the formulas above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}+2x)\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x+{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "35388b84-1b96-58fb-b1e8-46477370aa96" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5212 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168094 :tutorial/content "Recall the following derivatives of the inverse reciprocal hyperbolic functions:\n$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x) & =-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}, &  & \\,x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x) & =-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}, &  & \\,x\\in (0,1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x) & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}, &  & \\,x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we arrive at the following basic integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x+C, &  & \\,x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ) \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x+C, &  & \\,x\\in (0,1) \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x+C, &  & \\,x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use these formulas for finding integrals. For example, let's find the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3x\\sqrt{1-x^2}} + \\cos x \\right) \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of integration and the formulas above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{2}{3x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "77d4740c-3cac-5695-aa67-597e3c0ce164" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5213 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168095 :tutorial/content "Consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{csch} ^2(2x+3) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nAs it is, this integral doesn't resemble any of the basic integrals we have encountered so far. However, it is very similar to the basic integral for the hyperbolic cotangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{csch} ^2 u \\, \\textrm{d}u = -\\coth u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $2x+3$.\n\nSo, let\n\n$$\nu=2x+3\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\, \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intcsch^{2} (2x + 3)dx &= \\intcsch^{2} u \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\intcsch^{2} udu \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(-\\coth u) + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\coth (2x + 3) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f5adb32a-3a2c-5911-99ec-0efc700958aa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5214 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168096 :tutorial/content "Consider the integral\n$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{4x^2-1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAs it is, this integral doesn't resemble any of the basic integrals we have encountered so far. However, we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{4x^2-1}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = 2 \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(2x\\right)^2-1}}\\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhich becomes very similar to the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\operatorname{arcosh}  u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $2x.$\n\nSo, let $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\text{\\arcosh}u+C \\\\  & =\\text{\\arcosh}(2x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d6c1e767-1a3f-5be8-829b-9afaafaa5d0e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5215 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168097 :tutorial/content "Consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{6x\\sqrt{1-36x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nAs it is, this integral doesn't resemble any of the basic integrals we have encountered so far. However, we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{6x\\sqrt{1-36x^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{1}{(6x)\\sqrt{1-\\left(6x\\right)^2}}\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich becomes very similar to the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic secant,\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = -\\operatorname{arsech}  u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $6x$.\n\nSo, let's substitute $u =6x$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=6 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{6} \\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{6x\\sqrt{1 - 36x^{2}}}dx &= \\int(1)/(6x\\sqrt{1 - (6x)^{2}})dx \\\\\n&= \\int\\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\\int\\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}du \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{6}arsech u + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arsech}  (6x) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1e26b4ef-51bd-56ef-82e1-878d39f180ed" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5216 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168099 :tutorial/content "We can also complete the square to integrate hyperbolic *arcsecant*, *arccosecant*, and *arccotangent*.\n\nFor example, let's consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\left(x+3\\right)\\sqrt{-x^2-6x}} \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAs it is, this integral doesn't resemble any of the basic integrals we have encountered so far. However, we can complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-6x & =-({x}^{2}+6x) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}) \\\\  & =-((x+3)^{2}-9) \\\\  & =9-(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug the above into our integral and collect a factor of $9$ under the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+3)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-6x}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x+3)\\sqrt{9-(x+3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{3(x+3)\\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{x+3}{3}\\right)}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{x+3}{3}\\right)\\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{x+3}{3}\\right)}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis now looks more familiar! Let $u = \\dfrac {x+3}{3}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = 3\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+3)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-6x}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{x+3}{3}\\right)\\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{x+3}{3}\\right)}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a basic integral for an inverse reciprocal hyperbolic function, and we can solve it in one step:\n$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡\\left(\\frac{x+3}{3}\\right)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5f0c7466-a41d-5101-b125-8e6f68fba3ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5218 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168100 :tutorial/content "We derive formulas for the derivatives of the inverse hyperbolic functions from the functions' definitions. \n$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFor instance, let's show that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh} {x}) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh} {x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2+1}\\big).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\cdot (x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}})}{x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}+x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\cancel{(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we can obtain the other formulas." :tutorial/id #uuid "05e38300-7cf8-5c4d-b35e-139ea77d72ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5225 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Derivatives of Inverse Hyperbolic Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168101 :tutorial/content "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$ \n\nWe wish to prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\operatorname{sech} {x}) = -\\operatorname{sech} {x} \\tanh{x}.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}  x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cosh x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation using the chain rule, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)}^{-1} \\\\  & =-{(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)}^{-2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can prove the results for the derivatives of the hyperbolic cosecant and cotangent in a similar way." :tutorial/id #uuid "cb6ff220-98cb-5e8a-8a09-958d158b0d10" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5226 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Result for the Derivative of the Hyperbolic Secant"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168102 :tutorial/content "Recall that \n$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1-x^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x = {\\color{blue}\\operatorname{artanh} {x}} + C, \\qquad {\\color{blue}x\\in(-1,1)}.\n$$\n\nBut we have just seen that\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1-x^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x = {\\color{red}\\operatorname{arcoth} {x}} + C, \\qquad {\\color{red}x\\in(-\\infty,-1) \\cup (1,\\infty)}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the integral on the left-hand side is the same but we used different functions on the right. What is the difference, and how do we know which hyperbolic function to use?\n\nThe difference is the domain of the variable $x.$ When the $|x| < 1,$ we use the antiderivative $\\operatorname{artanh} {x},$ otherwise we use the antiderivative $\\operatorname{arcoth} {x}.$\n\nFor instance, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x\\,{|}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since the limits of integration lie in the interval $(-1,1),$ we used the antiderivative $\\operatorname{artanh} {x}.$\n\nWhile, on the other hand, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(3)-\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since the limits of integration lie in the interval $(1,\\infty),$ we used the antiderivative $\\operatorname{arcoth} {x}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "169c2d19-f0a1-557e-88ed-519503440186" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5227 :tutorial/title "The Importance of the Domain When Integrating Using Inverse Reciprocal Hyperbolic Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168110 :tutorial/content "It can be shown that if $X\\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p)$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = p(1-p)\n$$\n\nWe'll now prove these two results. First, recall that if $X\\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p)$, then the PMF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}p , & x = 1 , \\\\ 1 - p , & x = 0.\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum_{x\\in \\{0,1\\}} x \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nE[X] = 1 \\cdot p + 0 \\cdot (1 - p) = p\n$$\n\nFor the variance, we first recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E[X^2] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X]\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Var} [X] &= E[X^{2}] - (E[X])^{2} \\\\\n&= 1^{2} \\cdot p + 0^{2} \\cdot (1 - p) - p^{2} \\\\\n&= p - p^{2} \\\\\n&= p(1 - p)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8930f4c5-27bd-52df-8dce-05c534c99a55" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5370 :tutorial/title "Mean and Variance of the Bernoulli Distribution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168117 :tutorial/content "It's possible to convert every trigonometric identity to a corresponding *hyperbolic* identity using **Osborn's rule.** \n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of *two trigonometric sines*, change the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the Pythagorean trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\cos^2 x \\:{\\color{blue}{\\boldsymbol +}}\\: \\sin^2 x = 1\n$$\n\nNotice that this trigonometric identity contains a product of two sines. Therefore, to find the corresponding hyperbolic identity, we change each trigonometric function to its corresponding hyperbolic counterpart and change the sign in front of the corresponding product of sines. \n\nThus, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\cosh^2 x \\:{\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol -}}\\: \\sinh^2 x = 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can check that this identity is indeed true using the definitions of the hyperbolic sine and cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x & ={(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}))}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}))}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})}^{2}-{({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2x}+2+{e}^{-2x}-({e}^{2x}-2+{e}^{-2x})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\cancel{{e}^{2x}}+2+\\cancel{{e}^{-2x}}-(\\cancel{{e}^{2x}}-2+\\cancel{{e}^{-2x}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b6c4b399-c2b4-5fcb-a5b8-60bb6d23c29a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5552 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168118 :tutorial/content "We can compute integrals containing hyperbolic functions using the hyperbolic counterparts of the Pythagorean identity. We use Osborn's rule to convert a trigonometric identity to a corresponding hyperbolic identity. \n\nOsborn's rule states that to convert a trigonometric identity to a corresponding hyperbolic identity, we\n\n- replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function, and\n\n- for any product of two trigonometric sines, change the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\n$\\displaystyle \n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to calculate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cosh^2{x} - \\sinh^2{x} \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n\\cos^2{x} + \\sin^2{x} = 1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the corresponding hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n\\cosh^2{x} - \\sinh^2{x} = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\underbrace{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}_{1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x=x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "250a3423-70a4-5d38-9b0f-6f801a0479d1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5553 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168119 :tutorial/content "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$We can use Osborn's rule to write down the hyperbolic equivalents of the sectant-tangent and cotangent-cosecant identities.\n\nFrom the secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1 + \\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nwe have the equivalent hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n1 - \\tanh^2 x = \\operatorname{sech} ^2 x.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, from the cotangent-cosecant identity\n\n$$\n1+\\cot^2 x = \\csc^2 x\n$$\n\nwe have the equivalent hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n1-\\coth^2 x  = -\\operatorname{csch} ^2 x\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n\\coth^2 x - 1 = \\operatorname{csch} ^2 x.\n$$\n\nThese identities can be used to solve certain integrals involving the expressions $\\tanh^2{x},$ $\\coth^2 x,$ $\\operatorname{sech} ^2 x,$ or $\\operatorname{csch} ^2{x}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "525b90c4-f6ea-5c55-8602-49547e261df1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5554 :tutorial/title "Calculating Integrals Using Related Hyperbolic Identities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168120 :tutorial/content "We can compute integrals containing hyperbolic functions using the hyperbolic counterparts of the trigonometric double-angle formulas. We use Osborn's rule to convert a trigonometric identity to a corresponding hyperbolic identity. \n\nOsborn's rule states that to convert a trigonometric identity to a corresponding hyperbolic identity, we\n\n- replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function, and\n\n- for any product of two trigonometric sines, change the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\n$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\tanh}{\\mathop{\\rm tanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFor example, let's consider the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\int} \\sinh x \\cosh x \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that the corresponding trigonometric integral is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\int} \\sin x \\cos x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n  \n\nOne way of solving this trigonometric integral is to use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin 2x = 2\\sin x \\cos x\n$$\n\nApplying Osborn's rule, we get an equivalent hyperbolic identity:\n\n$$\n\\sinh 2x = 2\\sinh x \\cosh x\n$$\n\nRearranging the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sinh {2x}= \\sinh x \\cosh x.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we can calculate our original integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5aeb4951-903e-5815-85fe-2b3fdd1c992e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5558 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168126 :tutorial/content "The **hyperbolic secant** is the reciprocal of the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\n$$\ny = \\operatorname{sech} {x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cosh{x}} = \\dfrac{2}{e^x+e^{-x}}\n$$\n\nTo plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sech}  x$, we create a table with some values, rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\cosh x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27.31$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10.07$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.76$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.54$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.54$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.76$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10.07$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27.31$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\operatorname{sech}  x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.04$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.04$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nPlotting these points, we get the following curves:\n\n![](images/53/5370161b731c9bda369b732ab62e71bcba59b1b12d5cb22c359b3ec7577114ff.png)\n\nWe can summarize some of the basic properties of hyperbolic secant from its graph.\n\n- The domain is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$ and the range is $(0,1]$.\n- It's an even function (the graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis).\n- It has no zeros.\n- Its maximum value occurs at $x=0$.\n- Its graph is increasing over $(-\\infty,0)$ and is decreasing over $(0,\\infty)$. Moreover,$\\qquad$ $\\operatorname{sech} {x} \\to 0$ as $x \\to \\pm\\infty$." :tutorial/id #uuid "7b90aa16-1bc2-5f99-9040-f00d6a4052d9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6097 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168127 :tutorial/content "The **hyperbolic cosecant** is the reciprocal of the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\n$$\ny = \\operatorname{csch} {x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh{x}} = \\dfrac{2}{e^x-e^{-x}}\n$$\n\nTo plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csch}  x$, we create a table with some values, rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\sinh x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-27.29$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-10.02$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3.63$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1.18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.63$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10.02$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27.29$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\operatorname{csch}  x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.04$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.28$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.85$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{undef.}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.85$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.28$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.04$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nPlotting these points, we get the following curves:\n\n![](images/42/426fc7b972acb4a32935967497925b1a0f194f8adfcf8cf232e4edfcb34ce3cc.png)\n\nWe can summarize some of the basic properties of hyperbolic cosecant from its graph.\n\n- The domain and range are $(-\\infty,0) \\cup (0,\\infty)$.\n- It's an odd function (the graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin).\n- It has no zeros.\n- Its graph is decreasing over the entire domain. Moreover:$\\qquad$ $\\operatorname{csch} {x} \\to 0$ as $x \\to \\pm\\infty$ $\\qquad$ $\\operatorname{csch} {x} \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to 0^-$$\\qquad$ $\\operatorname{csch} {x} \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to 0^+$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d323fe32-5975-593f-b91d-a6182405e9c8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6098 :tutorial/title "The Hyperbolic Cosecant Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168128 :tutorial/content "The **hyperbolic cotangent** is the reciprocal of the hyperbolic tangent:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y = \\coth{x} = \\dfrac{\\cosh{x}}{\\sinh{x}} = \\dfrac{e^x+e^{-x}}{e^x-e^{-x}}\n$$\n\nCreating a table of values and plotting the points in the usual way, we get the following curves:\n\n![](images/97/971820e7f7714f95f6745c90bbee9c4c9265f463446b433929b9412f4e8625b7.png)\n\nWe can summarize some of the basic properties of hyperbolic cotangent from its graph.\n\n- The domain is $(-\\infty,0) \\cup (0,\\infty)$ and the range is $(-\\infty,-1) \\cup (1,\\infty)$.\n- It's an odd function (the graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin).\n- It has no zeros.\n- Its graph is decreasing over the entire domain. Moreover:$\\qquad$ $\\coth{x} \\to -1$ as $x \\to -\\infty$ $\\qquad$ $\\coth{x} \\to 1$ as $x \\to \\infty$ $\\qquad$ $\\coth{x} \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to 0^-$ $\\qquad$ $\\coth{x} \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to 0^+$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9b7c9f48-d3ae-56ce-8e9d-af191175fcb8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6099 :tutorial/title "The Hyperbolic Cotangent Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168152 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to calculate the integral\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2 x}\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following hyperbolic identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch} {x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n= \\int \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sinh{x}}\\right)^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x\n= \\int \\operatorname{csch} ^2{x} \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can see that this is just a standard hyperbolic integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\operatorname{csch} ^2{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x = -\\coth{x} + C\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f612c2c5-2457-59c4-899a-0847e3f278a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7913 :tutorial/title "Integration Using Hyperbolic Identities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168153 :tutorial/content "When applying Osborn's rule, we must be aware that sometimes a product of two sines is \"hidden.\"\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n1 \\:{\\color{blue}{\\boldsymbol +}}\\: \\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it may seem that there is no product of sines, and we do not need to change any signs in the corresponding hyperbolic identity.\n\nHowever, recall that\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n\\tan^2 x = \\dfrac{\\sin^2 x}{\\cos^2 x}.\n$$\n\nSo, there is a hidden product of sines within the $\\tan^2 x$ term, and we need to flip the sign in front of this term when writing down the corresponding hyperbolic identity.\n\nThus, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n1 \\:{\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol -}}\\: \\tanh^2 x = \\operatorname{sech} ^2 x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0cd7ebe1-9de5-5914-a1e9-4b88c94e5a0e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7914 :tutorial/title "Osborn’s Rule in Cases Where a Product of Sines Is Hidden"} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c9cc4f-a3ca-5b22-a9a9-481f2fe6587a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼\\operatorname{Exp} (4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170354} {:answer/id #uuid "19e0b67a-24a4-5072-bc52-060f2a5fd744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼\\operatorname{Exp} (2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170355} {:answer/id #uuid "440685a2-ae29-51f9-8634-84a784c93861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼\\operatorname{Exp} (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171712 :question/id #uuid "8ed57b1f-0082-5281-b061-ff2020ed7d66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12364" :question/problem "The fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=e^{x/2}$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x \\in [0,2]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\big\\langle \\text{_______}\\,, \\:-e^{x/2} \\cos\\theta, \\: -e^{x/2}\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWhat is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=e^{x/2} \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in [0,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=e^{x/2},$ and \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=e^{x/2},$ we obtain $f' = \\dfrac12 e^{x/2}.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,-{e}^{x/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{e}^{x/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $1$st component of the product is $\\dfrac{1}{2}e^x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171713 :question/id #uuid "a2ff0c76-0d26-51a2-a2fb-498ff5f85559" :question/math-academy-id "e-12365" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to find an integral expression for the surface area generated when the curve $x=\\sin z$ is rotated by $2\\pi$ radians about the $z$-axis for $z \\in [0, \\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=\\sin z \\geq 0$ for all $z \\in [0, \\pi].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\sin z,$ and\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (z,\\theta) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq z \\leq \\pi , \\, 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=\\sin z,$ we obtain $f'(z) = \\cos z.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{\\cos }^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171725 :question/id #uuid "930d7260-cabb-5b5a-a374-3b33c0770e99" :question/math-academy-id "e-13241" :question/problem "Find the cardinality of the set $\\mathcal{P}\\big((0, 1)\\big) \\times \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$." :question/worked-solution "Given nonempty sets $A$ and $B$, if either $A$ or $B$ is infinite, then\n\n$$\n\\big| A \\times B \\big| = \\max \\{ |A|, |B| \\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, note that\n\n$$\n2^{\\aleph_0} = \\mathfrak{c}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\mathcal{P}((0,1))\\times \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})| & =\\max \\{|\\mathcal{P}((0,1))|,|\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})|\\} \\\\  & =\\max \\{{2}^{\\mathfrak{c}},{2}^{{\\mathrm{ℵ}}_{0}}\\} \\\\  & =\\max \\{{2}^{\\mathfrak{c}},\\mathfrak{c}\\} \\\\  & ={2}^{\\mathfrak{c}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171753 :question/id #uuid "5d0fe2f8-d32b-5156-8a64-1e441768158a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14970" :question/problem "Prove by contradiction that $4 \\not\\mid (n^4 - 11)$ for any $n \\in \\mathbb Z$." :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n$\\phantom{\\lnot}P: \\quad \\forall n \\in \\mathbb Z,\\: 4 \\not\\mid (n^4 - 11)$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n$\\lnot P: \\quad \\exists n\\in \\mathbb Z,\\: 4 \\mid (n^4 - 11)$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $4 \\mid (n^4 - 11)$. Then, $n^4 - 11 = 4k$, where $k$ is an integer.\n\nSince the problem relates to divisibility by $4$, let's write the equation\n\n$$\nn^4 - 11 = 4k\n$$\n\nin terms of congruence modulo $4$.\n\n> Therefore, we have the following chain of equivalences:\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{4}-11=4k\\hspace{1em} & ⇔\\hspace{1em}{n}^{4}=4k+11 \\\\ \\hspace{1em} & ⇔\\hspace{1em}{n}^{4}≡4k+11\\hspace{1em} &  & (\\text{mod }4) \\\\ \\hspace{1em} & ⇔\\hspace{1em}{n}^{4}≡11\\hspace{1em} &  & (\\text{mod }4) \\\\ \\hspace{1em} & ⇔\\hspace{1em}{n}^{4}≡11-4\\cdot 2\\hspace{1em} &  & (\\text{mod }4) \\\\ \\hspace{1em} & ⇔\\hspace{1em}{n}^{4}≡3\\hspace{1em} &  & (\\text{mod }4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the residue of $n^4$ modulo $4{:}$\n\n> Hence, we have that $n^4 \\textrm{mod} 4 = 3$.\n\nNow, let's calculate all possible residues of $n^4$ modulo $4$. To do that, we substitute $n=0,1,2,3$ into $n^4$ and compute the residues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0^{4}\\bmod 4 &= 0 \\\\\n1^{4}\\bmod 4 &= 1 \\\\\n2^{4}\\bmod 4 &= 0 \\\\\n3^{4}\\bmod 4 &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that there are only two residues possible. So, we proceed as follows:\n\n> However, substituting $n = 0,1,2,3$ into the expression $n^4$, we obtain that\n> $n^4 \\textrm{mod} 4 \\in R = \\{0,1\\}$.\n> But this is a contradiction since $n^4 \\textrm{mod} 3 = 3$ by assumption.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, we conclude that $4 \\not\\mid (n^4 - 11)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171755 :question/id #uuid "3451995c-d28f-50f5-b5d8-63ac17c1495b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14972" :question/problem "Prove by contradiction that $4 \\not\\mid (14n+1)$ for any $n\\in\\mathbb Z$." :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\n\\phantom{\\lnot}P: \\quad \\forall n\\in \\mathbb Z,\\: 4 \\not\\mid (14n+1)\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P: \\quad \\exists n\\in \\mathbb Z,\\: 4 \\mid (14n+1)\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $4 \\mid (14n+1)$. Then, $14n+1=4k$, where $k$ is an integer.\n\nSince the problem relates to divisibility by $4$, let's write the equation\n\n$$\n14n+1=4k\n$$\n\nin terms of congruence modulo $4$.\n\n> Therefore, we have the following chain of equivalences:\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}14n+1=4k\\hspace{1em} & ⇔\\hspace{1em}14n=4k-1 \\\\ \\hspace{1em} & ⇔\\hspace{1em}14n≡4k-1\\hspace{1em} &  & (\\text{mod }4) \\\\ \\hspace{1em} & ⇔\\hspace{1em}14n≡-1\\hspace{1em} &  & (\\text{mod }4) \\\\ \\hspace{1em} & ⇔\\hspace{1em}14n≡-1+4\\hspace{1em} &  & (\\text{mod }4) \\\\ \\hspace{1em} & ⇔\\hspace{1em}14n≡3\\hspace{1em} &  & (\\text{mod }4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the residue of $14n$ modulo $4{:}$\n\n> Hence, we have that $14n \\textrm{mod} 4 = 3$.\n\nNow, let's calculate all possible residues of $14n$ modulo $4$. To do that, we substitute $n=0,1,2,3$ into $14n$ and compute the residues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14 \\cdot 0\\bmod 4 &= 0 \\\\\n14 \\cdot 1\\bmod 4 &= 2 \\\\\n14 \\cdot 2\\bmod 4 &= 0 \\\\\n14 \\cdot 3\\bmod 4 &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that there are only two residues possible. So, we proceed as follows:\n\n> However, substituting $n=0,1,2,3$ into the expression $14n$, we obtain that\n> $14n \\textrm{mod} 4 \\in \\{0,2\\}$.\n> But this is a contradiction since $14n \\textrm{mod} 4 = 3$ by assumption.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, we conclude that $4 \\not\\mid (14n+1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171851 :question/id #uuid "cfcca273-3b96-5812-8b0d-029875f0585a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8643" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 5\\cos \\pi t \\, \\mathbf i + 5\\sin \\pi t \\,\\mathbf j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-5\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+5\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-5\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)^{2}+(5\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{\\pi }^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\pi t+25{\\pi }^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\pi t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{\\pi }^{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\pi t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\pi t)} \\\\  & =5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the object's speed is $5 \\pi \\textrm{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v|| = R|\\omega|,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega$ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=5\\,\\textrm m$ and the angular frequency $\\omega = \\pi \\,\\textrm{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v|| = 5\\cdot |\\pi| = 5\\pi \\textrm{ m/s}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171852 :question/id #uuid "5614ccb1-e2be-5944-8229-61d62e5860fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-8644" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 4(\\cos 3t\\,  \\mathbf i + \\sin 3t\\, \\mathbf j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}+(-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{36}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡3t+{36}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{36}^{2}} \\\\  & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2.$\n\nNote that $||\\mathbf a||$ is constant, but $\\mathbf a$ is not.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2\\mathbf r \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad ||\\mathbf a|| = \\omega^2||\\mathbf r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=4\\,\\textrm m$ and the angular frequency $\\omega = 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf a|| = 3^2\\cdot |4| = 36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171895 :question/id #uuid "f6f11512-c37f-5922-b642-5290003b1d91" :question/math-academy-id "e-9492" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd1ba2536264e122c84377a1892c3ca86dd8f273b2eab9ba0e2c53eeb504ef65.png)\n\n  \n\nFind a definite integral with respect to $\\theta$ that's equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\,\\textrm d s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-\\sin{\\theta}\\,$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq  \\pi,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r = r(\\theta)$ for $\\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL=\t\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\sin{\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = -\\cos{\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171896 :question/id #uuid "6753365e-9915-5be9-9a6d-d4c40b2caea0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9493" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a55339eae9b193813ab696538c85696ad5f4267e2e31513bdb2c8998cd711311.png)\n\n  \n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\,\\textrm d s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2(1+\\sin\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq {\\pi},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r = r(\\theta)$ for $\\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL=\t\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=2(1+\\sin\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 2\\cos{\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+4{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)=1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting our integral using the above identities, we can evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)|\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}((0+1)-(-1+0)) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a37b340-a740-58c0-a293-244785e667c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168238} {:answer/id #uuid "bf087cdf-32b1-579c-90e7-eeec5d8b137e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168239} {:answer/id #uuid "17187044-41e5-52fc-ad18-ff0bc887e253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168240} {:answer/id #uuid "32e11d50-d5be-5c30-9eb3-22c7aa081734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168241} {:answer/id #uuid "667433bf-d73a-534b-98a4-38ddb8945173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168242}] :answer-field/id #uuid "667e2642-7ac8-5263-8b27-2e06f39cbdf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e3abafb4-90c4-5366-9f3b-38681c06a779" :question/math-academy-id "q-105735" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\Re (z) > 0$?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{∞}t^{z - 1}e^{-t}dt\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}\\frac{t^{z - 1}}{e^{t}}dt\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}\\frac{e^{t}}{t^{z - 1}}dt\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdaf06ad-8a5e-55d8-857e-f7335543f3c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}(1 + y^{2})\\sqrt{1 + 4y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168256} {:answer/id #uuid "d04a8a98-3f2a-5ac2-b54b-cdb02e7eb5c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1 + 4y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168257} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f4ca0c-8d40-5d35-9bc7-f5dd04d80227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}(1 + y^{2})\\sqrt{1 + y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168258} {:answer/id #uuid "02783d3a-d9a1-5452-822d-966d5e0130ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}(1 + y^{2})\\sqrt{1 + 4y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168259} {:answer/id #uuid "6841d782-b072-579a-b093-77118b70440e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}(1 + y^{2})\\sqrt{1 + y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168260}] :answer-field/id #uuid "86c16368-7829-5fe0-a98e-4f96d9855360" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168255} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bb9acef-e5be-5b06-abee-5c08fa99b877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386190} {:answer/id #uuid "d6756399-d36a-5b4b-96d5-b5ff1380e012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386191} {:answer/id #uuid "cf38efd4-12be-53ea-8790-5991f3174816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386192} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc87696-0f7f-5357-9620-dd2e02cbb9a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386193} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf9de78-7ed1-584c-bc4f-51bca014dd3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386193} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d86a85b-b70e-51ce-aacd-6ac51a7a9220" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bba1677a-6311-54bf-bad0-6a9e581cdd7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-106058" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x=1+{y}^{2}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [-1,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=1+{y}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [-1,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=1+{y}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y}\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -f{f}^{′}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′},\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f{f}^{′})^{2}+(f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=1+{y}^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2y.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74de9016-aec8-5c6f-9c78-665b929a97ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415522} {:answer/id #uuid "a106d988-56e8-55fb-9494-97df8b832cbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415525} {:answer/id #uuid "c191f424-9120-5984-b367-462661abdf0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415527} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf41d2a-2802-588f-84c7-9bab46e26c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415529} {:answer/id #uuid "e38d5fae-3d16-5915-8cda-f00c09b669d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415530}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415527} :answer-field/id #uuid "247cda82-3b8b-57b4-bd85-1eaf3e646212" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ff19c12b-ba54-5c73-b456-a37b42a5dcc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-106277" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is given above. What is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9836cab7-62d9-5b9b-9e5b-0f8589681132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168280} {:answer/id #uuid "edfed025-517d-5759-9dfb-109f455c153c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168281} {:answer/id #uuid "11d8412a-1bb4-5fbc-91cf-d6bd3b0d3538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168282} {:answer/id #uuid "1742e034-b26b-568f-8222-57562657b7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168283} {:answer/id #uuid "31950e0d-19c2-5cbb-a241-2a2d87b37bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168284}] :answer-field/id #uuid "611b9220-7457-5e31-922f-9a89d0be914e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e1a43770-bf79-531a-899c-ea54934eb477" :question/math-academy-id "q-107835" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\Re (z) > 0$?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle 2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}\\frac{e^{-t^{2}}}{t^{2z - 2}}dt\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle 2\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{∞}\\frac{t^{z}}{e^{t^{2}}}dt\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle 2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}t^{2z - 1}e^{-t^{2}}dt\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06dd37ec-93a9-5b21-89e4-561d7cf50bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256841} {:answer/id #uuid "0a8e4a81-ce37-5a0a-bf71-1d9989cbe036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256881} {:answer/id #uuid "297e7d94-4a80-565f-88bd-e5215df2ba8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257151} {:answer/id #uuid "4e711455-77c9-5616-92bb-d797cf1c8968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257454} {:answer/id #uuid "da8ca18d-520e-5091-a63b-d25c9509aa0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258691}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257454} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b3878a-36da-5c94-8d4e-f1633afbca83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2c8bb8d5-c684-5d53-8ebb-fc2e85b6f1e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-123355" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fbf4c3074641b18d12dc7372378550f4c37e0b9b81843c2e57629c891a59263.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfa492a8-e07a-522a-bf45-34af18c7a267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{2} θ\\tan^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168598} {:answer/id #uuid "1813a2d0-d5bd-52b4-99b5-efcb1d2a7640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + \\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168599} {:answer/id #uuid "2aba48aa-c769-5c6a-9e07-f0c67be25a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + 9\\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168600} {:answer/id #uuid "55f60188-6938-5f92-aae8-dedef3328cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{9 + \\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168601} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3a3ab0-69fc-59cc-bc73-08e310482454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + \\sec^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168602}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d4363fdf-f405-59b8-b8f3-f8bb7ba8806f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168597} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d61aec87-e699-59eb-980b-a6a6cf0bb0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405024} {:answer/id #uuid "94ab8694-5f19-5ea3-8d98-092aec26cb4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405025} {:answer/id #uuid "a57cacb1-b76d-579c-8c67-b4527f5232ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{9+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405026} {:answer/id #uuid "1eabb9a5-ecc0-5827-9b8a-fa684cf58678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405027} {:answer/id #uuid "9f675dbc-4798-5a7f-b170-06e1c7c60f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405024} :answer-field/id #uuid "9492d74f-ea7c-56c6-a9a7-679ef961d276" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f58568c3-ce47-5162-ad81-da8c754e43d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-123439" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is obtained by using the pointwise polar arc-length integrand for $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={\\sec }^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\pi /2<\\theta<\\pi /2$? Interpret the endpoint integrals as improper integrals." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={\\sec }^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{\\sec }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({\\sec }^{3}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3{\\sec }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\sec }^{6}⁡\\theta +9{\\sec }^{6}⁡\\theta {\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\sec }^{6}⁡\\theta (1+9{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\sec }^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese endpoint integrals are improper and diverge to $+\\infty.$ The requested expression is determined by the pointwise arc-length integrand on $-\\pi/2<\\theta<\\pi/2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172290 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "477217b2-25da-5eb5-bf11-b8dba3a034f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257407} {:answer/id #uuid "47c32564-aebd-58a5-91d4-f60e41bdce79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257410} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2954a9-a57d-5b93-b489-ad2d5cb06b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258004} {:answer/id #uuid "d940cf3f-dda0-5356-bac1-c5452d47f35d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258684} {:answer/id #uuid "ed844e7d-d658-557e-a456-73ff91011d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258004} :answer-field/id #uuid "343e4e11-bb27-5f39-9989-a4e9e01a1124" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "474b9c27-587d-5510-bc2c-fbc91f7d6903" :question/math-academy-id "q-123623" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta $ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta (1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{\\sec }^{4}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2809705b-8db8-563c-a442-e6f1acbbfe7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y{cos}^{2}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275842} {:answer/id #uuid "71c1c996-c586-5df6-8332-85e7a81d2723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{sin}^{2}⁡2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276505} {:answer/id #uuid "b9250b04-8df7-5d4c-af3c-374371efb26c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y{cos}^{3}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277119} {:answer/id #uuid "c237886b-db4d-5796-b8a6-e5704db492f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277203} {:answer/id #uuid "f483259b-1c3a-5300-b633-474756bee33f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277618}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277119} :answer-field/id #uuid "c141503d-105d-5e48-8e51-df696536e244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5660a1ea-1c96-5f39-8bde-a992c1f8c03a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161069" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\,{\\cos }^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\text{_______}\\,,\\,{\\cos }^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={\\cos }^{2}⁡y$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ is given above. What is the $2$nd  component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x={\\cos }^{2}⁡y\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={\\cos }^{2}⁡y,$ and  \n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -{f}^{′}f{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={\\cos }^{2}⁡y,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨{\\cos }^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y{\\cos }^{3}⁡y,\\,{\\cos }^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ae28bc2-f9a0-5d59-bace-6845445f5266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 4x^{3}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169144} {:answer/id #uuid "e84bd21c-42f3-570e-9500-c24f14ce38e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "π\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 4x^{3}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169145} {:answer/id #uuid "ea344160-f2e6-5a60-b449-05b530fdcad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 16x^{6}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169146} {:answer/id #uuid "00df6180-a1be-57fa-83f1-d9db68dffca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "π\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 16x^{6}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169147} {:answer/id #uuid "297c0a53-60c2-545d-a4e9-631696e3a55e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 4x^{6}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169148}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dd1a3809-886e-5fc6-b6f6-9ef2ef43a75a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169143} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af7bb4e7-5a7e-5614-af5c-b0557486f506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389134} {:answer/id #uuid "217657c7-12a1-539f-a238-a933de3cb8bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389135} {:answer/id #uuid "a7df0e0a-4e17-5d25-abd4-def686f904bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389136} {:answer/id #uuid "b38005c6-8f6d-54a6-8226-eb2b6a6a837e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389137} {:answer/id #uuid "83c1f7c4-9d23-598c-b5ad-74e8cd9c6a42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389137} :answer-field/id #uuid "54ef5f39-9801-5bd2-b219-adbef51cdebb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "929a0232-4773-59cb-9c62-800f13322539" :question/math-academy-id "q-161095" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y={x}^{4}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [-2,2].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y={x}^{4}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [-2,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={x}^{4},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f{f}^{′})^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={x}^{4},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=4{x}^{3}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+(4{x}^{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86f3d55e-239f-5c47-8cd5-360f6bacf82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170017} {:answer/id #uuid "a9dd7a3b-b137-5b3d-89ce-9cf972b7f3b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170018} {:answer/id #uuid "b37ebe4e-4511-5a83-b983-dd1de0e280b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218