random variables $Y_{1}∼Γ(2, 3)$ and $Y_{2}∼\\operatorname{Exp} (3)$. What is the distribution of $Y = Y_{1} + Y_{2}$?\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n| Distribution | $M(t)$ | $t$-domain |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $U[a, b]$ | $\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {e}^{t b} - {e}^{t a}}{t ( b - a )}} , \\hspace{1em} & t \\ne  0 \\\\ 1 , \\hspace{2em} & t = 0\\end{array} \\right.$ | $t ∈ R$ |\n| $N(μ, σ^{2})$ | $\\exp (μt + \\frac{1}{2}σ^{2}t^{2})$ | $t ∈ R$ |\n| $χ^{2}(k)$ | $(1 - 2t)^{-k/2}$ | $t < \\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\operatorname{Exp} (λ)$ | $\\frac{λ}{λ - t}$ | $t < λ$ |\n| $Γ(α, λ)$ | $\\left(\\frac{λ}{λ - t}\\right)^{α}$ | $t < λ$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d052de8a-4f04-5e04-b975-532c813f9c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(1, 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170884} {:answer/id #uuid "10ff75c3-daa4-53f3-8882-8cf83b4ba9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(1, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170885} {:answer/id #uuid "4b146c59-eb19-5c1a-9ad6-dee14307372d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(0, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170886} {:answer/id #uuid "77248f77-5b52-5bbb-9490-c54f729b58b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(0, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170887} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb24657-c3dd-53ab-a504-11156db31320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(0, \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170888}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a0caaede-533a-56f9-b7b6-2d7ce4c9d280" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170883}] :question/id #uuid "2c68225f-88f7-5039-acc8-1a1fd40fb051" :question/math-academy-id "q-256397" :question/problem "Consider the independent random variables $Y_{1}∼N(0, 1)$ and $Y_{2}∼N(0, 1)$. What is the distribution of $Y = Y_{1} + Y_{2}$?\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n| Distribution | $M(t)$ | $t$-domain |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $U[a, b]$ | $\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {e}^{t b} - {e}^{t a}}{t ( b - a )}} , \\hspace{1em} & t \\ne  0 \\\\ 1 , \\hspace{2em} & t = 0\\end{array} \\right.$ | $t ∈ R$ |\n| $N(μ, σ^{2})$ | $\\exp (μt + \\frac{1}{2}σ^{2}t^{2})$ | $t ∈ R$ |\n| $χ^{2}(k)$ | $(1 - 2t)^{-k/2}$ | $t < \\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\operatorname{Exp} (λ)$ | $\\frac{λ}{λ - t}$ | $t < λ$ |\n| $Γ(α, λ)$ | $\\left(\\frac{λ}{λ - t}\\right)^{α}$ | $t < λ$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12628c79-2a79-530f-b542-4583834ac97e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360909} {:answer/id #uuid "1f2794b4-cbd6-5628-92c2-5d9f9522d8d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360967} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa5373d-6695-59fc-8f24-0adc6f5294d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361028} {:answer/id #uuid "35e32fb5-6bc8-5759-8e8f-8d055df5a274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361078} {:answer/id #uuid "d01f1962-4697-5c4f-a9f0-839f272aad8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361767} :answer-field/id #uuid "83dec225-a187-59cf-a85b-4d96d129b48f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ac5215be-1a4b-5b45-94e1-594b34acf8a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-97541" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{\\sin }^{2}⁡3t+36{\\cos }^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({\\sin }^{2}⁡3t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡3t)} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $6\\text{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=2\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =3\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=2\\cdot |3|=6\\text{ m/s}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172652 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "090012e9-8b09-5e22-82c2-405486f3bc7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360870} {:answer/id #uuid "2970d2fe-c113-5f9d-80aa-064f4b9812ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361021} {:answer/id #uuid "4f0d479f-1a2f-565f-9242-2ce77bf9bf7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361183} {:answer/id #uuid "80ed953e-eeaa-5c93-ac60-673f6489ba2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361413} {:answer/id #uuid "f5695a4d-b2cd-5ae5-817f-fe1ebda956dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361938}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361413} :answer-field/id #uuid "e773854b-44fb-50ec-8323-54badb8226b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "37d38f20-8a2f-5a94-a0f5-f641f4040cd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-97542" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144{\\cos }^{2}⁡2t+144{\\sin }^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $12\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=3\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =2\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={2}^{2}\\cdot 3=12\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173126 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to carry out the following hypothesis test:\n\n- $H_0: p_1 = p_2$\n- $H_1: p_1 > p_2$\n\nAs we just saw, the variance of the difference between the two sampling distributions is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}_1 - \\widehat{\\,p}_2] = \\dfrac{p_1(1-p_1)}{n_1} + \\dfrac{p_2(1-p_2)}{n_2}\n$$\n\nNow, under the null hypothesis, the two proportions are equal. Thus, assuming that $H_0$ is true, we can set\n\n$$\np_1 = p_2 = p\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Var} [\\widehat{p}_{1} - \\widehat{p}_{2}] &= (p(1 - p))/(n_{1}) + (p(1 - p))/(n_{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Var} [\\widehat{p}_{1} - \\widehat{p}_{2}] &= p(1 - p)(\\frac{1}{n_{1}} + \\frac{1}{n_{2}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the two proportions are equal under the null hypothesis, we can use a **pooled estimate** $\\overline{p}$ of the shared proportion $p$ to form a point estimate.\n\n$$\n\\overline{p} = \\dfrac{x_1+x_2}{n_1+n_2} = \\dfrac{n_1\\widehat{\\,p}_1+n_2\\widehat{\\,p}_2}{n_1+n_2}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ give the number of sample elements from the first and second samples, respectively, with the characteristic.\n\nThus, our estimate for the variance is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}_1 - \\widehat{\\,p}_2] =\\overline{p}(1-\\overline{p})\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n_1} + \\dfrac{1}{n_2}\\right)\n$$\n\nThis is similar to the pooled variance estimate we saw in a previous lesson.\n\nAs a result, we can use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {(\\widehat{\\,p}_1 - \\widehat{\\,p}_2) - (p_1-p_2)} {\\sqrt{\\overline{p}(1-\\overline{p}) \\left(\\dfrac{1}{n_1}+\\dfrac{1}{n_2} \\right)} \\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- A pooled variance estimate is preferable to an unpooled estimate when the null hypothesis assumes $p_1 = p_2$. It can be shown that the standard error of the pooled estimate is lower than the unpooled one, making our hypothesis test more reliable.\n- On the other hand, suppose we have the following null hypothesis:\n$H_0: p_1 - p_2 = k \\neq 0$.\nIn this case, the variances cannot be assumed equal, and we must use an unpooled estimate for the variance, i.e.,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}_1 - \\widehat{\\,p}_2] = \\dfrac{p_1(1-p_1)}{n_1} + \\dfrac{p_2(1-p_2)}{n_2}.\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll only consider null hypotheses of the form $p_1 = p_2$." :tutorial/id #uuid "388c4ec8-6df7-5308-9eba-d8575c965a0f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10795 :tutorial/title "Pooled vs. Unpooled Estimates of Proportion"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173205 :tutorial/content "Suppose a particle $P$ moves along a circle of radius $R$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \n\n![](images/45/4564f4c4fcd8068f3bf1645a310afab7fcdc2f82fec6966cb220558c1e93639a.png)\n\n    \n\nNote the following:\n\n- The **position vector** $\\mathbf r$ extends from the origin to $P$ in the *radial* direction, while\n\n- the **velocity vector** $\\mathbf v$ points in the direction of the particle's motion, *tangential* to the circle.\n\nFirst, let's assume that $P$ is at the point $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can easily derive a convenient formula relating $\\mathbf v$ and $\\omega$ by differentiating $\\mathbf r(t){:}$\n\nDifferentiating once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & ={r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={(R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j))}^{′} \\\\  & =R\\omega (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy computing the magnitude of this vector, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v|{|}^{2} & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t)^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t)}^{2}) \\\\  & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\omega t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\omega t) \\\\  & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v || = R|\\omega|.\n$$\n\nNote that while the magnitude of the velocity vector (i.e., the speed) is constant, its direction is continuously changing, which means that an object in uniform circular motion also experiences *acceleration* (more on this shortly)." :tutorial/id #uuid "04a3ea53-9923-588e-b205-6980447b9231" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5805 :tutorial/title "Linear vs. Angular Velocity"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173293 :tutorial/content "We can use proof by contradiction to demonstrate the non-divisibility of certain expressions.\n\nFor example, let's prove that for any $n\\in\\mathbb Z$,\n\n$$\n6 \\not\\mid (3n+1)\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\n\\phantom{\\lnot}P: \\quad \\forall n\\in \\mathbb Z,\\: 6 \\not\\mid (3n+1)\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true by contradiction, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P: \\quad \\exists n\\in \\mathbb Z,\\: 6 \\mid (3n+1)\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> *Assume, for a contradiction, that $6 \\mid (3n+1)$. Then, $3n+1=6k$, where $k$ is an integer.*\n\nSince the problem relates to divisibility by $6$, let's write the equation\n\n$$\n3n+1=6k\n$$\n\nin terms of congruence modulo $6$.\n\n> *Therefore, we have the following chain of equivalences:*\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+1=6k\\hspace{1em} & ⇔\\hspace{1em}3n=6k-1 \\\\ \\hspace{1em} & ⇔\\hspace{1em}3n≡6k-1\\hspace{1em} &  & (\\text{mod }6) \\\\ \\hspace{1em} & ⇔\\hspace{1em}3n≡0\\cdot k-1\\hspace{1em} &  & (\\text{mod }6) \\\\ \\hspace{1em} & ⇔\\hspace{1em}3n≡-1\\hspace{1em} &  & (\\text{mod }6) \\\\ \\hspace{1em} & ⇔\\hspace{1em}3n≡-1+6\\hspace{1em} &  & (\\text{mod }6) \\\\ \\hspace{1em} & ⇔\\hspace{1em}3n≡5\\hspace{1em} &  & (\\text{mod }6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the residue of $3n$ modulo $6{:}$\n\n> *Hence, we have that $3n \\textrm{mod} 6 = 5$.*\n\nNow, let's calculate all possible residues of $3n$ modulo $6$. To do that, we substitute\n\n$$\nn=0,1,\\ldots,5\n$$\n\ninto $3n$ and compute the residues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 \\cdot 0\\bmod 6 &= 0 \\\\\n3 \\cdot 1\\bmod 6 &= 3 \\\\\n3 \\cdot 2\\bmod 6 &= 0 \\\\\n3 \\cdot 3\\bmod 6 &= 3 \\\\\n3 \\cdot 4\\bmod 6 &= 0 \\\\\n3 \\cdot 5\\bmod 6 &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that there are only two residues possible. So, we proceed as follows:\n\n> *However, substituting $n=0,1,\\ldots,5$ into the expression $3n$, we obtain that*\n> $3n \\textrm{mod} 6 \\in \\{0, 3\\}$.\n> *But this is a contradiction since $3n \\textrm{mod} 6 = 5$ by assumption.*\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> *Therefore, we conclude that $6 \\not\\mid (3n+1)$.*\n\nNow that we've figured out the details, let's state the full proof." :tutorial/id #uuid "82bebf08-8ca4-5a8b-b916-204ff964f843" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9335 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175694 :question/id #uuid "a8cc8b8f-463b-5734-a33d-e915159cba32" :question/math-academy-id "e-11003" :question/problem "If $\\cos x =- \\dfrac {1}{3},$ find the value of $2\\cos( x + 4\\pi)+3.$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y = \\cos x$ is $2\\pi.$ Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = \\cos{(x+2n\\pi)} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos ⁡x & =\\cos ⁡(x+2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =\\cos ⁡(x+4\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\cos{x} =- \\dfrac {1}{3},$ then $\\cos{(x+4\\pi)} = - \\dfrac {1}{3}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cos ⁡(x+4\\pi )+3 & =2\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175696 :question/id #uuid "f4649856-d777-5468-a7f4-df50479e36d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11005" :question/problem "If $\\tan\\left( x\\right) = -2$ for some value of $x,$ then find the value of $\\tan\\left( x-2\\pi\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\tan x$ is $\\pi.$ Therefore, for any integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\tan (x+n\\pi).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡x & =\\tan ⁡(x+(-2)\\cdot \\pi )=\\tan ⁡(x-2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\tan\\left( x\\right) = -2,$ we get that $\\tan\\left( x-2\\pi\\right) = -2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175698 :question/id #uuid "f76b3e8f-5580-5cb0-8d6c-85711c446602" :question/math-academy-id "e-11007" :question/problem "If $\\sec x =2 \\sqrt 2$ then what is $\\sec(x+8\\pi)?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y = \\sec x$ is $2\\pi.$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} = \\sec{(x+2n\\pi)} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sec ⁡x & =\\sec ⁡(x+2\\cdot 4\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =\\sec ⁡(x+8\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\sec{x} = 2\\sqrt 2,$ then $\\sec{(x+8\\pi)} = 2\\sqrt 2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175702 :question/id #uuid "d4ebf4f8-d45a-5657-9331-1a6867110288" :question/math-academy-id "e-11011" :question/problem "If $\\cot x = 1$ for some $x,$ then find the value of $\\cot(x+5\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y = \\cot x$ is $\\pi.$ Therefore, for any integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cot{x} = \\cot{(x+n\\pi)} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cot ⁡x & =\\cot ⁡(x+5\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =\\cot ⁡(x+5\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\cot{x} = 1,$ then $\\cot{(x+5\\pi)} = 1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175703 :question/id #uuid "08253dde-510a-5451-ba5c-64d42d647db2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11012" :question/problem "If $\\cos x = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4},$ then find the value of $\\cos (-x-6\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\cos{x}.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\cos{x}$ is $2\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\cos{x}$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos(-x) = \\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\cos{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos ⁡(-x-6\\pi ) & =\\cos ⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\cos ⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\cos{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos ⁡(-x) & =\\cos ⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\cos (-x-6\\pi)= \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175704 :question/id #uuid "0b146642-914c-52ce-9939-86752e95dcd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11013" :question/problem "If $\\tan x = -\\dfrac{2}{3},$ find the value of $\\tan (-x+7\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan{x}.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\tan{x}$ is $\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\tan{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan(-x) = -\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\tan{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x+7\\pi ) & =\\tan ⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 7) \\\\  & =\\tan ⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\tan{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x) & =-\\tan ⁡x \\\\  & =-\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x+7\\pi)=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175705 :question/id #uuid "6bd67fa4-16b5-5505-bfc5-35a00f54e980" :question/math-academy-id "e-11014" :question/problem "If $\\sec{x} = -\\dfrac{11}{6},$ then $\\sec(-x - 14\\pi) =$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y = \\sec{x}.$\n\n![](images/fb/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\sec{x}$ is $2\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\sec{x}$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\sec(-x) = \\sec{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\sec{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sec ⁡(-x-14\\pi ) & =\\sec ⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 7) \\\\  & =\\sec ⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\sec{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sec ⁡(-x) & =\\sec ⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sec(-x - 14\\pi) = -\\dfrac{11}{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175707 :question/id #uuid "2c8233f9-6749-5244-a209-6bf41b64388f" :question/math-academy-id "e-11016" :question/problem "If $\\cot x = 2,$ then what is the value of $\\cot (-x+7\\pi)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\cot{x}.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\cot{x}$ is $\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\cot{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cot(-x) = -\\cot{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\cot{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cot ⁡(-x+7\\pi ) & =\\cot ⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 7) \\\\  & =\\cot ⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\cot{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cot ⁡(-x) & =-\\cot ⁡x \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\cot (-x+7\\pi)=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175713 :question/id #uuid "45ec3318-742d-582c-bdf1-d9f35f0bd99c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12561" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta),$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =  \\big\\langle \\text{_____} , \\: r^2, \\: 2 r \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\ny=4{x^2}+\\dfrac{z^2}{4}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx =\\dfrac1{2}X, \\qquad\ny = Z, \\qquad\nz = 2Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $\\mathbf{f}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx =\\dfrac1{2}X, \\qquad\ny = Z, \\qquad\nz = 2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get \n\n$$\nZ = X^2 + Y^2.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\nY = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nZ = z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}X,Z,2Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r \\in [0,\\infty)$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} \\dfrac1{2} r \\cos\\theta}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175743 :question/id #uuid "ef75201e-1a29-5f84-90fa-b5f13ee6092d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1496" :question/problem "To produce a certain brand of t-shirts, a manufacturing company bases the price of each unit on the price function $p(x) = 50-0.1x$, where $x$ represents the number of units produced and sold per month. Each unit costs $\\$20$ to make, and the company’s fixed costs are $\\$2\\,000$ per month. Calculate the profit function $P(x)$." :question/worked-solution "We know that the profit function is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=R(x)-C(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the number of units, $C(x)$ is the cost function and $R(x)$ is the revenue function.\n\nWe're given the price function $p(x)$, so the revenue function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(x) &= (number of units) \\cdot (price per unit) \\\\\n&= x \\cdot p(x) \\\\\n&= x(50 - 0.1x) \\\\\n&= 50x - 0.1x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that the fixed costs are $\\$2\\,000$ per month, and each unit costs $\\$20$ to make. Therefore, the cost function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(x) &= (fixed \\cos t) + (number of units) \\cdot (\\cos t per unit) \\\\\n&= 2000 + x \\cdot 20 \\\\\n&= 2000 + 20x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now calculate the profit function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\n&= (50x - 0.1x^{2}) - (2000 + 20x) \\\\\n&= 50x - 0.1x^{2} - 2000 - 20x \\\\\n&=-0.1x^{2} + 30x - 2000\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175810 :question/id #uuid "fb50c5bc-799c-5762-9c4f-d661cc91e836" :question/math-academy-id "e-8706" :question/problem "Given that the moment-generating function (MGF) of the random variable $X$ is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle M(t) = \\dfrac{1}{(1-2t)^2}, \\quad t < \\dfrac 12,\n$$\n\ncompute $\\textrm{Var}[X]$." :question/worked-solution "We use the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Var} [X] &= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&= M^{″}(0) - [M^{′}(0)]^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express $M(t)$ in a form that is easy to differentiate:\n\n$$\nM(t) = (1-2t)^{-2}\n$$\n\nComputing derivatives of $M(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM^{′}(t) &= (4)/((1 - 2t)^{3}) \\\\\nM^{″}(t) &= (24)/((1 - 2t)^{4})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM^{′}(0) &= 4 \\\\\nM^{″}(0) &= 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Var} [X] &= M^{″}(0) - [M^{′}(0)]^{2} \\\\\n&= 24 - 4^{2} \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175820 :question/id #uuid "616e03ca-b0fc-53f1-baf0-8034d7731891" :question/math-academy-id "e-8885" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 4\\sin{t}, \\: z \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y \\rangle = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 4\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\frac{16{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}2\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}4\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\cos{t}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175821 :question/id #uuid "6745a851-eaa4-50a0-b76a-3d847284c586" :question/math-academy-id "e-8886" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi),$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) =  \\big\\langle 3\\cosh{\\phi} \\cos\\theta, \\: 4\\sinh{\\phi}, \\: \\text{______} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{9}-\\dfrac{y^2}{16}+z^2=1.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx = 3X, \\qquad\ny = 4Z, \\qquad\nz = Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $\\mathbf{f}.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $r^2-Z^2 = k^2$ can be parametrized as $\\langle r, Z\\rangle = \\langle k\\cosh\\phi, k\\sinh \\phi\\rangle$* for $\\phi\\in \\mathbb R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx = 3X, \\qquad\ny = 4Z, \\qquad\nz = Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nX^2 + Y^2 - Z^2 = 1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\nY = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nZ = Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola $r^2 - Z^2 = 1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta$ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n\\langle r, Z \\rangle = \\langle \\cosh{\\phi}, \\: \\sinh{\\phi} \\rangle,\n\\qquad \\phi \\in \\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3X,4Z,Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4Z,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in \\mathbb R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} \\cosh{\\phi} \\sin\\theta }.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175823 :question/id #uuid "3c0c59e2-ceaa-54ab-bb56-3cb6daadb5f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8889" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,y) = \\big \\langle 4\\tan{t}, \\, y, \\, \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad t\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,y)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\dfrac{z^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{16} = 1$ for $z \\geq 2.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola $\\dfrac{z^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{16} = 1$ for $z \\geq 2$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x , z \\rangle = \\big \\langle 4\\tan{t}, \\, 2\\sec{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\n\\qquad t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{\\sec }^{2}⁡t}{4}-\\frac{16{\\tan }^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t-{\\tan }^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{tan}t,\\hspace{0.167em}y,\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}2\\operatorname{sec}t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$ and $y \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\sec{t}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175824 :question/id #uuid "2b8d5d04-51f1-5394-931a-384d88692ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-8890" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi),$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) =  \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 3\\sinh{\\phi} \\sin\\theta, \\: 3\\cosh{\\phi} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = -9.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $\\mathbf{f}.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $z^2-r^2 = k^2$ can be parametrized as $\\langle z,r\\rangle = \\langle k\\cosh\\phi, k\\sinh \\phi\\rangle$* for $\\phi\\in \\mathbb R.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\n\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \n\nz = z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ {z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola $z^2 - r^2 = 9$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta$ with the positive $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/4e/4e7b508432f12b8dc98e37ae2786d220eb97bdc58fe5a29c5f7728cac46471f0.png)\n\nUsing the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n\\langle z, r \\rangle = \\langle 3\\cosh{\\phi}, \\: 3\\sinh{\\phi} \\rangle,\n\\qquad \\phi \\in \\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in \\mathbb R.$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}3\\sinh{\\phi} \\cos\\theta} .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175864 :question/id #uuid "dcb243ce-1363-560e-a208-59ef9749bafa" :question/math-academy-id "e-9237" :question/problem "Given that the moment-generating function of the random variable $X$ is given by\n$M(t) =\\dfrac{4}{5} + \\dfrac{1}{5} e^t ,$\n\ncompute $\\textrm{SD}[X]$." :question/worked-solution "We use the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSD[X] &= \\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var} [X]} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{M^{″}(0) - [M^{′}(0)]^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing derivatives of $M(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM^{′}(t) &= \\frac{1}{5}e^{t} \\\\\nM^{″}(t) &= \\frac{1}{5}e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM^{′}(0) &= \\frac{1}{5} \\\\\nM^{″}(0) &= \\frac{1}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSD[X] &= \\sqrt{M^{″}(0) - [M^{′}(0)]^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{5} - \\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{25}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66f7a2b8-23c9-5110-8e24-f664a2447594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364662} {:answer/id #uuid "8b83da41-3c56-5bc4-85a9-468d168ca70b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{csc}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364663} {:answer/id #uuid "775f11c6-37a0-5ac7-b461-a5d7115f3767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364664} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1b2546-f11f-5044-a149-48fa7c0b8940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364665} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff668a4-f940-589a-8790-3b71b79abc55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364666}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364665} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a20c6d2-699b-56bb-8123-fa833c0c85c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6c9aca83-9ce8-55b9-8ced-bafcb2d3c448" :question/math-academy-id "q-103998" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z)=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{_____},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩,\\hspace{2em}t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 3.$ What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 3$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-(3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =9({\\sec }^{2}⁡t-{\\tan }^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sec}t,\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}3\\operatorname{tan}t},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}3\\operatorname{tan}t}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c2c186-ead9-52d5-95f7-1097013aebd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238127} {:answer/id #uuid "0cdf707d-db9b-5eae-871b-a8456de68dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238170} {:answer/id #uuid "0eac8810-412a-52ba-b2f1-89e7458955e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238182} {:answer/id #uuid "c68450c5-5961-53e7-a146-fb3f4a33acfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239801} {:answer/id #uuid "cebd5953-2e51-5082-b045-f925d625b9e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239867}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238170} :answer-field/id #uuid "290ba1ed-ead1-5126-9578-728034b643df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "736a4dfd-f4fe-5ef5-9a6f-9cd95b3ae752" :question/math-academy-id "q-104007" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=16.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}=16$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,z⟩=⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b40a20f-8038-544c-8b4e-15eaab7d992c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238283} {:answer/id #uuid "ae88c49b-269f-5572-bf80-6533639b24c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239573} {:answer/id #uuid "b327c39b-cbd4-579e-b0e6-3b7a10481d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239617} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3d3b30-eefe-5763-9f2f-479762cea5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240125} {:answer/id #uuid "ed84fdd3-4e1d-524c-9a2b-6b53ffca1d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240145}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239617} :answer-field/id #uuid "8757e807-550e-56b2-8622-fab92b08435a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5c824c2a-5ae5-5a01-bf81-c7a30b4783b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-104008" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,Z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,Z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3X,Z,2Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,2(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b20ca503-21db-50db-bfc8-c57756c67580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\sinh ϕ\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173465} {:answer/id #uuid "b568c032-9099-5aff-838b-dba10fd1e514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\cosh ϕ\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173466} {:answer/id #uuid "bce6c511-c918-5399-ad74-91cab3bd1268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sinh ϕ\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173467} {:answer/id #uuid "120c93b3-50e8-52bf-a4f1-ce422b628666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\cosh ϕ\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173468} {:answer/id #uuid "63e2bba7-d273-54d8-b407-eb1ba09811c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sinh ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173469}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cdb49437-7cdc-508c-9e66-1fbf571f0a43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173464} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e74aaf41-4899-56ca-88d4-0fd63e225d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369005} {:answer/id #uuid "71dbc8de-0ad8-5cf8-8d5e-bcbaf61d1be2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369006} {:answer/id #uuid "5ecbe3b3-4c2b-532f-844b-120ea9bc5269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369007} {:answer/id #uuid "4d691f3a-68c2-57c5-992c-335b4be89b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369008} {:answer/id #uuid "0c62fc99-88c6-5d8f-8103-76d2ee581884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369009}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369007} :answer-field/id #uuid "722fe973-8403-5bb6-b006-31e3971ebd91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8fb6d030-7dfd-5cce-9b5b-d200f2dc6225" :question/math-academy-id "q-104317" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {Z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨Z,r⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3X,Z,2Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,2(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a749ded0-2fb8-5d57-a65d-979e2ef8edd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\sin x\\sqrt{1 + \\cos^{2} x}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173507} {:answer/id #uuid "f402d949-5cd4-56b1-bd76-5a119f45abc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}x\\cos^{2} xdx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173508} {:answer/id #uuid "0538f271-9c1d-52ab-af5c-03d48163d2c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\sin x\\sqrt{1 + \\sin^{2} x}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173509} {:answer/id #uuid "077afecb-b76b-5b22-822e-052d0c159b2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}x\\sin^{2} xdx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173510} {:answer/id #uuid "630b4aa9-ffa2-5990-95cf-0ed4bfbf7ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}x\\sqrt{1 + \\cos^{2} x}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173511}] :answer-field/id #uuid "132765ce-c248-5a65-a076-6cabdd57f346" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173506} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd7cbc0c-1ac9-5d2c-8c1e-c90064a0020d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386096} {:answer/id #uuid "ce12d4d4-d1f3-5db5-a2e1-487a2dbee039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386097} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2323ab-4264-53da-ac17-09aa02263099" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x{sin}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386098} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3b5d27-917f-51f1-9569-ad74dbcaf182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x{cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386099} {:answer/id #uuid "6c3afdc6-7909-5577-8288-788734f3eb0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386100}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386097} :answer-field/id #uuid "036154be-8ded-5df0-8368-91d401b8a252" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2ddd5046-3b26-5239-932f-f9bb69f6472e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104518" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\sqrt{1+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7bc11c-66f0-516c-be38-a48045d964f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238162} {:answer/id #uuid "3e74bf5b-a988-54bf-b10b-2eefe28915c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238601} {:answer/id #uuid "501240ae-b8a0-5acb-a0c9-930075c91b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238746} {:answer/id #uuid "bff3c513-d899-5100-8a5d-0ae0cc06c64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(\\pi \\sqrt{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239723} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f99997-a927-59f4-93d4-69604382fa20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240247} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ca857f1-1bc6-513d-8d2d-903414af839c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "40e44b1f-1930-5622-81b2-291ea09677cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-106034" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z$ whose projection onto the $xz$-plane is bounded by $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and the $x$-axis for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂y}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂y}{∂z}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z,\\,(x,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/be/be5dc3bf3ed4b6b7bee06d583b69e40a3922edede1e61a6f0b68d2ce0767f92a.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂y}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂y}{∂z}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\sqrt{2+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\sqrt{2+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=2+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x,$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\\hline x & 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\hline u & 3 & 2 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\sqrt{2+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{3}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({3}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e1f42a9-2410-5d00-a50f-5c6a17763473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416994} {:answer/id #uuid "31f893f9-5f3d-577a-921d-33855e0c2e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416995} {:answer/id #uuid "4d46be50-d6db-513b-9ef7-cbc8edd3addb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416997} {:answer/id #uuid "bc499839-44bb-51b3-bb95-1413004e1a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417009} {:answer/id #uuid "ce04ef35-1259-5b77-8dbd-404063317f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417010}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416995} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4a2d2b-0aea-53f7-a0ec-6d74774ae6df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8231d418-c039-5ff1-a299-022f1329d9d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-108613" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det (A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(3)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9deac94-910d-5b63-9e57-f2b87ce35388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386296} {:answer/id #uuid "1ed1599f-3aad-5db6-88b0-67ffb6cefac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386297} {:answer/id #uuid "a98ce391-9389-52eb-9e86-0f61cb9645c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386298} {:answer/id #uuid "c1239255-deea-5186-b902-2c62a0e4f9b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386299} {:answer/id #uuid "77e95aa7-899b-565b-8665-45a05cc48157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386300}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386300} :answer-field/id #uuid "1868ed6f-a38d-5a16-93a8-ed4612cf63a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7a50741c-c4f5-5a02-bb86-14c9da06d5cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-108719" :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det (A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}6-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )(-9-\\lambda )-(5)(-10) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-4)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3febb2be-1e0e-57f4-b6ed-5294431dbf2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241754} {:answer/id #uuid "6a31fea1-8bb6-594b-a263-ac4249c16046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242134} {:answer/id #uuid "c04dbc75-5a51-5999-b545-da162bad5ab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242863} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8e4b9c-6a6f-5ce3-bc4a-d8fa4a8737b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242997} {:answer/id #uuid "dc417db0-b01e-5493-a115-97232876670e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241754} :answer-field/id #uuid "023d7f78-9f38-5655-93fe-685d7af1bae5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8fce4ae0-ab7d-5b76-858c-c6d19c621a6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109094" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det (A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-8-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-8-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-5\\cdot (-5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-3)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1441c511-da12-53ae-8f9f-c5ea647d372c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244500} {:answer/id #uuid "7c7e1b6c-2ab7-59c0-9f5a-5f9c53675cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245404} {:answer/id #uuid "9d3ea264-8cea-5f04-ac0a-1a432e019399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245712} {:answer/id #uuid "de4be96b-1fd2-5c0e-9a1e-5b6fbb0e9166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246281} {:answer/id #uuid "ef6b3d93-1eda-5a13-827c-9e25c91d6216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246432}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246432} :answer-field/id #uuid "4da5d5fa-ba17-5504-b9bf-88fe68f53ded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "036242ed-9cf3-5798-a052-46558ecda9fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-112453" :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det (A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-(-2)(1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +42 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -7)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(7)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(6)=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8d64cd9-814d-58fb-80c5-981e49a732cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173963} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c01190-e27e-5616-be41-d8df6d91f26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173964} {:answer/id #uuid "46687dff-418e-5ccd-bcf3-3c8457c08c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173965} {:answer/id #uuid "502155f4-21a9-5061-a7ac-09aaa8e0c94e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173966} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4e4095-203a-53cd-a484-8e188f50c144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173967}] :answer-field/id #uuid "14d7b6b6-6200-5232-b27e-479dfbf68345" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173962}] :question/id #uuid "7dc2d525-8556-5b6e-b9bd-3a8a6a90f312" :question/math-academy-id "q-112929" :question/problem "Given that the moment-generating function of the random variable $X$ is given by\n$M(t) = \\frac{1}{2}(e^{-t} + e^{t})$,\n\ncompute $\\operatorname{Var} [X]$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95bf4225-7f2b-533b-b53e-c9b1fee93f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267480} {:answer/id #uuid "9832c278-c6fe-5a16-af70-2a5d2cbee4ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267511} {:answer/id #uuid "ad699b9a-4479-5d4d-a163-8f41699cbfeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267711} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ef3bf2-09ff-56a5-a1d9-4ee8d61fd861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268141} {:answer/id #uuid "e58c1d7f-af4f-5dd4-b8bb-d3a27371d68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268174} :answer-field/id #uuid "caecf20f-30a6-5b32-b66b-f7b3637a7847" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "195eeaaa-ad0f-5b04-bea4-14b7076ff8c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-136545" :question/problem "If $\\cos ⁡a=-\\frac{1}{3},$ then $\\cos ⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\cos ⁡x.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $\\cos ⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos ⁡(-x)=\\cos ⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos ⁡(-a) & =\\cos ⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19df7d47-b87b-5e46-8360-7533c525ec2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266358} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2908b8-2ead-5a77-be9f-804bc07bb7fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266672} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccc1b1b-9a04-5bb8-a591-9f5cc4c7efc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267102} {:answer/id #uuid "8b14d04b-7661-55d9-a6d2-74ec1e93a05a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267392} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bfc5ef-972c-5d0d-9011-6abdfdf11b45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267642}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266358} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6737d96-a059-592e-9cfb-df4b5f79acd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ccd7ae50-1c4e-5bcc-8b8b-d405f5154f0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-136548" :question/problem "If $\\tan ⁡a=-2,$ then $\\tan ⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan ⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $\\tan ⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan ⁡(-x)=-\\tan ⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-a) & =-\\tan ⁡a \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "588a98ae-e895-5d1f-a060-70be67c693e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282441} {:answer/id #uuid "62681fc8-d5c6-577b-9bf9-1705571f5024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{r}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282533} {:answer/id #uuid "9374a3a2-89e0-58eb-b3c6-bb2f138c13cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282958} {:answer/id #uuid "ac85d646-d77e-5c6e-95e3-7177f30031d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283182} {:answer/id #uuid "e9581f9e-a97c-52be-b0a7-67333108e4ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{r}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283725}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282441} :answer-field/id #uuid "618a3631-67a9-5cd6-b615-80bb8566b821" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "857271c4-29f7-50af-9c53-ede1aa45db8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-164541" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=2X,\\,z=Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=2X,\\,z=Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get \n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,2X,Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "4f4c978c-c420-5a82-91c5-679c8911965b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174815}] :question/id #uuid "e73d6d90-037d-5077-a406-b9e384470ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-256204" :question/problem "Find the moment-generating function $M(t)$ of $X∼\\operatorname{Exp} (4)$ for $t < 4$.$M(t) =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "3feb3830-34e6-5da4-86fd-fae250e69e41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174821}] :question/id #uuid "c62b81e2-37b2-546a-acf9-82c41dac9b44" :question/math-academy-id "q-256357" :question/problem "Find the moment-generating function $M(t)$ of $X∼\\operatorname{Exp} (1)$ for $t < 1$.$M(t) =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ba1a255-23c5-558c-890e-352eda666203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438028} {:answer/id #uuid "81040fec-7087-5a36-951b-b14d732d3182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438029}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438028} :answer-field/id #uuid "d005fdab-8b78-5385-b8aa-903a81e93357" :answer-field/key "The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi .$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439043} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb0045bc-f2f1-542d-bb18-f3e3a32d2249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438030} {:answer/id #uuid "72151a10-5577-5c23-b443-f401e6df03cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438031}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438031} :answer-field/id #uuid "b788b23d-3122-5950-affc-e24e292331da" :answer-field/key "The function returns only positive values on the intervals $(\\pi n,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n)\\,.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439044} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f160f821-e2d0-550e-97fc-505a5402176a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438032} {:answer/id #uuid "6d124318-fd4c-51c0-8d63-f26f3899820c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438033}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438032} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba780c82-a764-5e2e-ac73-af96e4c3fadd" :answer-field/key "The function has zeros at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ac898e3a-bbc1-536e-8415-2d3fbc798e28" :question/math-academy-id "q-27638" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\tan ⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\tan ⁡x.$\n\n![](images/32/326418398ce1a33f03a21f3bdb58107856f4ccb72241429f1604486281e60822.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi .$” is false. The function has an asymptote at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and the period is $\\pi .$ Therefore, the asymptotes occur at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement “The function returns only positive values on the intervals $(\\pi n,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n)\\,.$” is true. The function returns only positive values on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and the period is $\\pi .$ Therefore, the function is positive on $(n\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )\\,.$\n\n- Statement “The function has zeros at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$” is false. The zeros occur at $x=0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ which can be written as $x=n\\pi .$\n\nSo, the correct answer is “The function returns only positive values on the intervals $(\\pi n,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n)\\,.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c63e26d-074e-5c36-8b58-33b7f0f8843d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324084} {:answer/id #uuid "781d1a4b-1bc8-581a-92ed-92f43c30d4bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324968} {:answer/id #uuid "7b971c04-f79d-51cf-bd5b-9f7c7631cab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325011} {:answer/id #uuid "acc11610-a434-5194-92fd-1427c352ec10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325438} {:answer/id #uuid "f04a03aa-bf67-5848-a19c-45ba01434156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326093} :answer-field/id #uuid "218338cd-5fd4-5bce-b4d9-5812bff2cbd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b212f7d8-65d4-5b14-984d-a9ab26791d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-38480" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $\\det (A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-6-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-4)(5) & =0 \\\\ -12+6\\lambda -2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{{4}^{2}-4(1)(8)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+2\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4-2\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-4-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(4-2\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+2\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-1-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-1-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2-2\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(2)(2)}{(-2-\\text{i})(-2+\\text{i})}=\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6fd58e8-aaed-556d-888a-e6413102b169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460041} {:answer/id #uuid "81922915-68b9-5c54-8bee-784c6d39c2d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460042}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460041} :answer-field/id #uuid "115e9b49-91a9-5c59-b043-b405028a08eb" :answer-field/key "$(0,5]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460887} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "888077d5-4d82-5b5b-b8a4-3a038fc1dc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460043} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2c41fb-497d-5e52-8591-3ab354912d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460044}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460044} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a6e85c2-8629-572f-8e6a-e48e9eb0fb66" :answer-field/key "$[1,5]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460888} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3533d2b7-ddae-5ec8-a6cc-5bc6ad0e2a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460045} {:answer/id #uuid "68144902-7f34-51b9-8ac2-be7a5b881945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460046}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460045} :answer-field/id #uuid "f62c0f8c-c76e-5f5e-a218-b4bcdc54f373" :answer-field/key "$[0,5]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5ee96606-8ba8-569e-8f90-21918ad24c63" :question/math-academy-id "q-47787" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y={\\log }_{2}⁡x?$ \n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,5]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,5]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,5].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[0,5]$ because this interval contains points where the function is not defined. In particular, it is not defined at $x=0.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[1,5]$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "913d488a-1cb5-5c9d-b418-a303a532d697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175673} {:answer/id #uuid "e022a1e0-2917-515a-a4da-8f85b48e486e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175674} {:answer/id #uuid "0129d36a-9d08-5a39-a6a4-1a26ef49ec94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175675} {:answer/id #uuid "17db9076-8829-58ae-899b-d081b09885de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175676} {:answer/id #uuid "5c03dc9b-946a-578a-a915-fdb3060af88d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175677}] :answer-field/id #uuid "db3f96f3-05f7-508f-9a19-014e81299daf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175672}] :question/id #uuid "df1d2b6e-daa9-5b69-834e-225f97396034" :question/math-academy-id "q-98296" :question/problem "Given that the moment-generating function of the random variable $X$ is given by\n$M(t) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - 2t}}$,\n\ncompute $\\operatorname{Var} [X]$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176990 :tutorial/content "![](images/86/867722106ffd8445b62a98ab43197ac2fba646faff95017ef7e92c254a97fb6e.png)\n\n  \n\nThe surface $S$ shown above consists of the part of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ that lies under the plane $z=9.$ Let's compute the surface area of $S.$\n\nThe plane intersects the paraboloid when $z=9.$ Thus, the curve that describes the intersection points is\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = 9,\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $3$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nTherefore, our surface can be described by the equations\n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2, \\quad (x,y) \\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y): \\, x^2 + y^2 \\le 9\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the surface area of $S,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions into our surface area formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by changing the variables to polar coordinates $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta),$ we have $\\textrm dA = r\\,\\textrm d r\\, \\textrm d \\theta.$ Thus, our integration region becomes\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta): \\, 0\\leq r\\leq 3,\\:0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+4(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}\\,}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,d\\theta \\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}\\,}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}\\,}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi {[\\frac{1}{12}{(1+4{r}^{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{(1+4{r}^{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(37\\sqrt{37}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the area of our surface is \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}(37\\sqrt{37} -1).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1f3795a6-1971-55b3-9109-8565cd3830a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5344 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177007 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable. We know that the variance of $X$ can be expressed in terms of the raw moments $\\textrm E[X]$ and $\\textrm E[X^2]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2\n$$\n\nLet $M(t)$ be the moment-generating function of $X$. Since\n\n$$\n\\textrm E [X] = M'(0), \\qquad \\textrm E [X^2] = M''(0)\n$$\n\nwe can express the variance of $X$ in terms of derivatives of the MGF evaluated at zero:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] =M''(0) - [M'(0)]^2\n$$\n\nFor example, let the random variable $X$ have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x) = {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} , & x = 0 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} , & x = 1\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThen, the MGF is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM(t) &= f(0)e^{0t} + f(1)e^{t} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the first derivative and the second derivative of MGF, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}, \\\\\nM^{″}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(M^{′}(t)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{3}e^{t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Var} [X] &= M^{″}(0) - (M^{′}(0))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} - \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "02f648cc-8ecf-5c4f-bfe9-eb5c570473db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5530 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177024 :tutorial/content "We can construct a cost function $C(x)$ for a business using the following equation:\n\n$$\nC(x) = (\\text{fixed cost}) + (\\textrm{number of units})\\cdot (\\textrm{cost per unit})\n$$\n\nLet's break this equation down a bit:\n\n- The **fixed cost** is the part of the cost function that does not change. Irrespective of how many units are produced, fixed costs are always the same. Fixed costs typically incorporate rent, utilities, communications, employee wages, taxes, etc.\n- The term $(\\textrm{number of units})\\cdot (\\textrm{cost per unit})$ represents the **variable cost.** This part of the cost function changes depending on how many units are produced.\n\nFor example, suppose that a company produces $x$ units of sweaters per month. Each unit costs $\\$49$ to make, and the company's fixed costs are $\\$4\\,500$ per month.\n\nWe can calculate the cost function $C(x)$ for this company as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(x) &= (fixed \\cos t) + (number of units) \\cdot (\\cos t per unit) \\\\\n&= 4500 + x \\cdot 49 \\\\\n&= 4500 + 49x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the cost of producing $50$ units, we substitute $x=50$ into $C(x)$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(50) &= 4500 + 49(50) \\\\\n&= 4500 + 2450 \\\\\n&= 6950\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it costs the company $\\$6\\,950$ to produce $50$ units." :tutorial/id #uuid "11fcd59d-eb78-5fcb-888b-8d6cb279db9c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5687 :tutorial/title "Cost Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177054 :tutorial/content "![](images/7a/7a1647aa543ef6b9c4c2004e358a91b8d034bd8d72cc53e72bf0dc69266366bc.png)\n\nTo find the horizontal tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives $y'(\\theta)$ and $x'(\\theta).$ \n\nDoing this using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta$ where $y'(\\theta)=0.$\n\nWriting down the equation $y'(\\theta) = 0$ and factoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1 & =0 \\\\ (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n2\\cos\\theta-1 = 0, \\qquad \\cos\\theta + 1= 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n2\\cos\\theta-1 = 0,  \\qquad 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta + 1=0, \\qquad 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\pi.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have three candidate solutions:\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\qquad \\theta_2 = \\pi, \\qquad \\theta_3 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether any candidates have $x'(\\theta) = 0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solutions into the expression for $x'(\\theta).$ If $x'(\\theta) = 0,$ further investigation is necessary.  \n\n- Substituting $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(1+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_2 = \\pi,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+1) \\\\  & =-0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we draw *no conclusion* regarding $\\theta_2 = \\pi.$\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ is a valid solution.\n\nWe now make the following conclusions: \n\n- We have horizontal tangents where $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}.$ These correspond to the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively.\n\n- This analysis gave no conclusion regarding the point where $\\theta = \\pi.$ We'll learn to deal with points like this in future lessons.\n\nFor the remainder of this lesson, we will not concern ourselves with the case where $y'(\\theta)$ and $x'(\\theta)$ are both zero. However, it's worth carrying out step 3 regardless when solving these problems, as this will help develop good habits that will come in handy later." :tutorial/id #uuid "c2158ee2-ecde-50ce-b68e-b23dde7e3b42" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6268 :tutorial/title "Finding Horizontal Tangents to Polar Curves"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177061 :tutorial/content "![](images/20/20b8d6a35a9925c0442830f0abd20c522f0e3a420d27e6239e43120edbfc55d3.png)\n\nTo find the vertical tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives $y'(\\theta)$ and $x'(\\theta).$ \n\nDoing this using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta$ where $x'(\\theta)=0{:}$\n\nWriting down the equation $x'(\\theta) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = 0, \\qquad 2\\cos\\theta+1= 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = 0,  \\qquad 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are \n\n$$\n\\theta = 0,\\pi.\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n2\\cos\\theta+1= 0, \\qquad 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have four candidate solutions:\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 =0, \\qquad \\theta_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}, \\qquad \\theta_3 = \\pi, \\qquad \\theta_2 = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether any candidates have $y'(\\theta) = 0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solutions into $y'(\\theta)$ and check that $y'(\\theta) \\neq 0.$ If $y'(\\theta) = 0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_1 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-1 \\\\  & =2+1-1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = 0$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) & =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_3 = \\pi,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2}-1-1 \\\\  & =2-1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we draw *no conclusion* regarding $\\theta_3 = \\pi.$\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_4 = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) & =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_4 = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ is a valid solution.\n\nWe now make the following conclusions: \n\n- We have vertical tangents where $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta = 0, \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.\n$$ These correspond to the points $P, Q,$ and $R,$ respectively.\n\n- Again, this analysis gave no conclusion regarding the point where $\\theta = \\pi.$ We'll learn to deal with points like this in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "afebb67f-3a1e-5c0a-95b1-9a5065f21f09" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6386 :tutorial/title "Finding Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177078 :tutorial/content "To find a parametrization of one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{25}-\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1\n$$\n\nfor $x \\geq 5,$ recall that the corresponding hyperbola in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y \\rangle = \\big\\langle 5 \\sec{t}, \\: 4 \\tan{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad t\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can check that this indeed parametrizes our base hyperbola by substituting the parametric equations into the equation of the base curve: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t-{\\tan }^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{sec}t,\\hspace{0.222em}4\\operatorname{tan}t,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b77aec62-7485-5124-b4df-403bfa5dd1f8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7854 :tutorial/title "Hyperbolic Cylinders"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177079 :tutorial/content "We can also parametrize elliptic paraboloids using cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\nFor example, let's parametrize the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\nz=\\dfrac{x^2}{9}+y^2.\n$$\n\nThe first step is to perform a substitution that transforms our paraboloid to one whose horizontal cross-sections (traces) are circles. To do this, we first define the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nx = 3X, \\qquad\ny = Y, \\qquad\nz = Z\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our paraboloid, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =\\frac{(3X)^{2}}{9}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that if $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\nY = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nZ = z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3X,Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the polar parametrization of our surface is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r, \\theta) =  \\big\\langle 3 r\\cos\\theta, \\: r\\sin\\theta, \\: r^2 \\big\\rangle, \\qquad r \\in [0,\\infty), \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$\n\nLet's now consider a case where the elliptic paraboloid has an axis of symmetry that's not parallel to the $z$-axis." :tutorial/id #uuid "10a22181-65aa-58a4-9036-60689da8b843" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7857 :tutorial/title "Parametrizations of Paraboloids in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177080 :tutorial/content "We often need cylindrical polar coordinates *and* hyperbolic functions to parametrize a hyperboloid.\n\nFor example, let's parametrize a hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\nx^2+y^2-z^2=9.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by transforming to cylindrical polar coordinates in the usual way:\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola $r^2 - z^2 = 9$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the vertical plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta$ with the positive $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/80/807e7c016733140d391ff69e0aa9695fe0927f4575607edc9b084eae5b7426a4.png)\n\n   \n\nUsing hyperbolic functions, we can parametrize this hyperbola as follows:\n\n$$\n\\langle r, z \\rangle = \\langle 3\\cosh{\\phi}, \\: 3\\sinh{\\phi} \\rangle,\n\\qquad \\phi \\in \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nTherefore, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following parametrization of our hyperbola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in \\mathbb R.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4b0402bd-31a4-5ae8-9b6b-45d2015cb5d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7858 :tutorial/title "Parametrizing Hyperboloids of One Sheet"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179436 :question/id #uuid "98ebb2b6-475b-53c5-acae-0b1b0f0778d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-12441" :question/problem "![](images/87/8797a50ddc0750a97190f96d8bc1cffe70c6ef9cc8e55a46f5a75d6b6702c81d.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \n= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{array} \\right]} \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]}\n+ \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$ \nDescribe the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf{T}$ is \n$$\nA = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179505 :question/id #uuid "453d4ec3-98f5-5db1-a046-a6b15c13049d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4097" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of $\\log_2,$ what value of $x$ satisfies the equation $2^{x+1}=3^x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take $\\log_2$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+1} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{x+1}) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\log_b \\left( b^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left(3^{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+1 = x \\log_2 \\left(3\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x{\\log }_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ x{\\log }_{2}⁡(3)-x & =1 \\\\ x({\\log }_{2}⁡(3)-1) & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{{\\log }_{2}⁡(3)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179526 :question/id #uuid "692dbdea-922d-568c-bc00-4972dd733ca9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6305" :question/problem "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = 2x+y^2$ along the quarter-circle $x^2+y^2=4,$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-2,0)$ in the second quadrant." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](images/d0/d0ae53dd5d0932cbf834a166e851c227f75cb546b591a9fd43930ec06f0e9ddf.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = 2x+y^2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179529 :question/id #uuid "1cadcaa9-876e-56cb-b8b3-17695827c4f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7035" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7a6e67435aab40d5bf3630c66f79241008357e1549b1c77b4c0bdfea2b453883.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\dfrac{y\\,\\mathrm{d}x - x\\,\\mathrm{d}y}{x^2+y^2},\n$$ where $C$ is a part of the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $\\left(0,2\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a parametrization for the given path is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & ={x}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{y}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dx-x\\,dy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {x}^{′}-x\\cdot {y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}},dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{-4{\\sin }^{2}⁡t-4{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}{4({\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{-4({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)}{4({\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)}\\,dt \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,dt \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179572 :question/id #uuid "3fe61e6a-ce68-5f85-98f3-e4af5a39de5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9438" :question/problem "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = x^2-1,$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-1 \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({\\cos }^{2}⁡t-1)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-1\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179573 :question/id #uuid "5ebfa0d1-bf16-56d0-a58f-2b16720b7aad" :question/math-academy-id "e-9439" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fda8bfb0533b86dc4050d1b8e309e332996b26263b1052d3e251bf0a5c98e816.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C}12xy\\:\\textrm{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter-ellipse $4x^2+y^2=4$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-1,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{y^2}{2^2}  = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+4{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = 12xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =12\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}12xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+3{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =24{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+3\\cos^2 {t},$ $\\textrm d u=-6\\sin t\\cos \n t\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-4{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}({4}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}(8-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{56}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179578 :question/id #uuid "1990e97c-7244-5b6d-a5d5-69cd4b91ad21" :question/math-academy-id "e-9460" :question/problem "What expression is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_\\limits{C} \\dfrac{x}{y} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the ellipse $x^2 + 16y^2 = 16,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{1^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 4\\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=4\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{15{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{15{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179579 :question/id #uuid "a4c10c26-84c5-5ec7-9468-93986e27f492" :question/math-academy-id "e-9462" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y) =x^2-2$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 4.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+4{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2-2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =4{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-2 \\\\  & =2(2{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t,$ $\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{0}^{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{4\\pi } \\\\  & =2(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179588 :question/id #uuid "239120b8-0553-5cff-aee1-f50253af11ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-9488" :question/problem "![](images/71/71df4fc7bd52fe5dd97c7e01502dec2ad68ae31fdf224cccd812c6b7318c70b7.png)\n\nFind, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C} 2xy \\:\\textrm{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is the right half of the ellipse $9x^2 + 4y^2 = 36,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{2^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 3\\sin{t} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+9{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+4({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = 2xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{5{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{5{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179591 :question/id #uuid "2b0b2d1f-df55-56cf-9ae9-ccef499e2352" :question/math-academy-id "e-9517" :question/problem "Construct a definite integral that is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C x^3\\,\\mathrm{d}x + z\\,\\mathrm{d}y + y\\,\\mathrm{d}z,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix $\\mathbf{r}(t)=2t\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos t\\,\\mathbf{j} + \\sin t\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in[0,\\pi]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) =2t, \\qquad \ny(t) = \\cos t , \\qquad \nz(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{3}\\,dx+z\\,dy+y\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+y\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2t)^{3}\\cdot (2)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }16{t}^{3}-{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }16{t}^{3}+({\\cos }^{2}⁡t-{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }16{t}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179596 :question/id #uuid "c18fbc4a-24d5-517d-9a3c-949a11787076" :question/math-academy-id "e-9528" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C y\\,\\mathrm{d}x -x\\,\\mathrm{d}y ,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the ellipse parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 3\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in[0,\\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny(t) = 3\\sin t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-3{\\sin }^{2}⁡t-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,dt \\\\  & =-3{t|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-3\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179597 :question/id #uuid "e4893301-34d3-5f63-9d1c-715588d98d40" :question/math-academy-id "e-9531" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}) \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." :question/worked-solution "The notation $\\displaystyle\\oint$ is used to remind us that we're dealing with a line integral over a *closed* curve.\n\nWe will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad\ny = \\sin{t}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-(1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179606 :question/id #uuid "c1924df7-205f-512e-b024-72f5a654f062" :question/math-academy-id "e-9560" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = (y - x)\\,\\mathbf{i} - (x + y )\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) = 4\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 4\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos t, \\qquad\n\ty = 4\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y-x=4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t), \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-(x+y)=-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{\\sin }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-16(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179607 :question/id #uuid "e9d8e6c2-d346-52fb-918a-5d5c95cd986c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9561" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $9x^2 + y^2 = 9.$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} +3\\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = 3\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =xy=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{2}={\\cos }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\cos t, \\textrm d u = -\\sin t\\,\\textrm d t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({\\cos }^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{\\cos }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{\\cos }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{1}10{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179609 :question/id #uuid "5f6de8db-7738-5df7-8cf8-8e37f6ab483f" :question/math-academy-id "e-96" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0} \\:\\dfrac{\\tan 2\\theta}{\\theta}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{θ \\to  0}\\frac{\\tan 2θ}{θ} &= \\lim_{θ \\to  0}(\\left(\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{\\cos 2θ}\\right))/(θ) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{θ \\to  0}(\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{θ \\to  0}\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$. So, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin 2\\theta}{\\theta} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\nwhich is an indeterminate form. Instead, let's recall the following special limit:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x} =1\n$$We rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta }{\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta }{2\\theta }) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\sin {\\color{blue}{2\\theta }}}{{\\color{blue}{2\\theta }}}\\end{aligned}\n$$Let $x = 2\\theta$. Then, since $x\\to0$ as $\\theta \\to 0$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2 \\cdot \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}2\\theta}{\\color{blue}2\\theta} = 2 \\cdot \\lim_{x \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}x}{\\color{blue}x} = 2 \\cdot 1 =2.\n$$\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0} \\: \\dfrac{1}{\\cos 2\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos 0} = \\dfrac11 = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{θ \\to  0}\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ} = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "112fa180-a9ff-5a56-b2fe-80282d87c197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253821} {:answer/id #uuid "60cf1536-90f0-50e5-951e-3b49ab45ab85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254535} {:answer/id #uuid "bc1a3b6b-ca8b-5902-8ca8-8bc8bf6be7a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255352} {:answer/id #uuid "e0be5a81-a209-5ccd-96ce-0c6a965890c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255666} {:answer/id #uuid "e86b4ea1-a0c8-561f-af0b-f93a91a9e5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254535} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f0a4ffd-5d50-5cdb-9c19-928ff9fa4767" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b79282b0-117d-50c1-87eb-8d6109e91a7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122136" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+16{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =16{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-16{\\sin }^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =16({\\cos }^{2}⁡t-{\\sin }^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t,$ $\\text{d}u=2\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =32{\\int }_{0}^{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =32\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{4\\pi } \\\\  & =32(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c24c262-4398-5446-bc9d-27645b4f0cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}(2\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177424} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c38c61-4b53-5b3b-9117-947ce6775169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}(\\cos t + 2\\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177425} {:answer/id #uuid "de07f689-6383-5efe-90dd-91b2b4623e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}(\\cos t + 2\\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177426} {:answer/id #uuid "0eaf656d-f304-5384-9efa-03f1f9909af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}(2\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177427} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce387ae-f4c2-5ae9-a54a-6936b3b4fe44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}(\\cos^{2} t + 2\\sin t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177428}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8f90318c-819f-5544-b17a-bb82ed972697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177423} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "493c72c7-d782-5717-bb31-6b0270646703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387176} {:answer/id #uuid "a9663772-7b34-5824-82d4-530f49fc606a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387177} {:answer/id #uuid "c397de39-3c94-523e-b199-d82ded805b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387178} {:answer/id #uuid "9e22557f-8b13-5a8f-9cf2-c6c3704ac0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387179} {:answer/id #uuid "dad10713-d816-5d20-9f77-54c2d84897ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387180}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387180} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7ee2bd2-6018-5d27-a3ae-041cc035912b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c8f5c163-ed26-54b5-8aab-82f3a498034d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122263" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+4{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{\\sin }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18621f5a-7133-571b-88ef-53eb0deed3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253880} {:answer/id #uuid "644bb80a-08b0-5a24-bfe8-6878fa59640a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{5{sin}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254569} {:answer/id #uuid "9c6a3057-6ace-5530-947c-d9f8b98ee510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+15}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255048} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e161d4-6bd1-5a00-a1f3-e07b0c70af01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{5{sin}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255171} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1ba099-2805-5efa-92dd-2e4223b1218b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255173}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253880} :answer-field/id #uuid "56c08944-18ef-5fdc-9330-4e14892bd61a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "939eabb6-2bce-5cdb-8479-d0b9ae63294a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122269" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3bf746cfb21805587a65c7beeac90e75464f630bab1fbf3453de69ca3e6639eb.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{4y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is the left half of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=16,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{4y}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{4y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{15{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e7f000-2d2b-551e-80dd-e04e2acee2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253701} {:answer/id #uuid "1dfb99c9-c4f2-5b3f-bd12-1c6c7092dd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253922} {:answer/id #uuid "4821693d-3621-5434-a86b-7624d2be2de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254283} {:answer/id #uuid "8efd5e79-5f84-56f4-a89d-cbfaa679144d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254926} {:answer/id #uuid "f844b804-93be-5b90-bda6-d59e3a5be4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255842}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253922} :answer-field/id #uuid "72ee3597-1dd0-5de1-b56b-43f4da9ab07f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dcd58e0c-8630-5c7a-a6a1-6bb6a8bcb260" :question/math-academy-id "q-122440" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(4,0)$ in the fourth quadrant?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](images/0b/0baaafbe9846ccef2c06a3b1a86eccac394344afc2dfd94bba2907ebb1cf2362.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+16{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22a10aec-2de8-522a-80d0-b6ac69c3fc36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+5}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257069} {:answer/id #uuid "245e377f-d794-5faa-80dc-046dd074c5d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257088} {:answer/id #uuid "59416cb9-04ed-59ee-bb6f-e3a4d50a609c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257556} {:answer/id #uuid "922d00d6-8ac5-59b8-b6b0-c621f39534d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258071} {:answer/id #uuid "aa428e1b-e786-5467-88b9-85fb757023e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+5}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258266}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257556} :answer-field/id #uuid "f90be87a-aed6-507f-bb3d-7e829ec213e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4f83a1e2-579b-5a18-9def-ff09a4759ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-123193" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b0f3ae9a464812957c297c09ff3f65bfe5964bdac70298e72ec3dbde2325e72.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is the right half of the ellipse $6{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=6,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $\\sqrt{6},$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+6{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =6{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-6{\\sin }^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =6({\\cos }^{2}⁡t-{\\sin }^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
